ČSSD doesn't know Voldemort or confectionery in Prague
Apparently, the new councillors of the CSSD will have to overcome a certain language barrier in order to understand their old-fashioned colleagues from the ODS in the Prague Council and in the council.
Aktuálně.cz "tested" the members of the new ČSSD board, as they knowing the notoriously established slang, which was created at the town hall during the last years, when Prague was ruled by current coalition partners.
The coded dictionary, introduced by Prague’s political cream during the previous era of Mayor Pavel Bém, describes some of the most well-known figures, situations and cases in the metropolis.
Surprisingly, it turned out that the new councillors didn't understand well-known terms
At least that's what he's saying.
"Who's Voldemort?"
"I really don't know."
"I'm more of a rookie in Prague politics," Lukáš Kaucký, the councillor for culture, responded to the test.
And although he is a political veteran, Councillor Karel Březina reacted similarly.
For example, the term "golf hole".
"It's the only expression you're presenting to me, which I sometimes use."
"I don't generally recognize slang."
From the glossary, published by Aktuálně.cz this June before the ODS electoral congress, the editors selected six examples, mainly those that are notoriously associated with the Prague Municipality.
The socialists were supposed to explain the terms "Voldemort", "the biggest golf hole in Prague", "going to the pastry shop", "bully neck", "treason of the capital" and "shelling".
A trio of councillors agreed with the quiz, the fourth - Antonín Weinert, who is supposed to head Prague's education system in the future - promised an interview at first, but later did not answer the phone.
Fifth councillor Petr Dolínek refused the test.
"I don't deal with these things at all, and I won't evaluate howing how civic Democrats are having fun."
"Now I'm figuring out how we're going to understand each other as part of our programs," he responded.
As has already been said, Councillor Lukáš Kaucký would reportedly not recognize in the headline above that we are talking about an influential businessman and a friend of Bém Roman Janoušek.
"Voldemort" Kaucký doesn't tell him anything, and the only term he correctly estimated in the test is "the biggest golf hole in Prague".
"It's the Blanka tunnel," Kaucký says, and immediately adds: "But that's more from the newspaper."
"I'll have to pay for a language course," he jokes.
Not only matador Karel Březina, but also councillor for the environment Lukáš Plachý, deciphers the golf hole as a Blanka tunnel to the administration.
Both Social Democrats then know who Voldemort really is, and Lukas Plachém is also familiar with where to find this figure - he suspects the meaning of the term "go to the pastry shop".
"I mean going to a Prague-based company," Plachý is close to deciphering that it is the headquarters of Janoušek's companies.
In the same way, Březina recalls that he drew his knowledge from newspapers rather than to use such a dictionary.
In social democracy, according to test attendants, there is no similar well-established vocabulary of slang expressions.
"I've consulted with my colleagues, but it looks like we really don't have anything like that."
"We call ourselves names, sometimes abbreviations and distortions of names are used," says Petr Hulínský, the chairman of Prague's ČSSD, who is nicknamed Hulda.
"At school they called me Bříza, but I don't know if my colleagues call me that, too," admits Karel Březina.
Newspaper Aktuálně.cz has tried the name and is working.
Councillor Weinert is for the parties of Děda or less friendly Dědek, the representative Miroslav Poche, who drew attention to himself before the election fraud in the sponsorship of the party, is nicknamed "Igráček" by some colleagues.
Nicknames are also the only thing the Social Democrats admit in intra-party language.
Hulínský is said to don't like to hear Hulda, but the daily Aktuálně.cz has also recorded another nickname of this Prague chairman.
"Doctor of baton law" is said to be reminded that Petr Hulínský obtained his title JUDr. at the Bratislav police academy.
The government wants to curtail the torture of "witches," has issued a brochure
Women pronounced in Burkina Faso have several common features.
Rather, it belongs to the older generation, they cannot participate in work, for example, for health reasons, are free and do not have children.
The one who "becomes" the witch is sent into isolation, so as not to threaten the rest of the village.
"Men will very rarely have this fate."
"Even young healthy women aren't being pushed back because they're helping to work and giving birth to children," describes the order's sister, who cares for more than a hundred women in a special house in the West African state of Burkina Faso, who has expelled their communities from their ranks.
The local government has now decided to take action against an unusual "social measure."
She has published an informational brochure that should help curb the witch hunt.
There's a catch.
"More than 70 percent of adults in Burkina Faso can't read it," Die Standard said.
Accusation of witchcraft is also common in other African countries.
Seventy-two-year-old Ammy Hemmah died last week in Ghana, which is adjacent to Burkina Faso, after three women and two men tortured her, pouring kerosene and setting herself on fire.
The case was reported by the BBC.
Offenders say it was an unfortunate coincidence.
According to their version, an oil that is used for ritual anointing has ignited.
He is said to have flared after the group wanted to "expel the evil spirit" from the woman.
The isolation of "witches" is supported by people, regardless of the level of educational attainment.
Der Standard quoted a forty-two-year-old businessman from Burkina Faso as saying: "Devours of human souls have always posed a danger," he stated.
While in Burkina Faso the witch hunt is trying to moderate the government, in Gambia the president himself participated last year.
“After the death of his aunt, Gambian President Jammi unleashed a large-scale witch hunt that was supposed to be involved in her death and try to kill the president himself with the help of black magic.”
"He invited shamans from nearby Guinea, who, accompanied by police officers, soldiers and the president's personal security, forcibly diverted randomly selected men and women from Gambian villages to the president's farm in Kanilai," Amnesty International said in a report.
On the farm, imprisoned people had to drink a potion from herbs with hallucinogenic effects, which in addition caused intestinal and kidney problems.
Two incarcerated stays on the farm did not survive.
After international pressure, nearly a thousand prisoners were released.
Bakal's tenant: Drowned in stoves, higher rent signed
The older stocky man stepped out the door of the client center of the company RPG Byty "coarbaron" Zdeněk Bakala, the largest private owner of rental apartments in the Czech Republic.
Under his arm he carried a file and in it new lease agreements to three apartments: his, his daughter and his son, who is in prison.
To all three, deregulation just raised their rent.
Instead of 3500 a month, the son will pay 4100 crowns for the apartment 2+1 of the second category from next year.
"It's not hot water there, and petrama has to be drowned," the man points with his finger in his son's new contract, on whose signature he has power of attorney.
"But I've signed it," he explains, half annoyed and half-resigned.
Civic association BytyOKD.cz, which described the new lease conditions as "incorrect and immoral," advises tenants not to sign the new contracts and wait until December 7 to publish instructions on how to proceed against them.
But a man just waving his hand at it.
"One says it, the other," he frowns.
Ostrava is becoming a large laboratory of deregulation.
After the New Year, the vast majority of flats that still have non-marketing rent in the Czech Republic awaits.
Aktuálně.cz therefore decided to visit the "laboratory" in person.
Havířovská waiting room RPG is one of a total of fours in the Ostrava region and on Tuesday at nine in the morning it is already full.
Right next to the door stands a security guard from a security agency and a starting device.
"Lease 2011", shines a special button on its touchscreen.
One tap and a paper with a number comes out of the machine.
You won't even be able to read "157" and your figure is already beeping on a large display under the ceiling of the waiting room.
It's your turn in half a second.
After a while, an older married couple comes to ask about the rent, but the rush in the waiting room is clearly not related to new rents at all.
Most people came to solve common problems as usual - requests for corrections, insurance claims and the like.
Letters with new leases are received by tenants in the Ostrava mailboxes for most of the total of 44 thousand apartments of the RPG portfolio since about mid-November.
RPG Byty proposes an increase in 15 to 38 percent of their rent in letters.
A special counter for new leases was opened at the client center on 18 November.
"It's been running for over a week, so it's only in the run-in," the worker replies behind the counter after a moment of hesitation when the client introduces himself as a journalist.
Deregulation and Bakal's former mining flats are a flammable topic.
Before the elections, he was quite obnoxiously seized by the vice-chairman of the parliament and Ostrava native Lubomír Zaorálek from the ČSSD, and even now the local newspaper is full.
"Twenty years, I've been looking for a new floor."
"Neither was replaced by a board in the toilet," the man relieves in his introduction.
Most so far, according to workers, pensioners are coming to see if they will get a rental discount if they sign in time, as the RPG promises them in a special offer.
Half the discount on the February rent will also be given to other tenants, who will sign the new contract by December 15.
The Havíř "deregulation" partition, however, is definitely not overwhelmed with queries.
"Of yesterday's 360 clients, only about forty to fifty people were here because of the 2011 lease," the workers behind the counter estimate.
On the website and its bulletin boards, RPG proposes an individual negotiation of contracts where it will be needed.
However, if both parties do not agree on the lease, it will have to be determined by an independent court.
“It’s not about threats, it’s about information about only a possible and entirely legitimate legal procedure,” RPG said.
"There's no one to sue here," thinks one of the employees of RPG Byty, and the man on the street in front of the client center makes it partially right.
"I've got a 13-thousand pension and I have five grand left to eat, I don't have enough to judge," he argues.
After all, the worker becomes uneasy after all.
"We'll see."
"Mr. Zaorálek tells them in the media not to sign, so people are probably waiting," he wonders.
The regulation of leases ends in the majority of the Czech Republic with the New Year of 2011 and, according to RPG Byty, their increase is an economic necessity.
"Existing rents still do not reach the amount necessary for the simple maintenance of the housing stock," recalls Pavel Klimeš, director of the residential portfolio of RPG Byty, that it is precisely the angry tenant complaining of the neglect of the housing stock, according to homeowners, the main reason for the increase in rents.
While in the five years between 2005 and 2010 RPGs from rents could invest four billion in apartments, in four years from 2011 to 2014 it is supposed to be 5.2 billion.
Meanwhile, a large-scale screen hanging at the ceiling opposite the padded seats of the Havířov waiting room repeats some red highlighted information.
"The increase in rents in 2011 by 15% and less relates to 31 200 apartments, that's more than 90% of RPG apartments."
"And it will always be the most by 7.50 Kč per square meter per month," it appears on the screen.
For only 4,400 flats from the RPG portfolio, the rent rises by 38 per cent.
"In these cases, it is an increase from an extremely low amount of 19,66 Kč per square meter," points out the RPG.
For 2600 apartments, the rent will remain the same next year.
RPG Byty promises that deregulation will not drive up rents jumpy, but it will be spread over four to eight years.
In addition, it adds another commitment: all the money from the increase in the rent (see the chart) the company puts it back to the apartments, mainly to thermal savings - new windows and insulation.
Preferentially, they should be given to people who sign an agreement to increase rents.
There is also a nursery with offices, the architects understood
The Avriopoint administrative building in Brno is the new seat of Arch.Design.
No one would expect it to also hide a mini-school with everything that belongs to it - with colorful walls, imaginative furniture, a bunch of toys and even a miniature playground on the outdoor terrace.
Since October of last year it can be attended by children of Arch.Design employees.
The design-architectural company Arch.Design, from whose studios came a number of important buildings in Brno, has been counting on space for its corporate mini-school since the first drawings of the Avriopoint building.
It employs almost 90 people, mostly at the age of babyboom, and cared that women designers and architects could quickly return to work.
Together with the possibility of short-time, the nursery is a benefit that Arch.Design offers to its employees within the social program.
Builders and architects thus have the opportunity to combine demanding employment with the care of a child.
The mini-preschool was invented by the employees of Arch.Design and with the support of the management they managed to obtain a subsidy from the European Union and the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs.
The corporate mini-kindergarten is funded by the European Social Fund through the Operational Programme Human Resources and Employment and the state budget of the Czech Republic.
Thanks to grant funding (a subsidy of 2, 8 million CZK), they were able to purchase equipment and equipment and provide qualified personnel and operations for two years.
After exhausting the financial subsidy (2 years), Arch.Design is obliged for 3 years to run a mini-nursery at its own expense.
The preparation of the project took one year and was significantly assisted by Economy Rating.
Care is provided by qualified staff, children are cared for all daily by a nurse and kindergarten teacher, so children can go to the kindergarten without age restrictions, yet most of them are older than 3 years.
Because it is a "mini kindergarten", only 5 children can be present at the same time, but during the day there are more.
The operation of the nursery is flexible according to the needs of parents and the children of all employees have the same right to use the nursery, regardless of the hierarchy of position in the company.
Although it is an individual upbringing and care, the programmatic content in the mini-school is comparable to the framework programme of pre-school education.
Children not only play here, but they are also provided with high-quality teaching - from basic cognition to preparation for school benches, English language is also taught.
The class is equipped not only with toys, stimulating games, but also with a computer that children can use.
The presence of young children directly in the company creates a friendly and positive environment.
Parents can check their child at any time and there are stressful situations with delayed pick-up of children from kindergartens distant from the parents' workplace.
Parents use working hours more productively.
Not surprisingly, there is a naturally loyal employee-employer relationship.
Arch.Design has become one of the Czech companies that offer a significant benefit in recruiting new employees, are family-friendly and through this program has strengthened HR marketing.
The house of bones fascinates even after 100 years. Look into the interiors from Gaudí
The roof looks like the back of a prehistoric lizard, the staircase resembles the spine of a dinosaur, and the balconies are an open fish mouth.
Such is the Casa Batllō house in Barcelona by the Catalan architect Antoni Gaudí.
Thanks to its organic shapes, the house has earned the nickname House of Bones.
Organic shapes, wild colors, the unusual use of stone, fragments of tiles and mosaics, a façade resembling scales and ubiquitous soft curves, all this is typical of Antoni Gaudi's Art Nouveau.
The facade of the house is played by dream lines, tender colors and textures reminiscent of nature and animals.
Geometricity, rhythm and a well-thought-out order can be seen during detailed observations.
At night, the entire façade of the houses sounds even more dramatic, it is illuminated so that the impression of the shine of the colors is further enhanced.
The winding and twisting of the shapes appears equally in the interiors.
Wood in waves copies window frames, railings, decorative elements of doors, peepholes and moldings.
Located in Eixample on the bustling shopping street Passeig de Grácia No. 43, the Batlló house was rebuilt in 1877 by architect Emilio Sala Cortes.
The reconstruction took place for two years and the house was completed in 1906.
The investor, the wealthy industrialist Josep Batlló, originally insisted on demolishing the old building and building a completely new one, but soon accepted Gaudí’s idea for an unusual and cheaper reconstruction.
Antoni Gaudí, however, did not propose an apartment house Casa Batlló himself.
It was collaborated by architect Josep Maria Jujol.
It was on his designer board that decorations were created for the representative apartment of the owner of the property and the use of mosaic on the facade.
A number of other artists and artists also worked on the façade.
Working together on Casa Batlló’s design of the house kicked off Jujola’s successful cooperation with Gaudí.
Jujol participated in the construction of Gaudí's house Casa Milá and Park Güell, in which there are other works by Gaudí.
From the locals, the house soon after its completion earned the nickname "House of Bones" thanks to its organic shapes.
Because of a specific window-opening mechanism, others began to call it a "yawning house."
It is also said that at the top of the building is depicted the sword of St. George, patron saint of Catalonia.
Hidden religious motifs also contain other sculptural elements of the building.
Gaudí designed a unique system of central heating with ventilating vents and typical chimneys.
Bizarre may seem their forty-five-degree inclination.
In creating Gaudí he absolutely did not respect the building regulations of that time and did not allow himself to be bound by them.
At the beginning of the construction, he obtained a building permit from the city, but his data on the planned reconstruction were so vague that two years later, during the completion, the city council was horrified and asked for a remedy.
For example, councillors were annoyed, for example, by the fact that the construction interfered with the pillars and into the public pavement.
There has never been a correction, on the contrary: just fifteen days after the call of the city district, Josep Batlló applied (and successfully obtained) permission to rent the two highest floors of the building.
The owner himself moved to the lower floors.
In the gallery on the second floor there was a social salon and on the third floor there was a bedroom and a private part of the apartment.
The ground floor was rented a jewellery shop.
The entire six-storey house intersects the staircases, which can be climbed all the way to the roof terrace itself.
The fundamental elements of the reconstruction were the abolition of all right angles and straight lines and their replacement with curved walls.
Gaudí also worked with the modern concept of interconnecting spaces.
The social part can be completely interconnected by opening the lounge door, and a larger company can comfortably communicate with each other without maintaining the privacy needed for more intimate conversations at the same time.
In addition to the new façade, the house with Gaudí’s reconstruction also received an unconventional enclosure and two extra floors.
Also impressive is Gaudí's play with light, blue and white glazed tiles, through which the inner yard is lined, and skylights.
The game of two colors adds maximum light on all floors of the house.
It was enough to simply bet on the principle that white reflects light more than dark, which, on the contrary, absorbs the sun's rays.
The Casa Batlló house, which was included in Unesco's World Heritage Fund in 2005, can be visited by anyone.
On the first floor of the house, the most spacious and opulent apartment is made available to the public.
The owners decided for this step only in 2002, on the occasion of the celebration of the hundred and 50th anniversary of the birth of the famous creator.
They stayed during the tours because they found that it was a very advantageous enterprise and, moreover, they expanded the tours with common and attic spaces, including the roof terrace.
Admission to the monument is definitely not negligible, it amounts to 16.50 euros, and students, pensioners, groups and holders of tourist cards have a 20 percent discount.
The amount greatly exceeds the price of admission to other Gaudí buildings in the city.
If you want to taste the true atmosphere of the house with dragon turrets, you have to quickly become friends with one of the occupancies and visit it right at home.
How to choose a mattress and grate: women have different requirements than men
Not the opulous bed frame, but the quality of the lying surface, i.e. the mattress and grate, give the foundation of a really good bed.
However, they must complement each other harmoniously to suit a particular user.
When choosing decides a lot of things, from weight, height, age, state of health and the gender of the owner.
Also important is the type of interior, into which the mattress and grate are purchased.
A typical example?
For example, latex mattresses are not suitable for weekend cottages, where they do not drown regularly, because they "warm up" more slowly.
In addition, they have a significantly higher weight than a standard polyurethane mattress.
It is necessary to reverse them regularly.
Because if they don’t have a lot of venting ducts in the core, and still lies on a solid plate, they retain moisture: Mold could also appear under them.
When choosing, the custom also decides.
Anyone who has spent a long time abroad, where very high mattresses are often used with a softening layer, will find it difficult to feel good on a low and with a hard surface.
"Perhaps all the hockey players who came back from the NHL have complained to me that they won't sleep here."
"They needed mattresses with a softening layer of lazy foam for their battered shoulders, heels and hips."
"Such are also recommended for people with diabetes, who have a disorder of the nervous system, so they misunderstand "information" from the joints and skin," explains Sylva Hadamcziková, who specializes in upholstered furniture.
For many hockey players, but also for other customers years ago, they were looking for mattresses with lazy foam.
"It's okay today, a suitable mattress has Tempur, Italian Magniflex, but also Czech Tropico."
You don't have to be an NHL fighter to appreciate lazy foam.
This "miracle" works simply.
It optimizes its stiffness and shape according to the absorbed heat or adapts to your warm body.
There's not pushing you, you won't be overturning at night like after a tough fight.
You'll just sleep better.
Lazy foam also acts as protection against bedsores.
When choosing the size of the mattress, add the length of the foot to its height, which is about 21 to 34 cm.
Both a man and a woman with a height of about 180 cm need a bed 210 to 220 cm.
The minimum width for one should be 90 cm for a double bed, a total of 180 cm.
For separate beds, for example, for singlists, at least 120 to 140 cm is recommended.
For comfort and a possible visit.
"Otherwise, the rule for the width of the bed is simple: when lying on your back, you should have your hands comfortably slightly bent in your elbow," advises Helena Prokopová, guilder of the Upholstery Guild, which has been dealing with the issue of healthy sleeping and furniture ergonomics for many years and lectures at vocational schools.
For a doubloon, everyone needs their own grating and mattress, but be careful when buying to match the heights of mattresses.
Otherwise, there will be nothing left but to put the grids to different heights, but you may not be able to do this with each bed frame.
Foam mattresses should have a height of at least 14 cm, types with a spring skeleton at least 15, of which at least three centimeters should be counted on the moulding and loosening layer.
"When touching your hands or sitting on the mattress, you must definitely not feel the springs," warns Helena Prokopová.
Each mattress requires a certain type of grating.
All-upholstered beds, when mattresses and the substrate are like conjoined Siamese twins, have nothing to do in the bedrooms.
Mattresses don't wind badly, you can't wash their cover, which is already common today.
And because we normally sweat 0.2 to 0.75 liters of sweat per night, it is clear that after five to seven years the mattress begins to resemble one large (and clogged) sieve.
That’s why experts reject advertising slogans about the life of mattresses up to 25 years, or even lifetime guarantees.
However, everything has its own but.
And that’s why advice from sellers: don’t give up to mattresses with a width of 120 or 140 cm of hygiene and a removable coating, but add a mattress protector, which you can easily remove and wash.
Even though mattress covers are commonly made today around the entire perimeter, so after unzipping we get two parts (for washing and the size of the washing machine very important!), the handling in larger types (and in one person) when shooting could resemble a duel of light weight in the ring with heavy.
Do you have a solid board in your bed instead?
Never! Never!
Maximum on the bed designed for one-nocture visitors.
However, provide the board (if it does not already have them) with ventilation holes and use a mattress with a spring skeleton and a height of at least 15 cm with springs in sacks (of which.Taschenfederkern) and a cultivating layer.
Polyurethane mattress does not belong on such a substrate.
Latex or sandwich mattresses (e.g. a combination of latex and coconut) will be suitable for non-positioning slats with 14 to 18 slats, but you will always be worthy grates with a larger number of slats, 28 or more, where the slats in the loin area are doubled and it is possible to regulate their rigidity.
There is no mattress with a sprink skeleton on the positioning slats, because they do not respond to positioning.
In addition to adjusting the stiffness of the slats, you can also indulge in the so-called shoulder cradle.
It is a special attachment of the slats, which allows you to push or even tilt the slat so that the shoulder sleeps nicely.
For men who have "shoulders all over their backs", definitely an important thing.
It is even remembered by manufacturers of mattresses, who in "men's" began to use a softening zone just in the shoulders area.
They also remember the women - they, in turn, give them a softer surface in hips.
For the most demanding customers are designed wing or plate grates with so-called multi-torsion suspension.
The pressure breaks down into multiple points and the voltage is reduced.
However, beware, as soon as you use on the right mattress and grate the wrong pillow (large and "bloated"), most "plus" will disappear.
The basic rule is simple: the pillow is supposed to support the head and the toes only interfere with the shoulders, so that the shoulder muscles can relax.
As a suitable size is recommended 50 x 60 cm, which is preferred, for example, in Scandinavia.
When lying on the side, the pillow should balance (along with the shoulder cradle of the grate or a softened zone in the mattress) the height of the shoulder.
Modern mattresses have something to do with the original straws stuffed with grasses after all.
Their manufacturers are increasingly using natural materials or at least admixtures.
It is also a trend for organic mattresses.
Raw materials from petroleum from which polyurethane foams are produced are replaced by natural oils, for example from jump and soy.
It is soy that is used by all known manufacturers.
"Bio for mattresses means that the core is made of material of purely natural origin," explains Petr Vašeček from Tropico Plus.
Natural substances are also used in the production of mattress covers, one of the first was aloe vera.
But you can also indulge in microcapsules to release the fresh smell of forest or lavender.
There's also division for men and women.
While men are "cooled" by a plant extract of birch, in order to absorb the cover of the mattress heat and cool the body, women, on the other hand, "heat".
Red pepper extract increases the circulation of the blood, making it weaker sex warmer.
Today's favorites?
Mattresses made of cold foam (it has larger pores with a broken cell structure, so it is more breathable than conventional polyurethane and offers better elasticity.
Combining the various stiffnesses of cold foam and "lazy" foam creates multi-zone mattresses that know exactly where the head, loins and heels should be.
For a high-quality mattress you will pay from seven thousand crowns and above.
Those that meet health parameters tend to have a lower VAT rate are treated as medical aids.
Gumotex was the first to achieve this advantage in the Czech Republic.
Saving on a mattress and grating is not easy.
Sometimes it is possible to use, for example, events of manufacturers or sellers who offer two mattresses for the price of one.
For example, Magniflex offers Duofreze mattress with sewn Memory memory foam into a cover for 11 990 crowns.
However, the second can already be purchased free of charge.
Tempur material kick-started an era of lazy or viscoelastic foams with an open cell structure and high density.
It originated as material for NASA because the astronauts were subjected to enormous congestion during launches.
Tempur softens in the heat, on the contrary, it strengthens in the cold.
It adapts perfectly to the warm human body and spreads its pressure optimally.
Therefore, it is ideal against the formation of bedsores, but also to increase the comfort when sleeping.
Viscoelastic foams are used not only for mattresses, but also for medical products, such as special pillows under the feet, which lighten the feet and venous system.
Domestic manufacturers, for example, use Eucatherm C or Twinsaver foam and the like.
More and more parents are wanting surveillance devices, so they want to control their children
Fearing for their own child leads more and more people to the idea of guarding via GPS.
The system originally created for the search for cars can also monitor the movement of people, and thus help, for example, in cases such as the disappearance of nine-year-old Anička Janatková.
So far, the device is about the size of a matchbox, but soon it can only be as big as a watch.
Many people were startled by the case of the missing Annie and began looking for ways to better protect their children.
Even at the cost of invading their privacy
"We try to tell everyone that this is not about spying, but another means to reduce the likelihood that a tragedy will happen," says Petr Pilin of Macro Weil, one of the suppliers of surveillance equipment to the Czech market.
"I admit that, in some cases, such devices can be misused, for example, if they put themselves in a bag for someone who doesn't know about it, but we haven't encountered such a case yet."
"Mostly it's actually bought by customers for safety reasons," Pilin added.
According to him, the child should always know for what purpose the "box" serves and how it is used in case of an emergency.
Baby psychologist Dana Bočková points out that if a child does not want to wear anything similar, he will just get rid of it.
"Even very young children demonstrate great creativity in how to lose some things on purpose when they don't want to be under control."
“Great anticipations were raised earlier by their parents, but they can be switched off just like these tracking devices,” Bočková said.
According to her, it is also questionable whether such a device would actually help in the event of an attack.
"When it comes to upcoming abduction, the perpetrator practically always ensures that the child does not have any electronics that can reveal its location."
"It's the phone now."
"When tracking devices begin to expand, they'll be looking for them, too, because their sales will certainly be boosted by massive advertising."
"In such a case, criminals will always be one step ahead of the police," the psychologist says.
However, it admits that a similar device can help in the event of a sudden attack by a paedophile.
According to Robert Čech from RC Monitoring, which offers tracking services for both cars and people, even if a tracking device is immediately destroyed after an attack, its presence can help.
"Devices can be set to regularly send a location after a certain period of time."
"If there is damage, at least the last location of the person in question will be available."
"More expensive devices have built-in and tiltometers, so even if a man just slips away and stays motionless, the device immediately sends a warning with a positioning," says Čech.
According to him, a similar service can be done by smartphones with navigation, where it is enough to upload special software.
The problem with the current surveillance units is that they are either devices similar to pendants the size of matches or smaller mobile phones.
There is always a risk of their loss.
According to Pilin of Macro Weil, this is why devices such as conventional digital watches are now being tested.
"This will have the greatest advantage in the fact that an aggressor may not know the tracking device at all."
"Or he doesn't recognize them until after a while and that can give the police extra time," he adds.
Whether the tracking devices develop into bracelets or pendants, they will still use the same technology that is used to monitor cars.
It is a combination of positioning through the GPS system (which is used by common self-navigation) and the function of the mobile phone, which reports the current position of the car.
The army has incendiary cords that they would wrap around the Czech Republic, but it will last only two years
The Czech army has purchased 445 kilometers of cords, through which explosives are launched.
Last year, the Ministry of Defence delivered 224 kilometers of cords to the warehouses.
The army thus has supplies for 225 years.
The problem is that the durability of the cords is two, a maximum of four years.
The army bought incendiary cords from the joint-stock STV Group, a. s. for 40 million crowns.
In justifying the purchase of such a huge number of incendiary cords, the MF TODAY has written off that soldiers consume 60 kilometers of fuses per year during training.
However, it is clear from the calculations of army pyrotechnics addressed by MF TODAY that all generals, army command officers, secretaries or cleaners would have to undergo very intensive pyrotechnic training every year.
Defense Minister Alexander Vondra and army commander Vlastimil Pick.
Only in this way you could justify the annual consumption of an army of sixty kilometers of fuses.
This length corresponds to 25 thousand explosions.
And even if that were the case, the military's supplies would be enough for more than a decade.
If, on the contrary, it were necessary for the basic training of any new professional at most two meters of incendiary cord, as the experts say, then the army, with a fresh purchase - even with the increased consumption of engineering or special units - secured supplies for the aforementioned 225 years.
At the same time, the soldiers of the Rapid Deployment Brigade have recently complained to Minister Vondra that they have almost no ammunition for small arms, so shootings are also limited due to the lack of money.
Not to mention the lack of underwear.
"It doesn't bother me that they buy such a large number of fuses from us repeatedly."
"But it's their decision, and I'm sure they'll know what it's for."
"For us, the army is a good customer, which we as traders try to accommodate," says Jan Děcký, sales manager at STV Group.
Jan Pejšek, a spokesman for the Ministry of Defense, explains the hundreds of kilometers of the purchased cord by saying that the army will have supplies of this "munition" for at least ten years.
However, Jan Děcký from STV Group speaks only about the four-year service life of the fuse and police or army pyrotechnics even about two years.
"With perfect storage, it can also be the four," says one of them, who, as a soldier, does not want to publish a name because of criticism of the army.
After the expiration of life, each ammunition must be carefully tested to see if it is still reliable and does not endanger soldiers' lives.
However, these tests aren't free and cost more money.
Another ministry's argument is a quantitative discount on 445 kilometres of incendiary cords.
"They cost us sixty percent less than last year's van two hundred and forty-four kilometers," a spokesperson for Pejšek interprets the opinion of the purchasers of the Ministry of Defence.
Just for comparison: the annual consumption of police pyrotechnicians who dispose of ammunition from the Second World War or after the Soviet army has not exceeded 1,500 meters for several years.
And the police are always ordering only such a quantity to consume this ammunition even before the end of life.
It has been proven for the safety of pyrotechnics, and it can also more flexibly obtain more modern and therefore more reliable types of cords.
The Ministry of Defence already has considerable difficulties with the liquidation of tens of thousands of tons of ammunition from the time of the Warsaw Pact, when the two-hundred-thousand Czechoslovak armies were preparing to drag on the Rhine.
According to pyrotechnics addressed by MF DNES, the same problem awaits in a few years: it will dispose of hundreds of kilometers of old fuses, which are now "cheesy".
To this day, I draw from the contacts I gained thanks to the competition
The winning place in the Tradesman of the Year competition helped Lukáš Pytloun in the sale of the franchise of his hotel.
The twenty-eight-year-old businessman Lukáš Pytloun from Liberec became the winner of last year's Tradesman of the Year competition.
His success story is like from a movie.
Lukas Pytloun liked to travel.
And abroad he realized that there is a lack of quality accommodation in Liberec at a reasonable price.
From savings from foreign brigades and using a bank loan, he bought a semi-destroyed house with five apartments.
The fact that he was still a third-year college student at the time was great courage.
“At that time, the ratio between the quality and the price of accommodation with us didn’t give me a chance not to do anything about it,” says Lukáš Pytloun.
The first renovated apartment began to be rented to foreign workers.
He invested all the profits back into the house, which he gradually converted into a pension with 54 beds.
Since then, in seven years of business, it has gradually managed to open four guesthouses in Liberec, two stylish restaurants and two hotels with a total capacity of more than 300 beds of all accommodation categories.
The most comfortable of them until recently was the four-star Pytloun design hotel.
This year, however, there was another, equally high-quality one.
"We just have a signed contract with a hotel in Ostrov near Tisá, where we have sold our franchise concept and other people interested in the Pytloun hotel franchise are applying," says last year's number one tradesman.
"And in this sale of the license to operate the hotel, it was precisely the victory in the Tradesman of the Year competition that contributed to a large extent," Pytloun adds acknowledgedly.
The victory brought him a greater acquaintance and popularity in Liberec and its surroundings.
"People got to know me on the streets, they often congratulated me additionally and I have to say it was pleasant," he recalls.
Participation in the competition is definitely recommended to all those who are trying for something.
There is no need to win, every visibility is good.
He says he still draws on contacts and acquaintances that he might not have gotten to.
"I started doing business regularly with some of them I've met thanks to the competition."
According to him, the fame associated with the success turned out to be a bit for every employee.
And he's in his 70s.
“I see more loyalty and pride in them to be involved in something that has gained general recognition.”
"It's not just my personal achievement, it's the result of the good work of all of us together."
"Only my name is pushed to the forefront," Lukáš Pytloun says modestly today.
I wish my successor a quieter year than we had at Farmet
Euphoria from victory was quickly replaced by the effects of the crisis.
Thanks to the purposefulness of the winner, however, Farmet has had worse times.
Karel Žďárský, the owner of Farmet, came for the prize for the first place in last year’s Vodafone Company of the Year competition.
At that time, the jury appreciated the effort through which it built a company with hundreds of millions of turnovers from a small tooling workshop in Česká Skalice.
"I can say that the award has encouraged not only me but all my co-workers and boosted our self-confidencence for the match with the competition," says last year's winner.
At the beginning of Farmet's history, there was a desire to do something useful.
And so, when the state offered Karel Žďárský’s father for renting a small workshop as compensation for communist-seized property, he did not hesitate for a long time, rejected his career in the company where he worked at the time, and with a few friends around his father started a company for plastic bathtubs.
Not long later, they began specializing in vegetable oil presses.
After eighteen years, it became the largest manufacturer of automatic presses in Central Europe.
Greater awareness, even among non-professional publics, came to the company after the success achieved in the competition through the media.
"Not only our customers began to perceive the Farmet brand significantly better, but also foreign partners began to appreciate that they cooperate with a company that has achieved greater success in their country," says Karel Žďárský.
Last year, however, wasn't just a sign of euphoria from the victorious.
The award was based on the company's economic results until 2008 and only partially from 2009.
And 2009 was the toughest year to sell engineering goods.
It culminated in the crisis, which has not yet touched so many citizens, but companies have already stopped their investment projects and have begun to restrict purchases of new machines and technologies.
"Of course, this situation has not been avoided by us."
"Revenues fell by 40 percent, we had to limit production and there were redundancies," explains Karel Žďárský.
A viable company has to be able to take such unpopular measures sometimes.
But they managed the situation and kept the company in profit, albeit smaller than before the crisis.
However, what they have not discounted in Farmet is an investment in research and development.
"From the subsidies we built a research and training center, we strengthened the presentation at international trade fairs, won the main awards in domestic exhibitions," says Žďárský.
All indications are that the crisis is behind them.
Revenues are growing again, along with a number of new employees.
"I wish the new winner a pleasant feeling of victory, but in a calmer atmosphere," says Karel Žďárský.
A survey that removes nonsensical regulations
The Absurdity of the Year poll is the fourth part of a business competition.
Some nonsensical regulations have already been repealed.
The infamous winner of the next year's Absurdity of the Year poll became the obligation to bring documents to the office, which are also accessible to officials online.
More than a third of less than four thousand voters voted for it.
For example, the Social Security Administration required a paper extract from the Commercial Register, although an official can retrieve it with two clicks of the mouse.
Thanks to the poll, it'll change.
"The Czech Social Security Administration will no longer require an extract from the Commercial Register during inspections by insurance payers," CSSA spokeswoman Jana Buraňová said.
To shine a light on all the regulations and regulations that companies and entrepreneurs in the Czech Republic have to comply with, and to abolish the nonsense is the purpose of the Absurdity of the Year competition.
It is for the fourth part of the business competition organized by Hospodářské noviny for the fourth time this year and is becoming increasingly popular among the public.
If about three thousand voters took part in the vote last year, there have been almost four thousand this year.
And no wonder, all sorts of legal obligations, which serve only to delay the business, are still enough and enough in our country.
"Perhaps in the number of lejsters for tax returns, we are at the top places in the world," says Karel Havlíček from the Association of Small and Medium-Sized Enterprises and Crafts of the Czech Republic.
Simplifying business has become a proclaimed goal for all governments in recent years.
Compared to the world, however, the situation in the Czech Republic is not particularly improving.
According to the World Bank, in terms of business conditions, the Czech Republic ranked 74th, which is eight places worse than last year.
The Absurdity of the Year competition was first announced in 2006 and takes place annually according to the same scenario.
Anyone can submit administrative and bureaucratic nonsense every year from April to the end of September, when there is a competition deadline.
The web address www.firmaroku.cz Then the organizer of the competition selects seven candidates for the "anti-price" and lets the public vote about it during October via the website www.firmaroku.cz/absurdita and www.ihned.cz.
Absurdity, which gets the most votes wins.
More than a hundred have applied for this year's poll, more precisely 105 regulations and regulations lacking sense.
Tips were sent by entrepreneurs, but also by professional unions and journalists.
Among the "finalists" of the competition there was, for example, a requirement that cars for the maintenance of highways and expressways pay tolls, or that entrepreneurs with twenty employees, two offices and two toilets paint every two years.
The list of this year's seven proposals is published on the competition's
The announcement of the competition, which always takes place at the beginning of November on Wenceslas Square in Prague, is a part of hapenning, which is regularly attended by the personality of political and social life.
This year, for example, Eva Svobodová, the director of the Association of Small and Medium-Sized Enterprises and Tradesmen of the Czech Republic or Petr Kužel, President of the Chamber of Commerce of the Czech Republic, assisted with this culmination of the competition.
"Economic prosperity cannot work where the state throws sticks under the feet of entrepreneurs, so my goal is to repeal at least three dozen paragraphs every year, which today only unnecessarily complicate business," said Martin Kocourek, the Minister of Industry and Trade.
Trying to abolish nonsensical regulations does not sound empty.
In 2007, people were most bothered by the obligation of the entrepreneur to report three days in advance to the Tradement Office in the place where the permanent establishment is located, the intention to go with the stand for an exhibition or pilgrimage.
This obligation has been eliminated by an amendment to the Trade Licensing Act.
The second most voted duty in 2007, which was reported by the Labor Office as its foreign employee a business trip of up to 30 days, was eliminated by the so-called green card law.
And a third of all good things.
Absurdity of the year 2009 became the order of physiotherapists to have a large mirror, refrigerator and two demand scales in the office.
This has been mitigated this year by the decree of the Ministry of Health - the obligation to have a refrigerator fell away.
The new category recognizes socially responsible companies
It evaluates ecological, humanitarian, educational or sports projects.
A novelty in this year's fifth edition of Hospodářské noviny competitions is the category entitled Responsible Company of the Year 2010.
The mission of this competition is the promotion of a reasonable and fair business.
"We want to support companies that take into account the impacts of their business on the environment and support socially responsible activities," says Patrik Bartholdy, regional manager of the mobile operator, Vodafone, which is the announcer and guarantor of the competition, as well as in the case of the Rebel Competition of the Year.
In this year’s first sub-category of this special subcategory of the main competition Vodafone Company of the Year 2010, companies for which success in business is not the only long-term goal.
The story of each of them is very inspirational and he would publish on a separate article.
Although the subject of their activity differs diametrically from each other, support and thoughtfulness can be considered a common denominator of their efforts.
In one case, it concerns, for example, disabled people and elsewhere, for example, personal problems or the health of their own employees.
Fourteen nationwide finalists included, for example, the Prague software company Et Netera, which supports the creation of informal sports teams among employees, who then participate in a number of tournaments in various sports.
Another example of a responsible company from the Pardubice region is the company Isolit-Bravo, which has been the main contributor to the endowment fund sponsoring handicapped children for seventeen years.
Its disabled employees are taken care of by a paper mill from Poděbrady, which has more than 70 percent of them, and has therefore won the title of Responsible Company of the Central Bohemian Region, and in the Vodafone Company of the Year competition it ranked third in the region.
In the Olomouc region, the accounting firm MiRyz Soft, which introduced flexible working hours, also won, reduced to an average of 35 hours per week, but without a proportionally reduced salary.
She was also awarded the title by the jury for preferentially buying from Czech producers, supporting civic activities in the vicinity and saving the environment.
"We try to recycle as much as possible so that our business and our administration have the least possible impact on the environment," explains Miluše Ryznarová.
Responsible IT company Enlogit from the Usti Region thinks about the health of its employees and has purchased ergonomic chairs and keyboards for each laptop, supports their work from home and uses catering services provided by the local sheltered workshop at their corporate events.
"In addition, we support the People in Need Foundation, which specifically helps the local Roma community," says Ondřej Suchý, the company's representative.
Last but not least, it is also worth mentioning the activity of the company Washing and Cleaning from the Karlovy Vary region, which contributes to their employees in the form of above-standard bonuses for their pension insurance.
"With us, in turn, we give contributions for food, transportation, upskilling and recreation," says Josef Strapáč from the Bruntál construction company Czasch, a winner in the Moravian-Silesian Region.
All regional finalists of the Vodafone Company of the Year competition were automatically registered in the category Responsible Company of the Year, who answered the questions of the questionnaire attached to the application and sent it no later than 15 August to the organizer.
Incorrectly completed or incomplete responses in the questionnaire were then eliminated by the organiser from the evaluation.
"If the representatives of the competing company did not answer the questions, they could not participate in the Responsible Firm of the Year competition, but they are not disqualified from the "main" Vodafone Company of the Year competition."
"It is not possible to register separately only for this competition, because it is a subcategory of the main competition Company of the Year," explains Karel Goldman from the organizing agency Bison & Rose.
At the Land Fund in Pilsen, the police arrested during a public tender
Anti-corruption polices have been arrested in the Land Fund building in Pilsen in recent days.
Detectives have deployed Želízka to a local businessman in the media sphere and real estate dealer Luboši G.
He allegedly tried to manipulate the current public tender for the settlement of restitution claims so that the land offered in the competition acquires a specific candidate.
Luboši G. was supposed to pay half a million crowns for it.
"So far, I can only confirm that the police are prosecuting one person for the offense of intrigues in a public contract and in a public tender," Antonie Zelená, head of the Pilsen prosecutor's office, said, adding that further details could not be provided in view of the ongoing investigation.
The forty-six-year-old businessman was released after 24 hours.
There can only be speculation about how the public tender could be affected, given the minimum of official information.
A spokeswoman for the Land Fund Lubomíra Černá, when asked by Právo, said that police officers had actually been arrested at a separate workplace in Pilsen, during a public tender.
"This was a client, not our employee," she said.
According to Black, the public tender has the correct name "Call for the submission of tenders for the transfer of land according to the Land Act" and takes place in the event that more legitimate applicants express interest in the offered land.
"Those persons under the call offer a higher value of their restitution claims to be settled by the transfer than the declared value of the land."
"According to the rights offered, the order of the acquirers is determined, and the Land Fund then transfers the land to the person who offered the highest entitlements," a spokeswoman for the fund explained.
Luboš G. apparently had information about who applied for the call and because he himself is also an authorized person, because he bought up a number of restitution claims, he offered those interested in the land that he would not participate in the competition.
For this he may have collected severances.
This would explain that he had such a large amount of money in his detention.
Luboš G. has had above-standard contacts at PF in Pilsen in the past.
He was involved in the case of the former head of PF Václav Sankot, who was criminally prosecuted for issuing in substitute restitution land on which a decision on the location of the building had already been issued.
Just at that time Luboš G. obtained from the fund, for example, land intended for the construction of public roads, specifically in the place where the entrance highway was to be built into the industrial zone Borská Pole.
Later it was very conveniently exchanged with the municipality for a house in the very historical center of the city, which had a value much higher.
That's when the town hall had to accept it, because otherwise it would endanger the building by blocking the land.
Households pay hundreds per month
From January, households will have to pay extra not only for all kinds of energies, but also for aqueous and sewerage, heat supply, and rents will grow somewhere.
Families will have to withdraw up to hundreds of crowns a month from January than they do now.
Electricity will become more expensive by up to 4.6 percent, gas will be about 2 percent more expensive, up to a tenth in some cities you will have to pay extra for water supplies, and many will also increase the supply of central heat.
The increase in the price of electricity in apartments should come out annually by a hundred-crown more expensive than now.
At the same time, all major suppliers have lowered the prices of power electricity, so a significant increase in support for the production of energy from renewable sources, especially photovoltaics, is to blame.
The gas will increase slightly, but at a time when people are drowning the most and have the highest consumption.
Therefore, it also has to account for hundreds of crowns extra per year.
While electricity and gas will become more expensive across the country, the change in central heating prices varies from place to place.
Price increases are mainly planned by larger municipalities.
For example, households in Pardubice or Hradec Králové will pay about 50 crowns more per month for central heating from January, more than 1000 crowns a year will be paid by families in Liberec.
Residents of Prague, in turn, are at risk of more than 9 percent more aqueous and sewage, if the city increases rents from infrastructure.
If the rent does not change, it will pay 6.8 percent.
However, the price in Prague will still be among the lower in the nationwide comparison.
In the north of Bohemia, where prices are already among the highest, aqueous and sewerage is supposed to increase by 7.4 percent.
For example, rent prices may rise significantly in 450 thousand rental apartments, where regulations have ended since January.
For example, CPI Byty, which owns about 2500 apartments in Ústí nad Labem, intends to increase the rent by up to one hundred percent from January.
Those who do not agree with the new amount of the lease intend to put the company in court.
Regulated rents paid by people in around 300,000 apartments could rise by up to ten percent next year.
Czech children get drunk with fernet and rum. Beer is out of fashion, it performs slowly
Fernet, rum, vodka, exceptionally even a better whisky brand stolen from their parents.
These are drinks, after whose drinking most often ends in the care of doctors drunks, who are not even fifteen years old.
The exception is not children who are treated repeatedly from the monkey in the hospital.
This results from the experience of doctors, who encounter similar cases on the receipts of children's wards of hospitals.
"Some beers or wines are completely unfashionable, because it takes a long time to get drunk with them."
"The trend is definitely hard alcohol," Luděk Pelikán, the head of the children's department at Hořovice Hospital in Central Bohemia, told Právo.
"If they manage to pull Daddy's bottle out of the closet, it's whiskey, otherwise fernet and vodka predominate."
"Vodka has the advantage of pouring it into their juice and there's not so much smell from them," he explained.
Eva Jurinová, spokesperson for Motol University Hospital, agrees.
"It's fernet or rum most often."
"It's modern alcohol today, especially in primary schools."
"In some cases, it was slivovitz they brought from home," Ms Jurin said.
According to Pelikán, thirteen or fourteen-year-old drinkers are a relatively common phenomenon on hospital reception.
Before Hořovice, where he has been working since this year, he worked for ten years on pediatrics in Motol, where ambulances are transporting scattered schoolchildren from a wide area.
"There was one girl lying with us about four times."
"She started her career at the age of twelve, when she was brought to us with an extra 3.5 percent of alcohol in her blood in combination with ecstasy," the doctor described a particularly serious case.
"She was at the exhibition center in Holešovice at a dance party."
"Interestingly, her mother didn't find it odd that she didn't know about her daughter for 48 hours," the chief said.
It is the disinterest of parents or even their considerable tolerance to drinking their own offsprings that is considered to be the main cause of increasing children's drinking.
However, according to experts, it is not possible to talk about dependence in the true sense of the word in children.
"The term alcoholic refers to several-year chronic dependence on alcohol with all the phenomena that belong to it, and this is not the case in children."
"For those who use alcohol repeatedly, it's more of a symptom of an unsuccessful family-social background," Pelikán explained.
According to spokeswoman Jurinová, about forty drunken children were hospitalized at a pediatric clinic in Motol until mid-November.
However, only those with severe intoxication who had to stay in the hospital will enter the statistics.
Children's drunkards, who are taken by their parents after outpatient treatment or taken home by the police, do not count.
In addition to when children just sit down after school and drink a bottle of liquor, according to Pelikán, there are three seasonal fluctuations that can be relied on: witches, New Year's Eve and the end of the school year.
"They're either sipping grief from the wrong report card, or they're celebrating the beginning of the holidays."
"But I've had seven children's drunks on behalf of witches at the same time," Pelican said.
Drunken, who spends the nice by detox under the supervision of doctors, is, paradoxically, at an advantage compared to one who suffers an unpleasant hangover the next day at home.
Morning headaches caused by dehydration and a lack of glucose will drive away irrigation infusions with glucose and nutrients that are lacking for the body.
"They're actually better off, because they've got a good waking-up after that beautiful evening experience, and they don't hurt."
"Only they're confused about where they are," Pelikán admitted, adding that he sometimes spoils patients' pleasant mood at least by listing the costs their treatment required.
The transport by ambulance and hospitalization on an acute bed will cost tens of thousands of crowns.
"Sometimes I used it as an educational means, when I calculated directly to those children how much their drunkenness cost and that they could also spend the whole summer at the brigade to earn for that one party," Pelikán added.
According to him, according to him, health insurance companies do not accede to regression proceedings, in which parents would enforce the costs of treatment, in the cases of children's drunkards.
Two L-159 jets are in foreign hands in Spain for the first time
Two L-159 jets out of a total of five units, for which the Czech Ministry of Defense exchanged the transport aircraft Casa C-295M, on Tuesday flew to Spain's Sevilla, where the manufacturer of Casa - EADS aircraft is based.
It is the first time that machines of this type have appeared owned by someone other than the Czech army.
So far, they have been abroad only for exercises or demonstration years.
These are two-seater versions of the L-159T1, which were still operated by the Czech army and which were airworthy.
The remaining three machines are single-seater L-159.
So far, they stand preserved in Aero Vodochody, and an intermediary of trade - Omnipol - has taken them over last week.
It is not yet known, according to Jan Pejšek from the press department of defense.
"Single-seat L-159 aircraft for exchange have been earmarked from long-term stored aircraft, without additional deposits and investments by the ministry's department."
"The two-seater L-159T1s have been exchanged in the current state and form," Pravu Pejšek said.
He added that the army will not remain without two-seat aircraft, because in May 2009 the Ministry signed a contract with Aero Vodochody worth 200 million crowns for the conversion of four unneeded L-159 machines into two two-seater machines.
One new aircraft was taken over by the army in July, the other will take over by the end of this year.
According to Pejšek, EADS has pledged to put the aircraft into the armament of some alliance countries.
"It is clearly stated in the contract that EADS is the purchaser of the L-159 aircraft and will be used to train pilots from NATO countries."
"It is therefore the customer's business how he will implement this thing," Pejšek said.
Some time ago, Hospodářské noviny reported that, for example, the Spanish army was not interested in the L-159.
The replacement of five L-159s for one Casa aircraft and the purchase of three other aircraft for 3.5 billion crowns was approved last year by the government of Mirek Topolánek.
The Ministry of Defence did not launch a tender for the purchase and addressed EADS directly.
At that time, the leadership of the defense argued that the exchange would support the future exports of L-159 abroad.
The Czech army has bought 72 machines from Aero and uses only 24, the rest she would like to sell.
Buyinging Casa planes is likely to have a trial.
The European Commission sued the Czech Ministry of Defence in a European court because it should have selected aircraft on the basis of a pan-European tender and not by addressing one manufacturer.
The army has already taken over three aircraft Casa, the last is scheduled to arrive at Prague's Kbely airport within a fortnight.
A longer indicator than a ring finger indicates a lower risk of cancer
Men who have an index finger longer than a ringer are at less risk of getting prostate cancer.
That's a surprising conclusion to a study published Wednesday by Britain's Cancer Journal.
It may be related to the masculine hormone testosterone.
"Our results show that the relative length of the finger can serve as a simple prostate cancer risk test, especially for men under the age of 60," said study co-author Ros Eeles, a professor at the Institute for Cancer Research at the University of Warwick.
Thus, the finger could help determine which men should undergo a prostate cancer test, especially in combination with a genetic risk or other threatening factors such as a family health burden, Eeles added.
Between 1994-2009, more than 1,500 prostate cancer patients and more than 3,000 healthy men watched with colleagues in Britain.
More than half of the participants in the study had an index finger on their right hand shorter than a ringer.
For them, it was the same probability of prostate cancer as for 19 percent of those whose fingers were just as long.
In the case of those which had an index finger longer than the ringer, however, this risk dropped by 33 percent, for men up to sixty, the decrease was even 87 percent.
The ratio of the length of the two of these fingers - formed in the prenatal period - apparently reflects the different levels of sex hormones to which the child is exposed in the womb.
Less testosterone corresponds to a longer indicant.
Earlier research has shown that testosterone promotes growing prostate cancer.
Václav Pačes, a leading Czech geneticist and former chairman of the Academy of Sciences, told the Czech News Agency that he was quick to know about the study, but he said it did not appear that the relationship between the length of fingers and cancer was "completely proven".
The Czech Republic is under snow, D1 and D5 are enclosed
The Czech Republic, like most of Europe, is covered in snow on Thursday morning.
On the roads and pavements lies mostly ridden snow, road workers in many places rode all night.
In the morning, the D5 motorway is closed between the 89th and 93rd kilometers in the direction of Rozvadov due to several truck and passenger car accidents, and there is also a D1 near Brno in the direction of Prague.
The dispatcher of the Central Automobile Club (ÚAMK) told news.
On the 91st kilometre there crashed a truck, on the 93rd kilometer collided two trucks, the accident claimed injuries.
Several passenger cars also ended up in a ditch.
There are also problems on the D1 motorway on the 185th kilometre in the direction of Prague.
Two trucks collided there.
Drivers should drive just behind Brno on the 190th kilometer or drive through Brno and then drive on the 182nd kilometre at Kyvalka.
There are multi-kilometre columns.
There is also an accident on the D11 motorway, right on arrival in Prague.
In Prague lies a several-centimeter layer of snow, thermometers show minus seven degrees Celsius.
Columns of cars are formed, for example, in Prosek, clogged is also Štěrboholská.
The surest means of transport is the metro.
Trams run with delay, for example, on the track between Charles Square and Náměstí Míru.
At eight o'clock in the morning, Prague's emergency crew is scheduled to meet again.
Railways and airports are also facing problems.
Czech Railways has already had to cancel seven express connections due to snow.
For example, for example, the line from Prague to Kralupy or from Prague to Beroun has problems.
Passengers should check the current situation.
Ruzyne Airport was closed from Wednesday 11 a.m
Most of the roads in South Moravia are already passable, but only with caution.
There are three side roads impassable in the Vyškov region, snow tongues are formed in the Brno region and snow chains are necessary to climb some roads in higher places.
Vyškovsko was one of the most affected places.
So far there are closed paths: Rousínov - Slavkov, Bohdalice - Kučerov and also Šaratice - Milešovice.
It will be necessary to put cutters until they are stretched.
When they will be cleared cannot yet be estimated.
Caution should be exercised in the White Carpathians region in Hodonín.
On the roads, which are maintained only by an inert sprinkle due to nature conservation, there is up to a five-centimeter layer of snow, ČTK said.
In the Czech Republic, from two to ten inches of snow fell, the most fell in northern Bohemia, often up to 18 centimeters.
About 15 inches of snow fell in the Moravian-Silesian Region.
Snowing in the Czech Republic is gradually ceasing.
U.S. soldiers have new "smart gun"
The U.S. military has deployed a new "smart" weapon in Afghanistan, known so far as the XM-25.
Generals hope that a new automatic rifle will affect the conflict with the Taliban and "change the game" in their favor.
The new weapon is like a small super-accurate cannon.
Its projectiles are guided by a laser with an accuracy of several meters.
Special 25 mm caliber cartridges, developed for this rifle and containing highly explosive explosive, effectively act as small grenades.
Bullets can be programmed to explode not only on impact to the target, but at any point of their ballistic track.
It can therefore be used against an enemy that hides behind some obstacle, behind a wall or in a trench.
It is usually not protected from above, and when the murderous rain of steel sharves descends on it, it can knock it out of combat.
According to Richard Audetto, who was involved in developing the XM-25, the new rifle is the first infantry weapon to use "smart" technology.
"In principle, the soldier treats her by finding a target, sends a laser beam that determines the distance, thus obtains a specified intentional point, adjusts the direction according to him, and presses the tap."
"Let's say you measured 543 meters."
"When you pull the trigger, the gun charges and fires a missile at a distance of 543 meters plus minus one, two or three meters," Audette said in a statement.
It will relocate 400 jobs
The Fujitsu Group will move its office from Bad Homburg to Frankfurt.
400 employees will move their jobs in July next year from the existing administrative building to Marienbader-square in Bad Homburg to Astro Park.
The Japanese company Fujitsu will move its office from Bad Homburg to Frankfurt.
On Wednesday, the industry giant, which specialises in computers and their maintenance, will move 400 men and women's jobs in July next year from an existing administrative building to Marienbader-Squares in Bad Homburg to Astro Park, a large administrative complex in Lyonska Street, in a part of Frankfurt called Niederrad.
According to Fujist spokespersons, another 100 jobs of the group, which are still divided in the region, should be moved there anytime soon.
The offer of the lease was said to be very attractive, and large-scale offices in Astro Park are said to be better suited to the working way of employees.
Also in Bad Homburg, Fujitsu was rented only.
Competent Managing Director Bernd Wagner said, "in Frankfurt-Niederrad, we found a central headquarters with excellent infrastructure."
An excellent connection with the airport is very important for a global company like Fujitsu.
The recent relocation of an important business within the region sparked joy yesterday for the winners and disappointment among the losers.
Markus Frank (CDU), a senior economic officer in Frankfurt, said he managed to get a "global player."
It's a "fantastic display of confidence in this location."
Fujitsu is said to suit the city, the settlement is an important impetus for Niederrad.
Bad Homburg mayor Michael Korwis (Greens) regretted Fujitsu's decision.
The city is said to have been informed by the company's management in a timely manner about its intention.
"Of course we have offered our help to the company," Korwisi said.
However, friendly contacts could not change the difficulties of the computer maker with the landlord of the property in Marienbader Square.
As a result, economic reasons were decisive.
It is now a matter of avoidinging a long non-occupancy in such a prominent location.
"Hopefully the owner and landlord of the building will be cooperative."
The house on Rathaus-square, which until now was used by Fujitsu, and the neighbouring "Kleine Vertikale", were among the most discussed building intentions of the spa town ten years ago, along with the other buildings on Marienbader Square.
Between the railway station and the town hall was created as a joint project of the city and a private designer of the so-called city entree, which was criticized both because of the massive development of areas that previously served as a parking lot and also because of the height of the buildings.
In 2001, the company of investment consulting and rating Feri, the manufacturer of software for travel distribution systems, Start Amadeus, as well as the computer maker Fujitsu, which was still associated with Siemens at the time, moved into the houses.
The buildings used by Fujitsu were acquired jointly in 2006 by Frankfurter Deutsche Immobilien-Chancen-Gruppe and the Morgan Stanley Real Estate Fund, and after a year they were sold the Spanish Falcon Real Estate Investments SA in Madrid.
The Fujitsu-concern from Tokyo is referred to as the world's third-largest IT-provider.
It offers, for example, computers and servers available to companies and also takes care of the necessary service.
The Rhine and Mohan branch is one of the largest in Germany, but most of Fujitsu’s employees are in Munich.
From Bad Homburg moves to Frankfurt-Niederrad a subsidiary of Fujitsu Technology Solutions, other subsidiaries in this location will be Fujitsu Services and a company called TDS.
In the Astro Park administrative building, 7000 square meters were rented for this purpose.
In the complex - its name draws attention to the starch floor plan, which is twenty years old, it also houses, among other things, the central union of the electrical and electronics industries.
Amazon shuts down the server - threats against Assange
WikiLeaks is getting tight.
The Amazon Internet service has turned off the unveiling platform on its servers.
It is possible that this happened under political pressure after the publication of hundreds of thousands of secret diplomatic dispatches.
WikiLeaks founder Assange is reportedly abstaining in the UK.
Wikileaks, the unveiling platform, is coming under increasing pressure in the United States.
Internet activists have lost access to their existing home server in America and are now looking for an internet home in Europe.
Meanwhile, prominent U.S. Senator Joe Lieberman urged other countries and businesses to boycott WikiLeaks.
WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange, who is listed wanted by Interpol, is scheduled to stay according to one newspaper post in the UK.
"Wikileaks is no longer on Amazon."
"Free speech in the land of the free," the activists told, posting short-lived news to Twitter on Wednesday (local time).
Shortly before that, the internet company Amazon had withdrawn WikiLeaks documents from its servers.
Internet activists have used Amazon Web Service (AWS) in the publication of classified diplomatic cables to handle a high number of access to documents.
Using Twitter, WikiLeaks explained the move: "Fine - we will now spend our dollars by employing people in Europe."
When Amazon has such problems with free speech, "then they should stop selling books."
Amazon halted the use of its WikiLeaks servers after collaborators of Senator Joe Lieberman conducted the search, CNN reported, based on information from Liebermana's office.
The chairman of the Senate's home defense committee threatened Amazon with a boycott, Britain's "Guardian" reported.
“I used to want Amazon to take this step earlier, given the earlier disclosures of classified information on WikiLeaks,” he said.
Lieberman, meanwhile, urged other countries to prevent a platform to use servers.
"Wikileaks acts illegally, blatantly and recklessly, putting our national security in danger and endangering life on the entire planet."
"No responsible firm - either U.S. or foreign - should help WikiLeaks in their efforts to disseminate stolen material."
WikiLeaks founder Assange, who is wanted by Swedish authorities on suspicion of rape, is on Interpol's wanted list.
Based on a report in the London newspaper "The Independent", the 39-year-old Australian in the UK is said to know the exact location.
An internet activist has always referred to an investigation in Sweden as intriguing his adversaries.
WikiLeaks spokesman Kristinn Hrafnsson said there had also been threats.
"Even there were calls for the assassination of Julian Assange."
That's which Assange rightly fears for his safety, Hrafnsson said.
That's why WikiLeaks will continue to hide.
Hrafnsson apparently reacted to remarks from North America.
In America, the former Republican governor of Arkansas, Mike Huckabee, demanded that a person be held accountable on WikiLeaks because of a betrayal accused and executed.
In Canadana, one adviser to the head of the government, Stephen Harper, said on television - apparently ironically, Assange should be "murdered" and Obama could use a "target plane."
In the meantime, a verbal firefight unfolded between the White House and Assange.
U.S. President Barack Obama's spokesman, Robert Gibbs, called it "ridiculous and absurd" that Assange demanded the resignation of Secretary of State Hillary Clinton if he was responsible for the recently released espional calls to diplomats.
"I'm not quite sure why we're interested in the opinion of one person with websites," Gibbs said.
"Our foreign policy and the interests of this country are much more significant than its website."
The U.S. government is now extensively investigating the security of its databanks.
A deputy director of the Center for Anti-Terrorism Measures has been appointed special officer, Russell Travers, the White House said.
Travers became responsible for the development of "necessary reforms in the structure" that are necessary following the publication of reports from WikiLeaks.
The White House also wants to review ways in which the entire government exchanges and protects information.
The U.S. government has also announced that it wants to take care of the safety of dissidents and activists if they are in danger due to WikiLeaks.
"We've done everything we can to get in touch with them," State Department spokesman Philip Crowley said.
"We're ready to protect them if that's necessary."
Ice Cream University
At "Gelato University" near Bologna, ice ice ice confectioners are preparing for a better time of year.
There is a lot of interest in the basic course for future ice icemakers, which lasts three days, and visitors come from all over the world.
Where does this ice cream come from?
From Gelatiere and from Gelataio.
Gelatiere, a slightly obsolete ice cream maker, produces cream according to all the rules of Italian ice cream art.
Gelataio removes the ice cream from the cooling box with a squeegee, wipes it in a wafer and sells it.
To find out, you don't have to go to Anzola dell'Emilia in the province of Bologna.
However, if you want to learn even more about ice cream, you will not avoid "Gelato-University" of the Italian company Carpigiani.
In 2003, an ice ice ice ice machine maker set up an ice cream university to match Italy's high ideals everywhere.
There is a huge interest in the basic course for the future Gelatieri, which lasts three days, and visitors come from all over the world.
However, there are also several-day special courses about chocolate ice cream and a practitioner in your own ice cream shop.
Someone longs for a new career in their Midlife-Crisis, others venture on the thin ice of their obscure desires.
This time it is 40 participants from 19 countries of all continents - from Brazil through Hungary to Saudi Arabia.
Jacky, an art teacher from California, loves Slow-Food movement and would like to offer a high-quality European product in the country of industrial Ice-Cream in the future.
Anke from Berlin is a corporate consultant and would like to start something of her own after spending years of stressful work.
Two young Egyptian women are dreaming of opening an ice cream shop in Kair.
One man from Malta and one from Brazil want to improve the quality of ice cream in their bars - and the Canadian housewife Julia has arrived because her children love Italian ice cream.
Maestro-Gelatiere Luciano Ferrari will first explain to the participants the ideal features of the perfect ice cream.
It is not only a good taste, but also silky softness and good consistency.
Luciano won't forget during his training the Southern and patriotic icing on the cake: "Italian Gelato is simply the best - and then there's a lot of romance."
The first step of production seems quite simple.
Luciano puts the basic ingredients of milk, whipped cream, milk powder, sugar and stabilizers into a large blender, which heats everything with an electronic program to 85 degrees and then immediately cools down to four degrees.
Then he turns the tap on the machine and drains the pasteurized white base cream, the basis of all kinds of milk ice cream.
Immediately, the associate professor is filled with questions: Is it possible to make ice cream only from natural substances without stabilizer?
How can a man experiment himself?
Luciano warns to get carried away by the spirit of the times into the unknown.
Whoever talks about ice cream "only from natural ingredients" apparently used stabilizers anyway - mostly a natural product, such as guar flour from guar beans.
Of course, it is possible to make ice cream without stabilizers, but it must be sold in one day, otherwise it will collide and the customer eats all iced beans.
Certain variations of basic matter are allowed.
It's going to be more exciting with aromatic essences.
Maestro Luciano distinguishes the taste of ice cream according to the ingredients that are bought in the supermarket either in the department with fruit or in the candy department.
In addition, there are powders and creams.
"Suppliers will try to impose a lot of aromatized additives on you."
"You should simply experiment."
Either with fruit sorbets with sugar and fresh fruit.
Or with an ice cream from a white base mass, which turns the eggs into yellow cream and cocoa to the basis for all sorts of chocolate temptations.
Participants are now allowed to participate in an ice cream machine course.
Luciano lets them always make four different kinds of vanilla, chocolate, coffee and hazeline ice cream - either with or without eggs in basic matter, with more or less vanilla aroma or other natural ingredients.
The same experiment is carried out with fruit types of ice cream.
More aromatic substances, more fruit puree - due to the taste it is not always better.
Hazelnut ice cream doesn't have to taste more hazelnut when it contains more hazelnuts.
The most popular ice cream from kiwi will be produced by a group that will use more fruit, less water and a little less stabilizer.
It is only when the balanced mixture highlights the taste of Gelato correctly, Luciano says.
The production of ice cream is a purely numerical task: If peach puree with a lot of sugar is used, the proportion of sugar in the basic recipe must be reduced.
For lemon ice cream, an extra serving of sugar is needed.
Luciano removes the device by which it determines the content of sugar in the fruit (winemakers thus measure the cider of grapes).
Luckily, the tables make the numbers easier.
Making ice cream no longer requires as much bodily effort as before.
It does not only help the device for the automatic production of basic matter.
There is also a device that mixes the finished ice ice cream at the same time and cools to minus the temperature.
Despite all the automation, we can't avoid minor accidents.
Brazainese Gustavo gets himself dirty and also his colleagues from top to bottom with a fruit puree when a huge blender gets out of control.
"In every course," Maestro Luciano believes, "it's only a matter of time for something like this to happen."
Revisors in dual light
The European Food Authority Efsa is linked to an industry that experiments with genetic plants.
This accusation has now been voiced by Testbiotech experts, which considers itself an independent institute for estimating the consequences of biotechnology.
“It sounds like a conspiracy theory, but we’ve documented it well,” Testbiotech CEO Christoph Then said in Munich on Wednesday.
Efsa's leading group of experts for gentechnically altered plants, Harry Kuiper, and another Efsa expert, have had a contract with Ilsi's lobbist union for several years.
It is funded by Monsanto, Nestle or Bayer.
Kuiper participated decisively in Efsa control regulations for genetic plants and thus stands in a clear conflict of interest, criticising Then.
Efsa's regulations coincide in whole paragraphs with Ilsi's documents and thus bear the manuscript of the industry, which should be controlled by an office in Parma, Italy.
This is the second time Testbiotech has highlighted a conflict of interest.
Recently, a senior executive from Efsa switched without additions or a transitional period to the Swiss agrarian concern Syngenta - and in doing so changed sides.
The Radio for Central Germany (MDR) has also recently unveiled the personnel interconnection of the EU-office for control with the industrial lobby, which has attracted the interest of both the Ministry of the Environment and EU MPs.
In the meantime, Efsa can't recognize a conflict of interest.
It works with all serious data, i.e. also those from the industry, if they are of highly professional quality, is stated in one statement.
Experts who work for Efsa aren't asked not to have contacts with the industry.
But he has to prove it.
Kuiper admits his work for the International Life Sciences Institute (Ilsi) in the USA.
It describes itself as a neutral forum of experts from science, industry and politics - among other things, to estimate the risk of biotechnology without a lobbyist's contract.
Independent experts such as Then criticise that gentechnically altered plants are currently allowed in animals without any feeding experiment, and that there is non-test reaction of genetic plants to environmental influences.
"We want to know, for example, what gentechnically altered corn will do in the field," says Then.
The current practice is only to test insulated proteins and not the entire genetic plant, which does not provide reliable results.
"If they're not looking for the risks, then how could they be found?" he asks lawsuist Then.
Angelika Hilbeck agrees.
The scientist has been researching gentechnik since 1994 and is currently working at the Technical College of Zurich.
In this area, as in the U.S., the industry is now increasingly influencing or discrediting critical scientists in Europe.
Therefore, there are almost no scientists who are no longer connected with concerns.
There are mostly no studies that are critical for companies.
“The industry controls security research,” Hilbeck said.
A trace of stones
Uli Gsell also bought some, a total of about 400 kilograms.
On Monday evening he stood in front of the station in Stuttgart, a tube on his back.
It was a demonstration.
It was snowing.
The story is known: In September, excavators arrived and began tearing down a large chunk of the northern wing.
The world in Stuttgart was not the same anymore: Thousands demonstrated against the S21, a billion-dollar reduction of the railway station.
When the cessation of the building was reached, most of the wing lay in ruins.
Broken into 6000, 7000 pieces.
Limestone from Crailsheim, a soft coarse stone that was used in 1914 and 1920.
Gsell is a sculptor in Ostfildern, 43.
He's secured a few of these pieces.
"These stones are relics."
"It must not disappear."
It unravels: "Cheap and poor stone, simple and primordial."
When he cut one, he smelled of cigarette smoke.
He doesn't know what he's going to do with the pieces yet.
Stones have time.
Uli Gsell too.
A trace of stones from Stuttgart leads into the forest near Tübingen to the former deponia of ammunition of the French army, where Manuel Rongen built his stone realm.
A 44-year-old man trades old rocks.
He bought all of them, which originated during the demolition in Stuttgart.
“It would be an embarrassment if they grinded into gravel or disappeared into deponia,” he says in his office, a wooden booth that used to be a guardhouse.
He recounts that they scolded him and threatened him when, in September, all the newspaper said that he had bought the remains of a northern wing from a company carrying out demolition.
But he's been talking to S-21 opponents for days.
And he explained to them that he wasn't responsible for demolition at all.
And when it's happening, it's the most reasonable when the stones continue to be used.
Then it took about 200 S-21-adversaries each one about 80 kilograms heavy block, the price per piece 6.50 to 14 euros.
As a memory, some also in the hope of returning it during re-construction.
Rongen bought 500 tons, a load of 25 large trucks.
About half are still in the woods, the rest have long ago been sold.
Gardeners, landscape gardeners, home builders.
They came from all corners and towed what fit in the trunk and the trailer.
Stones of a dispute from Stuttgart have long ago been transformed into "walls" or as the edges of flower beds with herbs.
“This stone isn’t really anything special,” says Rongen.
"If it wasn't for the political nipple."
He gets into his off-road car and goes for a ride through his 20-hectare kingdom.
He studied law first, interrupted his studies, later biology, then became interested in computers, and eventually became a stone merchant.
And a lover of stones.
It has 40000 tons of stones.
They will collect their treasures from all over Germany.
He says he's the world's largest old-stone merchant in the country.
Brakes.
In the snow lies artistically worked sandstone.
"Once they belonged to Marien-most in Dresden," he recounts.
Sandstone from the Elbe, yellow, warm, he's thrilled.
"My favorite stone."
We're moving on our way up through the forest.
It points to a pile: red sandstone, formerly a part of Karlsruhe railway station.
And beyond.
"Nice travertine."
Once belonged those blocks to the Neccarstadion grandstand in Stuttgart.
Everywhere in the snow lies rocks and stories, Cannstatt-travertine from the police presidium in Stuttgart, a red granite from the Sankt Blasien temple, a tuf from a hospital in Heilbronn.
What a job, what an effort, what a sweat.
When Rongen thinks about how stones used to be smashed by hand and transported with simple hoists and bullish wagons, he becomes completely respectful.
"It'd be a scandal if something like this were crushed."
He didn't become wealthy, not very wealthy at all.
Today, however, the stone dealer employs 14 people, and in addition, stonemasons, one sculptor, one blacksmither, one glassmaker and one gardener have settled up in the forest.
“We’re working hard, but it’s satisfying,” says the one who appreciates the leftovers.
They have to go to Cologne soon.
Stones will be rescued there, too.
Talent struggle for high-potentials
There's a lot of people for snacks.
16 students are pressed by sandwiches with salmon, tomato soups and candy.
The Technical University of Braunschweig and car manufacturer Volkswagen have invited students of engineering and design to a workshop.
Together with VW's developers, they're supposed to talk about the car of the future here ₸om - and, by the way, get to know Volkswagen.
Many companies are trying to succeed and bet on co-operation with universities in the race, as does VW.
Their mission is: As soon as possible to recruit the most gifted graduates in auditoriums, even before they reach the labour market and are interested in others.
Since universities do not publish any lists of the success of their students, companies try to discover exceptional talents in practice-oriented projects and get them.
At TU Braunschweig, the automotive concern is trying to gain talent the next day.
One young lady from the personal department is handing out pens, gummy teddy bears and plates with the VW logo.
It tells promising young talents about programs for practice, student jobs and suggests their trips to the group.
Nico Selle from TU Braunschweig takes care of the project together with VW.
They see advantages for both sides: "Students come into contact with the industry and gain experience."
"VW can simultaneously get to know students, become more familiar with interesting candidates, and think about who would come in handy as a collaborator."
Such co-operations, according to Selle, are not uncommon.
But the news is that students are so openly fought.
"Otherwise, it was more of an important professional project," Selle says.
The fight for recent graduates will probably be even tougher.
In many sectors, companies can no longer choose their employees from long crowds of applicants.
On the contrary, they have to try and try to attract a particularly highly skilled workforce.
In particular, graduates of engineering disciplines have an excellent negotiating position.
According to data from the Union of German Engineers, 40,000 engineering positions remained vacant in October.
And according to VDI's predictions, the so-called shortage of engineers will still grow.
Roman Dykta is the auditing and consulting firm of KPMG for talent hunting.
It organizes challenging Recruiting-events, such as KPMG-Race, a kind of modern treasure race in which participants have to find their way according to GSP coordinates.
The company plans to send eight promising students to the U.S. next year.
Participants can also visit KPMG branches and get acquainted with the company.
In advance, he will decide in two selection rounds who will participate.
Only Crème de la Crème from many interested parties will fly to the USA.
According to Dykta, there will be ever-to-tougher laws in the fight for High Potentials in universities.
"We want to be different from others with our Race."
"A simple trip on a sailboat was too little for us."
Looks like talent hunter Dykta's lucky.
"We have stayed in touch with all participants of a similar event last year."
“Some of them will end their studies soon and are planning to apply for a job with us,” he says.
In addition to direct addressing on campus, mainly small and medium-sized companies present themselves classically in the labour markets.
"We're trying to contact graduates at vocational exhibitions."
"Such as Intersolar," according to Sitha Stübe, head of the solar technology manufacturer Solarworld.
Bonn employs about 1500 employees in Germany.
Stübe knows the issue with young workers.
There are currently 35 positions on the company’s website.
"At the moment it's very difficult to occupy vacancies."
"It costs a lot of time and money," he says.
Another option is contact through professors at universities.
Solarworld cooperates with various universities, e.g. the University of Freiberg.
"Professors inform that it is possible to do a practice with us or to write a thesis."
A solar specialist doesn't organize any expensive advertising events.
Instead, the company strives to attract other advantages: personal atmosphere, low hierarchy and light communication.
"Great concerns often can't offer this," says the head of Stübe's personal department.
When looking for a job, money doesn’t play a major role.
Flexible working hours, caring for children or having a good option are other decisive factors.
Solarworld placed 55th among the top 100 employers in Germany in one Great Place to Work.
The institute compiles an annual list of Germany's best employers.
In addition, he asks co-workers how satisfied they are with their work and working environment and monitors the framework conditions of employees.
The head of Stübe's personal department hopes that the placement will bring her points for those interested in work.
“I believe that interested parties are particularly informed about the external evaluations of a potential employer,” he says.
Wolf Reiner Kriegler of the German Employer Branding Academy advises companies who want to build a long-term strategy with a good reputation as an employer.
It advises its customers to be more authentic.
And according to Mr Kriegler, this means, above all, internal measures.
“The best way to make yourself visible as an attractive employer leads through their own employees,” he says.
Professionally, this is called the internal Employer Branding, i.e. enforcing an employer on the market within their own employees.
"Satisfied employees are the best envoys."
When a company takes good care of its people and they identify with the company, then they will do advertising by themselves to their employers between friends and acquaintances.
"It's like playing with a string," Kriegler says.
Obama halts new oil wells
Over the next few years, there will be no new oil drillings in front of the U.S.-Global State of Florida.
The government in Washington has issued an appropriate ban - and in doing so has definitively scrapped earlier plans.
Also in front of the Atlantic coast so far, new wells are banned.
Interior Minister Ken Salazar said Wednesday.
The reason for the strict ban, which is expected to apply for at least the next five years, is an explosion on the "Deepwater Horizon" drilling tower in April.
Prior to this misfortune, which cost eleven employees their lives and led to the biggest environmental disaster in the U.S., President Barack Obama signaled under pressure from opposition and industry his willingness to allow new wells in the eastern part of the Gulf of Mexico.
Proponents are trying to reduce U.S. dependence on oil supplies from abroad.
To date, it is not unequivocally explained who bears responsibility for the devastating oil scourge in the bay.
The focus of the criticism is Britain's energetic BP concern, which is confronted by billion-dollar requests.
The commission of inquiry, commissioned by Obama, is due to submit results in December.
In October, Home Secretary Ken Salazar lifted the drilling ban that has been in effect since the explosion.
Subsequently, various oil companies submitted applications for new drilling intentions.
Obama and Salazar want to prevent a similar disaster with stricter controls and strict safety regulations.
At the moment, the audit office is fundamentally being reformed, drawing attention to itself with many corrupt accusations.
At the same time, Obama announced that it would abolish lucrative options for tax evasions for oil and gas concerns.
The industry now openly warns that stricter regulation will fundamentally reduce the volume of extracted oil in the bay.
"Government's plans are the biggest brake on the growth of the gas and oil industries," reads the result of a current survey in the field by consulting firm BDO - even before the drilling ban on Wednesday.
The American Energy Alliance complains about uncertainty in the industry: “No one knows exactly what regulation will change.”
Investments in new mining projects are shifting until further notice - at the expense of growth.
Ogoni area burned down
The water in the rivers is oil-brown, it smells like at the pump: billions of liters of leaked oil have devastated nature in the Ogoni region of Nigeria.
People feed on stealing, corruption, and illegally cooking oil.
Celestine Akpobari means well.
"Don't worry," reassures reporters an environmental activist: "Today you're going to get enough oil mud in front of the lens."
The 37-year-old Nigerian watched an attempt to photograph a boy wading his breash with water surrounded by smudges: the scene looks almost nice in a water gloss with rainbow colors.
The fact that there are brown clots of oil floating beneath the surface is not visible.
Earlier, she collected enough garnels in containers, Esther Badom tells the banks of the Bodo-Jetta River.
In the meantime, the slowly flowing water has become completely dead.
Five years ago, large amounts of oil poured out into a huge warming area from a broken lead.
Fishermen from the nearby town of Bodo must paddle for at least six hours to get to the waters where there are fish: the Ogoni area, located in the heart of the Niger River Delta, is one of the hardest-infested areas in the world.
On the way down the river with mangrove swamps with a lot of lateral tributaries, the entire range of the trigger is noticeable.
An engine on a boat boils brown porridge instead of bright water, and whoever plunges his hand into the water pulls out his oily fingers.
The roots of mangroves rise from the ground like black rods of licorice, the rarely-occurring palm trees leave their leaves sadly hanging.
In vain, we are looking for flocks of birds, which are commonly found in humid areas: Once the engine stops on a boat, it spreads in an earlier paradise, where it smells like at the pump, haunting silence.
“What’s going on here is the ecological leadership of the war,” says Celestine Akpobari sadly: “We’re living in a constant fear for health.”
Comparison with war is justified.
The Ogoni settlement area is a battlefield, where it has been fighting for several decades: more than 60 years ago, the first conquerors from Europe flocked here, where in the 1980s the first resistance with the later executed writer Ken Saro-Wiwa took shape, here Shell had to halt its production in 1993, because it was too dangerous for employees.
To this day, the company must not return to the Ogoni region: while it benefits daily from the three-size delta region of Switzerland, with more than 5,500 mining wells and 7000 kilometers of management more than two million barrels of black gold, oil fields in the Ogoni region lie fallow, the situation is too unstable for the group.
Every few hundred meters rises from the water of the steel structure like bizarre sculptures: "Christmas trees", so they are too tenderly called the heads of drilling holes.
They're 50 years old, most up to marrow rusted.
Every now and then, such a head of a drill hole breaks, narrates Akpobari, and then a constant stream of oil flows into the water.
According to experts, more than two billion liters have flowed into the delta ecosystem in 50 years: Every year the amount of oil spilled into the sea near Alaska during the "Exxon Valdez" supertanker crash 21 years ago - one of the biggest environmental disasters in human history.
There are controversies over who is responsible for the continuing environmental disaster.
While Shell refers to sabotage and organized oil theft for more than 90 percent of pollution, environmental protectionists refer to the cracking heads of wells and leaky lines as the main cause.
Shell requires much less safety regulations in Nigeria than is customary in the world, U.S.-U.S. scientist Richard Steiner alluded in 2008: “Only a number of failures in management are said to be “several times higher than elsewhere.”
In a place that looks deserted from a distance, an oily-stained open-air workshop emerges from the mangroves of the figure: they are completely naked and up to their necks from the oil.
Under one of the many barrels, which are partly united by leadership, partially arbitrarily scattered around the ground, a fire is burning: an illegal small refinery.
Oil has been heated in the barrel over the fire for several hours, explains Michael, the group's leader.
At first, gasoline flows out of the lead into the catch pot, later dripping at the end of kerosine's lead, and finally diesel.
In this way Michael and his four collaborators produce up to four barrels of 300 litres a day: "We'll make 34,000 Naira" - about 22 dollars.
Residents of the delta are taking advantage of chronic fuel shortages in Black Africa's largest oil state.
Nigeria owns only one refinery.
Ridiculously low price of 40 cents of euros per litre of diesel can still overwhelm illegal producers: because they have free raw material.
It comes from "bunkers" who connect to management everywhere in the Ogoni region: according to experts, at least 16 million litres of oil are lost every day across the Niger River Delta.
But only a negligibly small part of the temporarily stored raw material is illegally refined: The largest share will be filled by organized criminal networks, which include politicians and high-ranking representatives of the army, into tankers and sell it on the world market.
The Akpobari activist considers the debate about whether they caused the disaster and their interventions or their lax method of production to be purely academic.
"If oil companies weren't considered invaders, and if they didn't completely destroy the nature of fishermen, there wouldn't be sabotage or theft and illegal refineries."
In the meantime, Ogoni's relationship with Shell is so damaged that only the group's departure comes into consideration: "Perhaps other peoples like the Chinese will turn out to be better."
Michael is uneasy.
"Supreme time for you to disappear," says a muscular manufacturer.
"The soldiers can come every now and then."
If they were found by a special military unit, they would not be in danger of prison, but a painful bribe.
Also soldiers, who mostly come from the north of the country - like all Nigerians - want to profit from the raw material.
"This damn thing got our country to the bottom in all respects," Akpobari says.
Investigators have discovered a dangerous building error in A380 engines
Apparently, the A380 has a major safety glitch.
Investigators found a possible construction error during an inspection of the machine that had made an emergency landing, which could lead to a "catastrophic engine failure."
Airline Qantas wants to sue engine manufacturers.
Qantas airbus passengers seemed to have had more luck during an emergency landing in early November than has previously been known: Australia's aviation safety authority found a major safety glitch on the engines of some A380 machines in its inquiries, and is now warning of a possible construction error.
A possible glitch would have severe consequences: on one oil connection, cracks could arise due to wear-and-term, the ATSB statement said in a statement published this Thursday.
This could lead to a "catastrophic engine failure."
These are engines made by Rolls-Royce in 20 A380 aircraft.
Among them is Qantas airbus, which had to make an emergency landing in Singapore on November 4.
An Australian airline has now taken legal action against Britain's turbin turbine maker.
She filed the reasoning for the lawsuit before the Australian Federal Court in Sydney, which allows for further legal measures against the company.
Qantas boss Alan Joyce said his company would seek compensation for the incident in early November.
During an engine fire, parts were released and these damaged the load-bearing surfaces.
Rolls-Royce described burning oil as the cause of the damage.
Up to 40 engines must be replaced around the world after this event.
Australian investigators have announced they will conduct further safety tests with giant aircraft that are equipped with the engines in question.
The safety recommendation is the result of cooperation with Rolls-Royce, said ATSB spokesperson Martin Dolan.
The security shortage was considered important enough for everyone concerned to be informed.
After an emergency landing of the Qantas aircraft, the airline left its A380 fleet on the ground for several weeks.
Singapore Airlines has left three of its eleven A380s temporarily on ground.
Lufthansa replaced one A380 engine as a precaution.
The two-storey aircraft is the world's largest passenger plane.
Nationwide snowfall will create chaos in transportation
Germany all in white: it was snowing almost all over the country overnight.
There is chaos on many roads, connections by trains and planes are disturbed.
At least three people have died in traffic accidents.
Winter is chilling Germany: almost the whole of the republic felt snow in the night to Thursday at freezing temperatures - leading in many places to traffic chaos.
In the north and to the east lies in some places up to 15 centimeters of snow.
There is severely limited traffic on several motorways and many roads.
On the A7 near Flensburg, the road service was still deployed, but could not handle the new snow.
There was also strong gusty winds over the Baltic Sea.
The Federal Office for Navigation and Hydrography (BSH) expects a tumultuous torrentia of 1.2 meters above normal on the coast of the Baltic Sea in Schleswig-Holstein in the morning.
Snow drifts have caused significant problems in Saxony on highways and state roads.
According to Meteomedia's bad-weather headquarters, there was therefore a level of alarm in Saxon Switzerland - the highest level ever.
In part, 30 centimeters of new snow fell there, the strong and fresh winds causing drifts.
There were several trucks and passenger cars at night.
Also in Saxony-Anhalt, police authorities reported major difficulties on the roads.
On the A2, wind and snow caused slow traffic and constipation.
There were problems in Thuringia because of snow on the A4 and A9.
According to the German Railway, great difficulties occur in Saxony, Saxony-Anhalt, Thuringia and northern Bavaria.
The main problems are snow on the tracks, overturned trees on the tracks, or broken top lines and turnouts.
In Saxony, long-distance traffic from Leipzig to Nuremberg had to be halted.
One ICE had to stop to Munich in Saalfeld, Thuringia.
165 passengers received wool blankets, food and drink on the train from firefighters and paramedics.
There was a snow through the roads.
Many flights have been canceled at major German airports due to snow winds.
According to a spokeswoman for flight information headquarters, annulated flights have already been reported from Munich.
40 flights have already been canceled in Frankfurt, according to Fraport spokesperson.
The maintenance of roads is in constant commitment.
Although there are few passengers left at the airport without connections, there are 400 field beds ready for them.
There were accidents on many roads on Wednesday night.
In the evening, one truck driver died in a collision with a train at a railway crossing in eastern Holstein.
The driver was slightly injured.
Due to the snowy road, there was a similar accident near Nuremberg: there was also a truck at a railway crossing because the vehicle was stuck.
The driver has died.
Four passengers were injured on the train - the route from Bayreuth to Nuremberg remained closed until the morning hours.
And a 30-year-old woman died after she flipped onto a slippery road near Aschaffenburg with her car.
Meteorologists, however, have good news: snow will remain in the form of light powder, and therefore it will be lifted even with light gusts of wind.
But the wind weakens.
Only by the Baltic Sea will there be rapid winds with the strength of the gale.
But it's going to be very cold - locally up to minus 15 degrees.
Deutsche Bank is looking for buyers for towers-twins
Deutsche Bank wants to say goodbye to its sign: according to one newspaper contribution, the bank is looking for buyers for two towers with offices in Frankfurt headquarters.
The property could yield up to 500 million euros.
Deutsche Bank has just renovated its headquarters of the group: the towers-twies in Frankfurt's banking district have modernized; in the meantime, bankers have once again been allowed for their old jobs.
Now, however, the financial concern apparently wants the cost of the renovation back again - and is looking for buyers for both skyscrapers, according to the "Financial Times Deutschland," citing a spokesperson.
The spokesperson did not name the reasons for the planned sale.
"We are still investigating whether it is more meaningful to own or sell real estate and subsequently rent again," he said.
In doing so, Germany’s largest credit institute is once again renouncing its corporate headquarters after a short period of time.
The bank only took over the towers-twins in mid-2007 for 272 million euros from investors of their own fund and subsequently expensively renovated them.
According to the data, the energy reconstruction swallowed about 200 million euros.
The sale would probably bring a lot of money to the bank.
According to the newspaper text, industry experts estimate the value of previously renamed towers to "Greentowers" at a price of around 500 million euros.
A spokesperson said it was not decided whether the building would be sold again to the fund's assets or to an independent investor.
Thousands grieve for dead miners
More than 10,000 people have honoured the memory of buried miners in New Zealand who have been pronounced dead after several explosions in the mine.
It has still not been able to rescue the dead, because the security situation in the shaft remains complicated.
Nearly two weeks ago, there was a powerful explosion in Pike River Mine, near Greymouth.
29 miners between the ages of 17 and 62 were overwhelmed at the mine.
A probe was lowered, but it did not discover any trace of life into the shaft where the disaster occurred.
Due to high concentrations of poisonous gases and subsequent explosions, rescue forces were unable to descend due to safety.
After a second strong detonation, authorities and experts assumed that miners had no chance to survive.
It has still failed to rescue the dead.
They have now honored the memory of dead relatives and friends.
Tens of thousands of people gathered in Greymouth on New Zealand's south island in sunny weather.
They were silencing for two minutes.
To honor the memory of the buried miners, 29 tables were built, on them helmets, flashlights and the names of miners.
Also pictures and personal belongings, such as clothes and surfboards found their place here.
On state-run government buildings, flags waved at half-mast.
New Zealand had hoped that miners would again come out of the "depths of the earth" to the surface, New Zealand's Prime Minister John Key said.
"But they've never come home."
He can't say goodbye to his son until he's seen his corpse, said Lawrie Drew, whose son Zen has been swayed in a mine.
In addition to the prime minister and the relatives of the victims, several foreign diplomats also participated in the commemorative event.
British and South African miners were also overwhelmed during the November 19 explosion.
Paramedics began by extinguishing a fire inside a coal mine on New Zealand's southern island.
Once poisonous gases are dissolved, the cooling of high temperatures inside the mine will begin, Police President Gary Knowles told reporters.
To hell, warriors!
Is a horror movie suitable for an art cinema?
Director Nicolas Winding Refn is attempting to cross old borders again with his combat epic "Walhalla Rising."
What audience did Danish director Nicolas Winding Refn think of when he was filming "Walhalla Rising"?
A captivated Viking one-eyed fighter (Mads Mikkelsen) frees himself from his torturers and kills his adversaries with almost supernatural power - it sounds ideal for young men's spectators looking for bloody actions, because our hero helps himself with cruel methods of killing and rips his enemies even the insides from his belly alive when he's in a bad mood.
On the other hand, as he is actually called, he does not say a word during the whole film, and even the other characters speak at most one or two sentences, or allow themselves to be pulled down by grievous dying screams.
Otherwise, it is silent and raging in the horrifily beautiful hilly landscape of Scotland, which offers a backdrop to the world of Vikings about thousands of years ago.
The film is more ideal for adult cinema enthusiasts interested in existentialism and very calm camera shots.
It's good when a movie has multiple target groups, it's just a shame when they're eliminating each other.
Director Winding Refn, after filming his wild and stylized trilogy about drug dealers "Pusher", is considered a Danish version of director Quentino Tarantino, but stoically resists bringing his works closer to either more humour to the mainstream or less brutality to the artistic audience.
Most fans of an art cinema are apparently discouraged by splashing brains and guts everywhere.
Supporters of action movies get bored no later than the moment One-Eyed goes for freely explainable reasons with a group of violent Christian missionaries ships to Jerusalem and hangs all eternity in the wind and fog, before the team apparently arrives in America and continues to remain silent.
Not surprisingly, "Walhalla Rising" did not reach cinemas in Germany and has now been released only on DVD.
It's a shame mainly because of beautiful shots, but also because behind the distinctive façade, sometimes collapsed with blood, hides an exciting cinema that deserved a bit of attention.
It's not about dialogue or the exact storyline in this film, it's all an atmosphere, a feverish dream that's become a film.
And it's not for the spectator of escape.
Refn doesn't give him even a moment of relaxation and slowly chokes him with his chilling shots and grim hypnotic soundtrack, but above all indescribable hatred that flows from the characters' hearts.
Only a small blond boy who helps One-Eyed retains something like innocence, everyone else is driven by anger.
Of all, the most one-eyed hero, whose motives will never be explained, who will become, over time for enemies and transitional allies, a warrior from hell, perhaps an envoy of the devil.
He portrays destruction, but perhaps only for the wicked.
"Walhalla Rising" is a mysterious film that can be hated and admired, for anger, excitement and admiration.
An experience.
Mysterious earthworks
The IAEA has new warnings about a secret nuclear program in Syria.
However, the country resists expression - and will not even allow controls.
Around midnight from 5 to 6 September 2007, at least four Israeli F-15 I warplanes attack a mysterious square building in Syria.
It stands on the banks of Euphrat; the nearest larger city is 30 kilometers away and is called Dair as-Saur.
Her name appears in the documents of the International Office for Nuclear Energy (IAEA) when it comes to a reactor supposedly built by Syria with the help of North Korea in this square hidden in a valley that turns away from power.
Based on a U.S.-U.S. foreign intelligence statement from the end of April 2008, the reactor, also known as the nearby village of el-Kibar, would be well suited to produce plutonium - from which nuclear weapons can be built.
The technology of the 1950s is not suitable for anything else.
The CIA presented a video with unfocused satellite images.
Using a computer animation, she explained where the components of the reactor might have been before they were destroyed is supposed to be a diminished copy of North Korea's Yongbyon nuclear center model.
In addition, there were still presented photos that showed the building up close or even from the inside.
The reactor was said to have been shortly before launch, but still without fuel cells.
Today, more than three years after the bombing, it is not known much more than at that time, even the riddles have increased over time.
The former U.S.-president, George W. Bush, recently disclosed in his memoirs that the CIA was very confident before the attack that Syria was building an el-Kibar reactor, but basically did not have the basis that there was an "active nuclear weapons program" in the country.
Some intelligence agencies in Europe are even more cautious and speak only of an "alleged reactor," although they consider the CIA's presentation "credible."
They lack clear evidence, as is the IAEA.
When asked how sure he has a reactor in front of him, a senior UN diplomat responded in the summer, the IAEA is said to have "enough material to ask questions."
The debris of allegedly "unused military building" was quickly cleared by the Syrians and also properly aligned the terrain.
Nevertheless, it managed to find the IAEA team in June 2008 under the leadership of former Chief Inspector Olli Heinonen in an el-Kibar trace of uranium that was made by humans.
In addition, the IAEA has received satellite photos of commercial retailers from just after the attack, which should be more apt than those presented by the CIA.
Unfortunately, these shots aren't on the market because they were exclusively bought by an unidentified customer - possibly Israel.
Therefore, the IAEA cannot confirm their authenticity.
Syria has always maintained that it has never had a secret nuclear program, nor has it built a reactor.
Uranium comes from Israeli weapons, Damascus said.
However, this was disproved by the IAEA after particle analysis.
Since then, Damascus has refused to discuss the matter - as well as satellite footage.
In addition, it banned inspectors from conducting a second inspection in el-Kibar and access to the wreckage of a bombed-out building.
But Israel, too, is silent.
"We will never comment on this," reigning Prime Minister Ehud Olmert said.
The investigation of this case has been going smoothly since June 2008 and is "growing into a serious problem due to the credibility of the office," as a senior Western diplomat at the IAEA headquarters in Vienna has acknowledged - after all, this is a serious violation of the Nuclear Weapons Treaty overseen by the IAEA.
The IAEA sees three other devices that are supposed to be "in a functional connection" with the alleged reactor to be the key to progress - and, in contrast, still exist.
At the same time, the inspectors have not yet indicated, or which devices they are involved in, nor what function they have, or why they are suspicious.
According to Süddeutsche Zeitung, the IAEA is internally referred to as Marj al-Sultan, Masyaf and Iskandariayah, again derived from nearby cities.
The Independent Institute for Science and International Security in Washington (Isis) identified these objects and found a complex of buildings on satellite images, which is probably one of the suspicious objects.
It is apparently located inside or in the immediate vicinity of a military base - for exactly that reason Syria rejects IAEA's approach to these three sites.
It was said in Vienna that the Syrians were afraid of so-called fishing - that the IAEA would be pre-sent by the U.S. to track down military bases.
U.S. nuclear detectives were alerted to the facility by the U.S. Secret Service.
The IAEA has documents as well as satellite images that material has been transported back and forth between these three sites and a square near Euphrat.
In Vienna, a senior Western diplomat said at least one furnished should be "in the context of fuel production."
But there is "no evidence of sensible activities," i.e. uranium enrichment and new processing; processes that are necessary for the production of nuclear weapons.
The type of reactor is powered by uranium, which is not enriched.
But where the uranius comes from, and where plutonium should be chipped off later, there are only likely estimates on these central questions.
It is also not known whether debris, which could hide important clues, is stored in these three locations.
It's as if hundreds of truck loads have disappeared from the surface of the earth, no one seems to know where the debris has gone.
Only a few kilometers from el-Kibar, distant and on the other side of Euphrat lying the al-Tibni salt mine, which had Syria inspected in 1998 as a suitable warehouse for radioactive waste, the IAEA considers unlikely.
Former IAEA inspector Robert Kelley linked him to the reactor in one analysis of satellite images for the renowned Jane's Intelligence Review.
However, images from space show that after the IAEA demanded an approach, Syria carried out earthworks at all suspicious locations and removed large containers.
It might have been a coincidence, but it didn't help clarify suspicion.
In addition, diplomatic circles say that the IAEA has spent many months creating a plan for inspectors that would allow Syria to preserve possible military secrets - only the effort shows how much the interest of inspectors is.
Damascus, meanwhile, also rejected the proposal.
If a year ago Western diplomats in Vienna reassured that the reactor was destroyed, and that's which the thing didn't burn, U.S. Ambassador Glyn Davies had been talking about so-called special inspections for several months, which is one of Vienna's toughest means to enforce controls.
IAEA chief Yukiya Amano, who would have to propose inspections, has already spoken of the "possibility I'm inclined towards in the future," so far hesitating.
On the one hand, there is a big risk: on the one hand, there is a need for a majority in the IAEA's Governing Council, and many of the 35 countries represented are certainly not interested in escalating.
On the other hand, as one diplomat points out: "What happens if they don't find anything in the end?"
Love in a time of war
There have been difficulties since Angelina Jolie began working on a still nameless director's debut in the summer.
She herself wrote the screenplay, it is the love story of a woman from Bosnia and a man from Serbia.
It's not known much more yet - but even that causes restlessness.
There is persistent talk that the woman who is a Muslim will fall in love with a Serbian man who raped her during the war in Yugoslavia.
Bosnian women's associations immediately protested, so the planned filming from Sarajevo to Hungary moved quickly.
In the meantime, the film has been filmed in Hungary and is in post-production, but there is still protesting in Bosnia.
Bakira Hasecic of the Women's War Victims Organization wrote through the UN-organization to aid refugees UNHCR Jolie and demanded a meeting with the actress, the Bosnian newspapers have now reported - Jolie is also an ambassador of the UN-organization to help refugees.
It's not exactly diplomatic - it offered women a meeting in Hungary weeks ago, but they refused.
The meeting must be held in Bosnia, Hasecic said.
In the campsites where it was raped, love stories didn't take place, and Jolie's attitude towards the victims reveals enough about the script.
Its organization, therefore, has advised the UN-organization to aid refugees to dismiss Jolie as an ambassador, the Guardian reports.
Which scene did Jolie write exactly?
The Bosnian Ministry of Culture, which was given to read its script, allowed filming after minor tug-of-war in Sarajevo - it's not entirely clear why Jolie didn't come anyway.
The young author emphasizes that in her film she does not pursue any political intentions, that with great effort she devoted herself to the conflict in Yugoslavia, that she wants to show the terrible consequences of the war and give space to all parties involved.
Due to the content, it remains vague.
There is another version of the film circulating on the Internet, which sounds quite different: lovers knew each other before the beginning of the war and will meet again when she, like a Bosnian Muslim woman, is introduced to a Serbian camp, where he serves as a guardian.
Which really sounds pretty different than "a rape victim falls in love with his torturer."
The fact that Jolie's film really begins before the war is true.
Jolie argues that there is pressure on her that is "not fair and based on misinformation."
It can now be rescued by hekers from Hollywood: By publishing her script illegally on the Internet, as they have done many times before.
Nothing but gone
It's good for the Irish again this evening.
Not a sign of grief.
The home team dominates power significantly over its guests from Sweden, Finland and the USA.
Fans roar with joy at the sound of loud music, stomping their feet.
Bailey-Pub in Cork, southern Ireland, is boiling as players throw tennis balls at a distance of three meters into beer cups - and hitting each other.
"Beer pong" is called bizarre theater and it is possible to win 500 euros as a winning premium, plus tickets to Las Vegas for the World Beer Pong Championships in January.
Cian Tanner is a calm type; this screaming beer game and restrained informatist doesn't match much together.
Still, the 26-year-old man is trying to have fun, unwinding from the burdensome question of what the future holds.
His plan is not a playing passion in Las Vegas, he's going for an interview in New Zealand in January.
“I have this weird feeling,” he says.
"It's so far away and I don't have a job there yet."
Still: His girlfriend is already waiting for him in Auckland - "immediately got his dream job as an Eventmanager, maybe it's a good sign," the young man says, smiling.
Due to bad prospects at home, two out of three Tanner siblings have already attempted their luck abroad.
His sister Yvonne, a social worker, is in Sydney, his brother Colin now has a place as an IT-advisor in London.
Boys are leaving: As with Tanners, it looks in many families on the island.
100,000 Irish will leave the country in the foreseeable future, experts at the ESRI Scientific Institute for Economics and Social Affairs predict in Dublin.
They are counting on 200,000 emigrants by 2015.
For such a small country with only 4.5 million inhabitants, this is a major loss.
If the predictions are confirmed, there will be a similar emigration wave as Ireland suffered in the 1980s.
After a decade of ascension that has brought great well-being to the Irish, displeasure is now expanding again.
The economic crisis is particularly disturbing the young, educated Irish, who have so far experienced only rise.
The loss of employment and social descent seemed absurd in these years of boom.
Everyone was needed, the money was lost.
In the meantime, three out of ten women and men under the age of thirty are unemployed.
There are many letters in the Irish Times of readers who complain: "In Ireland, everything goes downhill."
"There should be an uprising against politicians and bankers - but who can, he goes away, and our country loses an entire generation."
"Last, please turn off."
Law student Leanne Caulfield, president of the Irish Student Union, sees the future in black because the government is only deceiving and offering young people no prospects for the future.
“They’re reducing the minimum wage, but they don’t have a strategy to create new jobs,” she said.
She observes her classmates wandering from one unpaid practitioner to the next.
Her biggest concern is she'll have to evict herself in four years.
"This idea is sad and depressing."
Men like Stephen McLarnon try to see the topic positively.
McLarnon organizes "WorkingAbroadExpo", an exhibition that offers an overall service on the topic of work abroad.
“For beginners in the labour market, working abroad is an excellent opportunity to take the first steps on the career ladder,” says the manager.
On its website are photos of surfers in Australia and skiers on a snowboard in Canada.
The message is clear: Working abroad is not galleys, but freedom and joy.
Seán O'Donovan has known about what it's like to build something somewhere else for six months.
Because the 29-year-old engineer had no job in Ireland, he is now employed along with 15 Irish colleagues on construction in Germany.
They're building a fashion house in Gelsenkirchen right now.
In January, it continues into Hanover or Berlin for the next project.
O'Donovan, meanwhile, rarely gets home because he works in extra shifts so he can repay a house-to-house loan at home.
“Sometimes the situation is really a little crazy,” he says when, for the first time in a long time, he crosses the threshold of his own house.
"I'm making money in Germany for a house in Ireland that I'm basically not in at all."
He laughs embarrassedly and walks through the colded rooms.
A small house stands in a good neighborhood with well-keen gardens, large expensive cars are parked in the entrances.
O'Donovan bought the house along with his sister four years ago for 380,000 euros - 100-percent funded by a loan.
"It was a crazy time, simple money was without many questions," she says.
He's afraid it wouldn't turn out well with a loan?
O'Donovan thinks for a moment, finishes the hot tea and says, "I don't have children I have to feed and I'm young; even if everything goes wrong, I can start over."
The civil engineer doesn't like the laments he hears so often now.
“No one has to starve – our situation isn’t quite as dramatic as it used to be on this island,” he says.
"Work abroad for a while, that's not the worst thing that can happen - maybe we'll just feel like the Irish."
He laughes and then talks about his father, who went to England during the last economic crisis.
"After seven years he was back again and life went on in Ireland, quite simply."
Cian Tanner likes this idea.
Leaving an open future and simply waiting to see how life develops elsewhere.
He's also been working in London for several years and why shouldn't it be good for him and his girlfriend in New Zealand?
But there's one other thing he's really concerned about in those days: celebrating his 27th birthday in Dublin on Friday.
She's afraid she might turn into a farewell party.
"Just no emotions," he says, drinking beer properly.
"Goodbye, that sounds disturbing."
"Let's call it a celebration of leaving, quite simply, with a few friends, a few drinks, a few games."
Ordinary Practice of Democracy
The promotion of democracy in Latin America is growing, as is the promotion of private entrepreneurship.
Criminal activity raises more concerns than unemployment.
And many regions consider Brazil to be more influential than the United States.
These are some of the results of the current Latinobarómetro survey, which took place in 18 countries and was published exclusively in The Economist magazine.
Since the survey has been carried out regularly since 1995, it can thus monitor changes in attitudes across the whole area.
This year's survey, which took place in September and early October, is dominated by two related matters.
The first is that Latin America is in a rather cheerful mood.
The second is to increase the stability of attitudes towards democracy and its key institutions.
Promoting democracy has increased significantly in several countries on the outskirts of the Pacific in South America (see Table 1).
For example, in Peru, where economic growth has averaged 6% a year since 2002, support for democracy has risen from a minimum of 40% in 2005 to 61% this year.
It has also risen in Mexico, where economics was recovering after a deep decline in production last year.
But only about half of respondents in Mexico and Brazil, the two most populous areas, are democrats convinced, which drags down the regional average (see chart 2).
About 44% of respondents across the region expressed satisfaction with how democracy works in their country in practice, this is the same number as last year, but compared to 2001, when it came to 25%, this is a big increase (graph 3).
Roughly 34% said they trust the council in their country and 45% trust the government, again these are the same numbers as last year, but this is a big difference compared to 2003 (17% and 24%).
Ten years ago, an economic crisis undermined democracy in Latin America.
Support arose during the global financial crisis, probably because many Latin American countries suffered only a short recession and enjoyed a strong recovery.
In addition, a stronger social security network has helped protect poor people.
(The United Nations Economic Commission for Latin America announced this week that the ratio of Latinos living in poverty increased only marginally in 2009 and fell to 32% again this year.)
However, the mood changes to a wide range from country to country (see chart 4).
The strong economic performance of Brazil and the popularity of its outgoing President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva are reflected in the widely accepted sense of progress.
This situation is rarer in Mexico and Central America, where the recession has been more serious and the state of violent crime has deteriorated.
For only the second time since surveys began, more respondents across Latin America see crime rather than unemployment as the most important problem (see Graph 5).
Roughly 31% say that either themselves or their close relatives were victims of crime last year, but that's a decrease compared to 38% from last year and is also the lowest number since 1995.
More massive economic performance is reflected in increasing support for private entrepreneurship and the market economy: around 71% of respondents across the area say that private entrepreneurship is necessary for development, this percentage increased from 56% from 2007 (see Chart 6).
About 58% agree that the market economy is the only system that will allow their country's development, this percentage grew from 47% from last year.
Interestingly, 62% of respondents in Venezuela have the same opinion (and 80% support private business there), although their left-wing president Hugo Chávez expropriates many private firms.
Brazil is a country that has a leading position in the area, as 19% of respondents mentioned (compared to 18% last year), followed by the United States (9%, compared to last year unchanged) and Venezuela (9%, versus 11% last year)
However, the United States is considered the most influential country by respondents in Mexico and in much of Central America, with Venezuela enjoying success in Ecuador, the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua.
Criticised elections
No one expected Haiti's general elections to be perfect on 28 November.
But they were much worse than outsiders trying to help the affected country to be afraid.
The election meant filling urns and recurring votes, ballot ballots smashed by canals, broken polling stations and intimidation.
Polling stations open after hours of delay.
Many Haitians, including many moved because of the January quarrel, roamed around trying to figure out where they could vote.
Some of them eventually gave up.
More than ten presidential candidates, including several presumed forerunners, shouted that it was a hoax and called after the election was cancelled.
Protests, some of them of a violent nature, have spread across the country.
Yet Edmond Mulet, a high-ranking U.N
Observers from the Organization of American States and the Caribbean Community cautiously said that "they do not believe that these inconsistencies, no matter how serious, did not necessarily cancel the process."
In other words, the show continues.
The electoral body, whose opposition votes are indebted to René Prévala, the unpopular outgoing president and his candidate Jude Célestin, is due to announce preliminary results on December 7.
An anticipated decisive duel is scheduled for January.
All electoral hopes are not lost.
Two leading opposition candidates, Michel Martelly, rapper and Mirlande Manigat, the former first lady, withdrew from their original call for the vote to be declared valid.
But the problem may arise if Mr Célestin, who has little or negligible support among voters, will be identified by the electoral commission for achieving a decisive fight.
Due to the rampant cholera epidemic and more than 1 million people who still live in tents after the earthquake, there were fears that turnout would be low.
In the end, many Haitians wanted to vote, but because of disorganization, they were prevented from doing so.
Roughly 250 thousand identity cards, which are necessary for voting, remained undivided shortly before the elections.
There's a lot at stake.
Donors see the election of a legitimate and popular government as a crucial step in Haiti's reconstruction.
And so it's clear that the Haitians will do it themselves.
Tips to the center
In the weeks when she was suddenly widowed, Cristina Fernández, Argentina's president, benefited from public sympathy, when her popularity rose from 20 to more than 50%, the highest level since she was elected in 2007.
Yet the conversion of this success into a successful campaign for a second term in next year's elections may depend on whether grieving Ms Fernández gives up the uncompromisingness of her late husband, predecessor and political partner Néstor Kirchner.
And on these emblems, everything will be established.
Mr Kirchner, who continued economic policy even after his wife had successfully become president, had the habit of drawing up budgets that undervalued tax revenues, economic growth and inflation.
This allowed the first couple to spend additional income at will.
Ms Fernández's government lost its majority in Congress last year.
But the opposition split and was further destabilized at the death of Mr Kirchner for a heart attack on October 27.
Some members of the ruling political movement Peronist have joined Ms Fernández.
But after chaotic sessions (in which there were slapping and accusing the government of corruption), Congress failed to approve the 2011 budget.
"I'm not going to argue," the president said.
The 2010 budget will now also apply for next year, while the president will have more space to spend at will.
The economy is overheating: growth of up to 9% is likely this year.
The official inflation rate now stands at 11.1%, but many private estimates and trade unionists speak of a number that is approaching 30%.
Mr Kirchner, who took office in 2003 when Argentina was plagued by an economic collapse, prioritized growth and poverty reduction, rather than controlling inflation.
He declined to negotiate with the IMF, burdening the private sector and clearing the INDEC, a statistical agency that stepped in to monitor official inflation statistics.
Can President Fernández now moderate this policy?
She called for negotiations between private enterprises and union leaders.
And its officials say they want to limit wage growth to 18% next year.
The economy was boosted by an increase in world prices for exports of Argentine agricultural products.
But inflation was disrupted by growth based on a major devaluation in 2002.
That may be why President Fernández is letting her know she wants to restore Argentina's financial ties to the rest of the world.
Earlier this year, the government formed an agreement with bondholders who had previously rejected a debt restructuring that Argentina did not pay in 2001.
President Fernández is now facing an agreement with creditor governments, which are grouped together at the Paris club, to which she owes roughly $6.7 billion.
Kirchners has always insisted that this fact should not be within sight of the IMF, as is usual.
But last month, Amado Boudou, the economy minister announced that the fund would provide "technical assistance" to propose a new national consumer-pricing index that will commemorate an introduction to INDEC cleaning.
It is not yet clear whether President Fernández will want to remain in politics after the end of her term when her husband is no longer by her side.
If he wants to stay, he needs the support of the middle class, which is tired of kirchnerism.
At the time of Argentina's return from their summer holiday, which they have in March, a wave of sympathy will decline.
If she wants to win the next election, she'll have to decide whether it won't be necessary to quietly renounce the many views her husband held in politics.
Half encouragement for China
Taiwanese voters are closer to a closer connection with the mainland.
What does Taiwan think about its big brother across the sea?
More than 7.5 million voters, 70% of those eligible, elected mayors and city councils in five city districts on November 27, which can be seen as a broader assessment by President Ma Ying-jeou.
President Ma himself is a representative of the feelings that predominate the mainland and have a strong commitment in business relations, including an agreement on partially free trade, in the largest settlement of relations with China since 1949.
President Ma passed the test, but only narrowly.
Although his Kuomintang (KMT) party ended up winning three of the mayor's five offices, it failed to win by a majority vote.
In addition, the Democratic Progressive Party (DPP), which is inclined t on Taiwan's greater independence, won nearly 50% of all votes against 45% of the KMT party.
If it were a presidential election, President Ma would be in trouble.
Since he was elected two years ago, his party's vote has declined by around 8%.
For the first time, the DPP party, together with KMT, was on city councils.
The election took place like most of the Taiwanese elections, which include noisy election trucks and mass rallies with flag-shaded girls.
Part of it was an obligatory mysterious assassination attempt.
Sean Lien, the son of Lien Chana, the honorary chairman of the KMT, was agitated for KMT candidates on the eve of the election.
A man from the crowd shot the young Mr. Lien cleanly over his face, wounded him and killed the spectator.
Bizarrely, it was not determined whether the gunman intended to injure Mr. Lien or settle a dispute with a candidate.
In any case, Tsai Ing-wen, the head of the DPP, argues that the shooting affected the election, suspicion falls on the DPP and leads voters towards KMT.
Perhaps the only major winner of the campaign was China.
It was remarkable how the question of the mainland itself remained in the background.
Instead, candidates struggled over plans for state housing construction and job creation.
Taiwan usually enjoys its de facto independence and hates China's military threats.
The KMT therefore tends to avoid ties to the government in Beijing.
But this time, the DPP has also refrained from arousing anti-Chinese attitudes.
It's a reaction to reality and the realization that China's trade is crucial to Taiwan's economic survival.
President Ma, on behalf of his party, affirmed that China's policy is acceptable to a majority of Taiwan.
It can be expected from the government in Beijing to continue with commercial bribes, together with its strategy to lure Taiwan back into the herd.
China did not waste time sending a negotiator to Taipei to arrange a summit on investment protection and medical cooperation.
They'd also like to see political integration, too, but in order.
It will be better to wait for this after the 2012 presidential election.
In any case, the DPP has discovered itself.
Two years ago she was at the wrong level: her former leader, Chen Shui-bian, was sent to prison on corruption charges.
The president in 2000-08 was a rioter and advocate of clear independence.
But under the leadership of Ms. Tsai, a new generation has evolved that is moderate and the DPP is working hard to attract experts and young people.
Ms. Tsai was proclaimed as the DPP's nominee for president, such as Su Tseng-changer, who held office under Mr. Chen.
Their party is now doing everything it can to show that they can also work with Beijing.
Ms Tsai has published plans for research institutes that serve as a point of contact between the DPP and China, provided that their negotiations can take place without previous conditions.
Ms. Tsai tells the Taiwanese public that her party represents a "credible alternative" to Mr Ma's hasty approach to China.
Mr. Chen seems angry with this shift.
In a statement issued from his cell, he expressed the view that DPP candidates would do better if they insisted that Taiwan is a separate country.
He even ordered Mr. Su, a pragmatist, not to run for president.
But even without Mr Chen's intervention, the DPP will fight for a choice between potential candidates.
KMT is authorized to search for one's own people.
Mr Ma's assessments were bad, despite the rapid pace of economic growth; the KMT candidates didn't want to go into the campaign with him.
His unpopularity appears to be based in particular on unemployment and the deepening divide between the rich and the poor.
A KMT spokeswoman says he will re-organise his cabinet, as well as local branches.
Strongly promoted proposals in support of Taiwan for establishing friendly relations are limited.
At the same time, the majority of Taiwanese citizens consider economic integration with China inevitable.
According to Antonio Chiang, a prominent publicist, "a point from which there is no return has already passed."
Grammy Award Nominations: Jazz Singer Esperanza Spalding is a Surprise in the Best New Artist category
Speaking at the request of the president and people must necessarily be noticed.
Esperanza Spalding, a 26-year-old jazz bassis and singer chosen by President Obama to perform at the 2009 Nobel Peace Prize, is described as one of the surprise Grammy Awards this year, with a nomination for Best New Artist.
Since Spalding has been a celebrated figure on the jazz scene since 2008, when her album "Esperanza" was released, the nomination came a bit late, but it's in line with Grammy rules, when an artist can't release more than three albums before "the public identity of a given artist is established."
Her ambitious 2010 "Chamber Music Society" has certainly taken a step forward and, thanks to her carefree vocal style and nimble hands with elements of pop, funk and Latin influences, we can talk about Spalding as a success recorded by Norah Jones in 2003, the closest equivalent for Grammy voters looking for a similar jazz work in this category in recent years.
Elsewhere Grammy judges rightly applaud Vijay Iyer Trio "Historicity" in the instrumental album category, an exciting, refreshing ride of the album that was a hot favourite last year.
Iyer will compete against the album by experienced saxophonist James Moody "Moody 4B" and keyboardist John Beasley "Positootly."
Trombone Shorty, a hoarse favorite from New Orleans and a frequent guest on HBO "Treme," has been honored in the contemporary jazz category, which is otherwise inclined to Stanley Clark, John McLaughlin and Jeff Lorber's albums.
Darcy James Argue's Secret Society, with steampunk's knowledge, has gained several well-deserved recognitions in the category of big ensembles, with chamber jazz Childs Ensemble in the competition and the still courageous Dave Holland, whose live album "Pathway" created a beautiful example of how a bassist can have a free hand as a bander.
Grammy Awards nominations: Michael Daugherty, Steve Mackey lead in the classical music category
Nominations for classical music have a wide range of musicians and composers from all over the world at the 53rd annual Grammy Awards.
Michael Daugherty and his "Deus Ex Machina" for piano and orchestra, with five points and Steve Mackey with his album "Dreamhouse", an eclectic orchestral work, with three points.
Among the nominees tied to Southern California, Estonian composer Arvo Pärt and his Symphony #4 "Los Angeles," a work that was commissioned via the Los Angeles Philharmonic and debuted at Walt Disney Concert Hall in 2009.
Composer based in L.A. Avner Dorman and his Mandolin Concerto also have a chance for the award.
Nominations in classical music are divided into 13 categories, which include orchestral, opera, choral and solo recordings.
The classical music producer of the year has five nominees: Blanton Alspaugh, David Frost, Tim Handley, Marina and Victor Ledin and James Mallinson.
Bruckner and his Symphonies Nos. 3 and 4 from the Royal Concertgebouw Orchestra; Daugherty and his "Deus Ex Machina" and "Metropolis Symphony" by the Nashville Symphony Orchestra; Steven Mackey and his "Dreamhouse" from the Boston Modern Orchestra Project and Synergy Vocals; "Sacrificium - the Art of the Castrati"
As for the opera category, the nominees are Berg and "Lulu" by the Orchestra of the Royal Opera House; Adolf Hasse and "Marc Antonio e Cleopatra" by Ars Lyrica Houston; Kaija Saariaho and "L'Amour de Loin" by Deutsches Symphonie-Orchester Berlin; Rodion Shchedrin and "The Enchanted Wanderer" by Orchestra and Chorus of the Mariinsky Theatre
Among the composers nominated for Grammy for contemporary songs are Pärt for Symphony No. 4, Daugherty for "Deus Ex Machina," Hans Werner Henze for "Appassionatamente Plus," Magnus Lindberg for "Graffiti" and Shchedrin for "The Enchanted Wanderer."
Pärt's Symphony No. 4 premiered at Disney Hall in January 2009 for a performance recorded by L.A. Philharmonic under the baton of Esa-Pekka Salonena.
The approximately 30-minute episode is called "Los Angeles" and is dedicated to former oil tycoon Mikhail Khodorkovsky, who is imprisoned in Russia.
Dorman and "Mandolin Concerto" were nominated for instrumental performances by soloists with the orchestra.
The composer from L.A. stated that he wrote a 15-minute work specifically for mandolin performer Avi Avital, who performed at a concert with the Metropolis Ensemble based in New York City.
"I've writted this with the help of tones from the Middle East and many North African rhythms," Dorman said in a telephone interview.
The conductor Pierre Boulez, 85, received a nomination in the category for orchestral performance, for leading the Chicago Symphony in the recording of Stravinsky's works.
Instrumental soloists who have received nominations include pianists Nelson Freire and Marc-André Hamelin; organist Paul Jacobs; violinist Julia Fischer; and harpist Sarah Schuster Ericsson.
Only women were among the nominees vocal soloists this year: Anne Sofie Von Otter, Cecilia Bartoli, Lucia Duchon, Vivica Genaux and Measha Brueggergosman.
In the classic transition category, soprano Jessye Norman won the nomination for her recording "Roots - My Life, My Song."
The planned mosque in Temecula receives the approval of the planning commission
The planned mosque in Temecula received the unanimous support of the local planning commission after more than five hours of often passionate public testimony.
Critics have attacked the project for promoting terrorism and it will lead to the suspension of trade.
However, jurors decided on Wednesday night that the new religious center complies with local laws and will be aesthetically impressive.
"Many good people have come to support this project - Christians, Jews, who you remember," Hadi Nael, chairman of the Islamic Center in Temecula Valley, said Thursday morning.
"I'm very proud of it as an American citizen."
"It means that justice will always prevail."
"Opposition is minimal" he added.
"Those who are against only shout loudly."
"We've got really good people in our valley."
Negotiations began at six o'clock in the evening and lasted almost until midnight, when most of the time it took discussions with people who were for or against the center. These voices were evenly distributed, Press-Enterprise says.
The discussion continued on the newspaper's website.
"There's no America anymore," one comment appeared.
"We're the Planet of the Apes with the Statue of Liberty set in the sand."
"America has fallen."
The planned Islamic Centers also sparked protests in other parts of the country, including near Ground Zero, the site of the tragedy that took place on September 11, 2001, when terrorists attacked New York.
In Temecula Valley, Imam Mahmoud Harmoush condemned the violence.
Its religious community of approximately 135 families had beent in Riverside County for years without an incident, but wished for a larger and more suitable space than the storage space it rented along with the filtration system manufacturer.
The new mosque with dimensions of 24 943 square feet in the Mediterranean style of architecture will also have two minarets and will grow to a height of 43 feet.
It should occupy a place of 4.32 acres on Nicolas Road, approximately three kilometers east of Chaparral High School.
Opponents can still appeal to the city council about the project.
Microbes with resistance to arsenic increase the possibility of new types of life on Earth or elsewhere
The discovery of a strange bacterium that can use arsenic as one of its nutrients expands the space to search for new forms of life on Earth and possibly beyond.
Researchers have discovered an unusual bacterium here on Earth, claiming it's an indication that life has options that stand outside the main elements that have so far been considered and basic.
"This organism has a dual ability."
"It can grow either thanks to phosphorus or arsenic."
"This fact is very strange, but it's not proof that this is a form of truly 'extraterrestrial' life," Paul C. W. Davies of Arizona State University, co-author of the report, appeared in an online issue of the journal Science on Thursday.
For a long time it was considered necessary for the life of six main elements - carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulfur.
However, scientists have found that bacteria, discovered in Mono Lake, California, are able to continue growing after phosphorus is replaced by arsenic.
"It's going to amaze you what's still possible," said Ariel D. Anbar of Arizona State University, co-author of the report.
This finding is significant for the search for life beyond Earth because researchers must be able to recognize life and know what life can look like, Anbar said.
The study focuses on microbes found on Earth.
However, a report on the news published to discuss it, which did not reveal the details of the find, prompted widespread speculation on the Internet that the report reveals the discovery of extraterrestrial life.
That's not the case.
The discovery "shows that other planetary organisms may be able to take advantage of other elements that drive biochemistry, and that the set of elements that we consider 'standard' may not be so absolutely necessary for life," explained Charles Cockell, a professor at the Planetary and Space Sciences Research Institute, Open University, in Milton Keynes, UK.
Cockell wasn't part of the research team.
"This work is unusual because it demonstrates the replacement of one element by another in basic biochemistry and biochemical structure," Cockell added.
It wasn't an accidental find.
Felisa Wolfe-Simon of NASA's Astrobiological Institute, which led the study, focused on Mono Lake because it exhibits high levels of arsenic.
Arsenic and phosphorus are chemically very similar elements, so she considered that a microbe that is exposed to both elements can be able to replace each other.
“Arsenic is poisonous mainly because its chemical behavior is very similar to phosphorus.”
"As a result, organisms are very difficult to separate these elements from each other."
"However, arsenic is so different that just like phosphorus doesn't work. When it gets into the organism, it starts working in biochemical processes," Anbar explained.
Scientists collect bacteria known as GFAJ-1 and expose them to an increasing concentration of arsenic that bacteria are able to adapt and grow.
Microbes grow better with phosphorus, but due to the fact that they can also survive with arsenic replacement, the possibility is increased that the form of life that uses arsenic can occur naturally, either elsewhere on Earth or on another planet or moon, where arsenic is more common.
Jamie S. Foster, assistant professor of microbiology at the University of Florida, declared that the idea of arsenic that could be replaced by phosphorus is not new, but there has never been an example on which this fact can be accomplished.
Arsenic was more common in earlier times on Earth, she said, so scientists speculated that it could have been used by earlier life forms.
"This suggests that there may have been other ways to create life, not just the way it was created in the early Earth period," Foster, who was not part of Wolfe-Simona's research team, said in a statement.
The research was supported by NASA, the Department of Energy and the National Institute of Health
Remaining Stolen Dogs Found in Compton
The remaining two of the four exhibition dogs stolen from the Bellflower Motel were found, the Los Angeles area sheriff reported Thursday.
Four show dogs - two akites and a welsh pembroke corgis - disappeared on Tuesday, when someone stole the Chevrolet Express van 2006, in which they spent the night at the Bellflower 6 Motel, authorities said.
Two dogs were discovered Wednesday afternoon, and the other two dogs then late Wednesday evening, said Steve Whitmore, a spokesman for the Los Angeles Police Department.
"The good news is that the dogs are fine," Whitmore said.
"They're in good condition."
"They were handed over to their rightful owners."
Whitmore declined to say exactly where the dogs were found, in what location, nor did he share detailed information relating to finding them.
“The investigation is still ongoing.”
"We don't want to talk about it," Whitmore said.
“We follow the rules.”
Dog owners, Kristina Rickard and David Peek, have not been reached to comment on the case.
They were very happy when their two akits were found at a shelter in Downey on Wednesday afternoon.
The sheriff's representatives found a van and two dogs separately in Compton.
Akity Trace, a three-year-old dog Trace and a 10-month-old bitch Didi, were uninjured, authorities said.
But Bunny and Peter, the Welsh Corgi Pembroke Rickard and Peek breeds, who were planning to take part in regional competitions on behalf of their Japanese owners this week, remained unfinished until their discovery late Wednesday.
Peek and Rickard, who live in Redding, are in the city this weekend to take part in the AKC/Eukanuba National Championship in Long Beach, where they planned to show Trace.
Other dogs were supposed to compete in other competitions, planned in the days before the AKC/Eukanuba show exhibition, Peek told The Times on Wednesday.
Southern California is just one stop on a couple's nearly-month-long trip to dog shows.
On Monday night, Peek and Rickard registered for a motel.
They often sleep in motels and park their car outside the room window.
They also married three dogs - a boxer's puppy, Parson Russell terrier and a Brittany hound - who remained with them in a motel room.
Akity and Pembroke Welsh corgis were left in a car where it was supposed to be cooler.
They opened the windows a little so that the dogs had enough fresh air in the car.
Peek and Rickard reported car theft at 6 a.m. on Tuesday.
Peek said he would offer a reward to anyone who would lead them to the missing dogs. Dogs have microchips implanted to identify them.
It wasn't clear on Thursday whether prizes would be awarded.
The lion is once again ready to roar now that MGM's restructuring gains final approval from the court
After 18 months of upheaval and uncertainty, MGM was finally given the green light so it could start again.
New York's federal insolvency judge on Thursday officially blessed "pre-wrapped" creditors' plan to settle some £5bn in debt, clearing the way for an 86-year-old entertainment company that focuses on the production and production of movies and TV shows.
By Thursday's decree that the plan meets the requirements of the U.S
The number of employees will fall to about 320 from the original more than 400.
MGM does not suddenly emerge from Chapter 11 until JPMorgan Chase agrees on planned financial resources allowing a loan of US$500 million to finance new operations and MGM production.
It is now a merger with an investment bank that itself needs 75 million dollars and funding is expected around mid-December.
The restructuring process began in May 2009, when investment bank Moelis & Co. was hired to advise management on how to solve its finances, as management struggled to afford interest payments of US$300 million a year.
On that August, Chairman Harry Sloan was replaced by Stephen Cooper, former CEO of Enron and turnover expert, known for his work for Krispy Kreme Doughnuts.
Attempts by Moelis and Cooper to resolve MGM's financial difficulties and 140 creditors' studies took much longer than expected.
After several months of discussions, the parties agreed to put the company up for sale at the end of 2009.
Although the auction attracted several bidders, none of them were willing to pay 2 billion U.S. dollars plus the finances demanded by creditors.
In the spring of this year, everything was back on the drawing board and the MGM team began working on a restructuring plan. After months of discussion and internal disagreements, the plan was eventually approved by creditors at the end of October.
When MGM officially rises from bankruptcy in the coming weeks, Moelise and Cooper will receive multi-million dollar bonuses for their work.
Moelis & Co., which has already received US$1.2 million, will receive $9.5 million payment.
Cooper's firms, which collect 4 million dollars for their services that govern the company's running, will receive a reward for success, the so-called "success fee".
Barber and Birnbaum are planning to resume production at MGM, which released only one film this year and put together a proposal for seven or eight films coming out in early 2012.
They are also planning to participate in the funding of two films, the title of which will be the "Hobbit," together with Warner Bros.
The studio needs to arrange a special loan of between 265 million and 275 million dollars to finance its share of these two films, directed by Peter Jackson.
Investor Carl Icahn, who owns approximately 18% of MGM's debts, was looking founded the possibility of merging the studio with Lions Gate Entertainment, in which he is the largest shareholder.
However, recent negotiations between the two companies did not result in an agreement.
Technological toys to keep your gift budget in balance
The leading candidate of this year is the most amazing toy iPad, followed by the video game system. In both cases, however, it is a significant hit to the gift budget.
Consider that for the price of one iPad you can buy five BMX rounds for 99 dollars, and one Kinect game costs just like 10 bead skewers or approximately 48 Play-Doh cups.
But there's a way to keep your budget for gifts in balance.
I've recently visited three national toy retailers - Wal-Mart, Toys "R" Us and BJ's Wholesale Club - and watched what electronic toys I can buy for less than 80 dollars.
It was immediately clear that Moore's Law was working; this year's tech toys have far more energy, at the same prices, if not slightly lower, compared to past years.
While relatively fewer new toys have emerged with associated sites, the two best-known toys of this type, Webkinz and Club Penguin, offers more.
Increasing the number of toys with button batteries, as well as toys with rechargeable batteries.
Here are some recommendations.
(All listed prices are from Toys "R" Us, except in cases that are marked otherwise.)
Sing-a-ma-jigs (Mattel, $13, from the age of 3) are stuffed animals that sing when you press their tummy.
At one press, the animal gives a tone, and the the other press begins to form a
If you buy more than one, they will sing together and harmoniously.
Caution: Once you start squeezing, you can’t stop.
The best choice in combining singing and dancing is Dance Star Mickey (Fisher-Price, $60, from 2 years)
Last year I borrowed a 14-inch animatric doll at Elmo Live technology, which can play Simon Says and is also able to walk for a month walk thanks to its innovative tilting leg with wheel blocking.
Kids are testing a Mickey dance instructor's toy, though their parents are complaining about a lack of volume control.
If you're a child of the 80's, you might remember Alphia, one of the first computer educational toys.
He's back, (Hasbro/Playskool, $40, for children from 3 years of age), with a backpack full of quiz cards that are designed to turn your car ride into fun with working with a self-preparating, funny workbook.
If you're squinting for a smartphone for your child, here are three independent alternatives.
VTech's MobiGo ($60, for children from 3 to 8 years of age) is the cheapest option with a simple use, with a qwerty keyboard, although games don't have as efficient teaching as Leapster Explorer ($70, for children from 3 to 12 years old), the first significant change to the Leapster series from Leapfrog, with a newer and brighter display.
No version is compatible with older units.
iXL from Fisher-Price ($90, for children from 3 to 7 years of age) uses the iTunes-like system to introduce games and e-books, and has an SD card slot that allows you to view family photos.
And finally, Scrabble Flash ($30, Hasbro, for children from 8 years old) is a set of five cubes that aren't larger than a match box, each has an LED display.
The goal is to create words by placing dices in a row before time runs out; it's great for competiting in improvised word formation.
More and more toys have at least some kind of online connection.
To the greatest extent, two Webkinz and Club Penguin toys were represented in toy shops.
If you want to buy your child an online toy from Club Penguin, you can choose, for example, Disney Club Penguin 2" Mix 'N Match Figure Pack, $5, for children from 6 to 12 years of age), which includes a coin with a hidden code.
When you activate the code online, your child will receive enough credits in the Club Penguin store for any two items.
This could include, for example, roller skates or a Puffle animal.
Slightly bigger than your toothbrush brush is Hexbug Nano (10$, for children from 5 years of age), the newest of a range of microrobots on a button battery.
The beetle moves quickly to 12 vibrating silicone legs in accidental direction.
It's fun when you make a maze out of cubes and see if it manages to find a way out.
It's also very resilient.
Ours was able to fall with the kitchen table and even spend some time in the mouth of our dog.
Introducing finer police controls in China
As a city military instructor, Tang Shenbin walked around the square, strictly checking his orders and handing out orders half-voiced with military authority.
He wanted women's members - China's sturdy enforcers of urban order, feared and despised for their harsh interventions and fondness for violence - to convey a certain impression on a cluster of bystanders.
"Stand up straight!"
"Hurry!"
Show them, he whispers softly, "what pretty girls look like!"
Four very young girls in white gloves and the same olive coats and pants stood to attention.
Four pairs of black boots are sorted like a ruler.
Four headgear fits perfectly on the hairs, which are perfectly tied with blue and white ribbons.
"Personally, I think they look average," Mr. Tang said disdainfully.
"Models are nice."
More than one government tried to revive the image of urban guards in China.
One city ordered the new conscripts to have a higher education.
Guangdong province has turned grey-green uniforms into supposedly more appealing blue.
Wuhan, in central China, was replaced by focused glances with strong armaments: in a report in 2009, it was stated in one report that 50 officers encircled an adamant wheelchair with snacks and stared at the owner until after half an hour the door-to-door vendor packed up and drove off.
Xindu, an urban county with 680,000 inhabitants in Chengdu, chose surgery as the main painting.
Since 2003, the county has supplemented its municipal police with 13 women who were selected specifically according to their appearance, symmetricity and young age.
The aim is to show, in addition to the coarse side of the police, also a finer, femin
Sadly, Scarlett Johansson herself would be in trouble to get into secret Chinese city groups.
Critics would argue for good reason.
Unlike the police, these police officers are only allowed to enforce a city ordinance, impose fines and other administrative penalties.
But Chinese medics routinely capture a different reality.
In January 2008, police inspectors from Hubei province beaten an uninvolved spectator to death after using his cellphone to shoot, as they were doing during the crackdown on a protest against the landfield.
Last year there was training of police officers in Beijing, the manual for this training was stolen and placed on the Internet. Among other things, there was a description of how to effectively beat the perpetrators without signs of blood.
This year, a door-to-door melon dealer in Shanghai got into a brawl with five police officers and suffered injuries to his left-handed brain.
One video game full of violence, which can be downloaded on the Internet, depicts Chinese police officers attacking street vendors.
"Chengguan has spooked the government," China Daily, the national edition, lamented last year after further discussion on tactics.
There was "really thoroughly cleansing" on the paper.
Skeptics say such an approach is not enough.
After the county advertised an interest in eight new women's police officers in October, The Beijing Evening News editorial looked at the question of whether women have actual responsibilities or are merely a distraction.
The answer seems to be a bit out of both.
The district demanded female contenders between the ages of 18 and 22 with a good figure and "five facial features in the right proportion."
Adept women should have a height higher than the diameter - that is, higher than 1.6 meters.
Retiremence at the age of 26 is mandatory.
Officials say the work is mentally too demanding for women over the age of 25.
"Their image is an important thing," one unnamed police officer told Rednet.com, like a government website.
"Candidates must first demonstrate the external qualities, which then determine others, including height, weight, facial features, etc."
Then comes the nature and "internal qualities".
Women are like floral vases, he said, adding, "In addition to being vases, they will also have other responsibilities."
Zheng Lihua, the deputy director of the county's city leadership, doesn't quite agree with that description.
But he noted that height requirements are also common in China for other jobs, for both sexes.
As well as the requirement for symmetrical facial features.
Which means that the question of a good appearance is the subject of discussion among the Chinese.
Disabled or mutilation will certainly not apply.
"He can't allow a lame or hunched man to serve with us," Mr. Zheng said.
"His image wouldn't be good."
Liu Yi, who keeps watch in Baoguang Square near the convent, is 22 years old, faces like an apple and a gently shaped mouth.
She doesn't think the emphasis on her appearance could have a sexist character, she said.
"Do you think I'm sexy in this uniform?" she asked with an ironic look.
Her collaborator with dimples, 21-year-old Xu Yang says "Our job is to represent the image of the city."
They have no objection to their limited performance, they said, because their career ambitions are greater than merely driving traders into the aisles where they can carry out their activities.
Every morning, the group faces more than ten marketers running around on foot or on wheels, trying to sell as many dumplings and tofu bowls as possible before being driven out.
"Mr. Wang, you have to leave."
"We've told you many times!" said Ms. Xu as the marketer began to run, and for a time he left his bike with a noodle cart.
Officers describe their responsibilities as more monotonous than strenuous.
"Every day it's basically about the same thing," said Huang Jing, 20, who is studying marketing in her free time.
"Great routine."
One of the reasons is that female cops don't have powers like their male counterparts so that sellers can confiscate the goods.
They can only threaten to report the fact to their male leaders.
The tendency is then to protect them from sudden public displays of hostility against the official apparatus, which is common throughout China.
This year, hundreds of citizens in Kunming, Yunnan province's capital, revolted after false rumours spread that police officers had been killed by vendors.
More than ten police officers or police officers were injured at night; 14 government vehicles were overturned or set on fire.
So far, Xindu has escaped such violence.
But peace can hardly be guaranteed.
Only two blocks from Baoguang's quiet square, where they had a patrol of a policewoman that morning, more than 50 people gathered on the street corner.
Officers seized a motorcycle that was being repaired on the pavement instead of inside the store, as the regulations require.
The owner of the bike could cry as he wanted.
A 15-minute distance in front of the cops, looking angry, with elbows against the vehicles and quickly away with the motorcycle and its owner.
Li Xuedong, 40, a coordinator attached to the men's group, stayed behind, his white badge turned to hide his name.
Like women's cops, coordinators are men over the age of 40, who already have only an auxiliary role.
In contrast, they are not educated in the maintenance and smoothing image.
"Sometimes we fight verbally."
"Sometimes we fight physically," Mr. Li said matter-of-factly.
"Mostly it's the public that's going to start."
Prosecutors won't accuse Senator Ensigen
The Justice Department's prosecutors cleared Senator John Ensign of a criminal allegation related to his affair with a former campaign adviser and his efforts to secure the lobbies, lawyers announced to the senator on Wednesday.
The decision ends the most serious legal threat that Mr. Ensix, a Nevada Republican Republican, has faced and who, up until the scandal, has been considered a possible Republican presidential nominee for 2012. His affair has made headlines and damaged his career.
Mr Ensigno's office said he was "certainly pleased" with the decision, which followed almost a one-year investigation.
The Senator could still face disciplinary action with the Senate Ethics Committee.
Mr Ensign's lawyers said the Justice Department had "informed us that Senator Ensign would no longer be the target of the investigation and that there was no plan to bring any further charges against him on the matter."
The Justice Department declined to comment on the case.
The decision came as a surprise to many ethical lawyers outside the Department of Justice who argued that there was serious evidence that Mr. Ensign was preparing a violation of a ban on federal lobbying.
Interviews, e-mails and other documents, which were released in October 2009 in the New York Times, said that Mr. Ensign was helping Douglas Hampton, one of his assistants, secure a lucrative lobby that was supposed to lead to the silence of Mr Hampton and his wife, Cynthia, about the senator's affair with her.
Mrs. Hampton once worked as a treasurer in the Ensigno campaign.
Mr Ensign has contacted a number of political supporters to find work for Mr Hampton and promote their views after employing Mr. Hampton to lobby for them, putting out so much documents and interviews.
Since Hampton had until recently been Mr Ensign's assistant, he was excluded from lobbying his former employer or Senate for a year.
Ethics experts say the unusual settlement of Mr. Ensigno and Mr. Hampton, who were friends before the affair, emerged in violation of a ban on lobbying.
From the evidence released "it has emerged that this is a clear case of drafting violations of the law," said Stanley Brand, a Washington-based lawyer who specializes in defending clients who are under investigation by the government.
While Justice Department officials declined to explain the reasons for the decision in Senator Ensign, Mr. Brand, and other lawyers specializing in ethics said the ministry was cautious in terms of political lawsuits, after a large number of failures, including a decision last year to suspend a lawsuit against former Senator Ted Stevens amid allegations of misconduct.
Federal prosecutors "clearly hid their weapons," said Melanie Sloan, executive director of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington (Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington), an attorney's group that filed several complaints against Mr. Ensign in the matter.
"It shows basically that high-ranking government officials are not covered by laws," she added.
Paul Coggins, Mr. Ensign's main lawyer, said in an interview that he had been called by the Justice Department's prosecutor on Monday and then the next day, and told him that the ministry had found no allegations against his client.
Mr. Ensign's advocacy team is working with prosecutors in an effort to convince them that the senator, as his office reiterated in Wednesday's statement, "acted in accordance with the law."
Mr Coggins declined to clarify the legal arguments he handed over to prosecutors.
"I'm not comfortable recounting the details of my discussions with the Ministry of Justice in addition to the fact that we think they've made the right decision," he said.
Last month, the Federal Electoral Committee decided to halt an investigation into a separate complaint in which an ethics group files allegations allegations that a payment of Ensigno's parents to $96,000 has violated federal electoral law.
The Electoral Commission said it did not have enough evidence to prove that the money meant nothing more than a gift.
One company for which Mr. Ensign helped Mr. Hampton find lobbying work was NV Energy, a major Nevada company that had long supported the senator.
One e-mail from December 2008 proved that, after Mr. Hampton began acting as a lobbyist at the company, Mr. Ensigen's chief of staff - John Lopez - communicated with Mr. Hampton and told him that he "pounded" on the Home Office regarding the subject matter being discussed.
Mr Ensign also participated in lunch with Mr. Hampton and another Nevada client, Allegiant Air, and called on the transport minister to arrange an appointment on behalf of the airline after hiring Mr. Hampton for lobbying work.
When news of the affair first surfaced last year, Mr. Ensign apologized to his constituents and resigned from his post as the Senate's fourth-ranked Republican leader.
After many months of stagnation, Mr. Ensign told reporters two weeks ago that he planned to take part in a third term in the Senate.
In a report that Mr Ensign is no longer the target of a criminal investigation by his spokeswoman, Jennifer Cooper, said the senator hopes the Ethics Committee will quickly follow the Department of Justice and end the entire investigation.
"Senator Ensign is looking forward to continuing his diligent work in the name of the Nevada people," she said.
Cheshire municipality's challenge turns to Twitter
Half a dozen of them wrote in the courtroom.
Minute by minute, they sent messages of gruesome testimony via Twitter.
Finally, the defense says more than 140,000 messages on Twitter dealt with raping and tying babies to beds in a house that had been set on fire.
On Thursday, Steven J. Hayes, who was convicted of killing three citizens of Cheshire, Connecticut, the family, took place and an official termination was to be held with the imposition of the death penalty, for which the jury voted.
But Mr Hayes' lawyers have already filed appeals to the court with requests and arguments that the court would likely employ for years.
One of the most provocative is that intensive reporting on the process - including the widespread use of Twitter by major reporters - created a "circus atmosphere" and this widespread, imminent penetration of information with outrageous details then introduced the jury in a decision in the sense of influencing the opinion of the public.
This claim could force judges to address the question of whether, with the advent of new technologies, not requiring new rules in the courtroom as well.
"As I've experienced with this trial, I think there's a real danger that the inclusion of new technologies in the courtroom affects the rights of fair trial for individuals," Thomas J. Ullmann said in a statement.
Jon C. Blue's High Court judge briefly dismissed last week's claims regarding last week's decree and that the media was "carefully controlled" and that there was no evidence pointing out that the jury had acted under the influence of the public.
But it's probably not the last word in this case.
For decades, courts have acknowledged that reporting notorious cases can raise queries about the justice process.
In a 1966 court, the U.S. Supreme Court overturned the conviction of a Cleveland chiropractor, Dr Sam Sheppard, for the murder of his wife, saying in his ruling that Sheppard versus Maxwell was a "carnival atmosphere" during the trial because of the media.
Some are convinced that this offence was the inspiration of the TV show and the film "Refugee."
Some lawyers said the appeals courts, which reviewed Hayese's verdict half a century later, could be surprised by the argument that new rules could be necessary to determine the mode of communication from courtrooms, which was unimaginable in Dr. Sheppard's case.
"There's a general cause for concern about the impact of the new media" in high-profile cases," said John J. Donohue III, a Stanford Law School professor who studied the death penalty in Connecticut and wrote a report saying it had been exercised arbitrarily.
Professor Donohue said some judges may be intrigued by the idea that restricting immediate communication from courtrooms can make the process fairer.
The Twitter case is only one outlined by Mr Hayes' lawyers, who would be able to argue for years to delay the execution of the verdict.
Advocate lawyers, for example, also argue that Mr Hayes's case may be biased when Judge Blue refuses to allow him to test the reactions of potential jurors by showing them a crime of horrors when choosing a jury.
Some of the jurors said they were horrified by photographs taken by Jennifer Hawke-Petit, 48, and her daughters Hayley, 17, and Michaela, 11.
Advocates say they will also reject the constitutionality of the death penalty, arguing that the judge incorrectly dismissed a juror who was critical of prosecutors and that his decision wrongly put more pressure on the verdict and directed the jury to think over the weekend.
The judges weren't separated.
But their arguments about the influence of the scope of the process may attract the greatest interest.
They said in opposition to the verdict filed last month that the case had dominated reports in Connecticut and was debated during an election campaign by candidates for governor and the United States Senate.
They argued that the media were constantly fanning the flames of emotion on this case.
"Daily rush of the media, cameras and reporters before the trial," the lawyer wrote, "was something that has not yet been seen here in New Haven, and it is perhaps also a nationwide rarity."
Their focus on Twitter is controversial.
In interviews, some journalists argued that imminent reporting was more outrageous than reported later and, from their point of view, judicial police should manage the scope of other trials.
But some lawyers have demanded appellate courts with frequently used arguments that social and technological changes require an adjustment to the courts.
Mr Hayes' lawyers are seeking this right.
In their archives, they were referring to the 1966 Supreme Court's decision on Dr. Sheppard's judgment, because of the breadth of the trial and wanted to clarify the requirement that the new media require new rules.
"This case," they wrote, "represents the Sheppard case versus Maxwell of the 21st century."
Can politeness overcome the abyss?
After a campaign that was notaceous for its offensiveness and negativism, a small amount of courtesy appeared in Washington this week.
Is this the first swallow of true cooperation between the two political parties, or merely an illustrative moment before the rapid return to party fighting in the capital?
Tuesday's deliberation between President Obama and parliamentary leaders yielded no major discoveries on crucial topics that must be discussed during the session.
In any case, however, it was a polite discussion about the differences of opinion of two parties, as well as defining their shared responsibilities, rather than another empty episode of partisan struggle.
Wednesday's public meeting of the president's deficit committee was also civilized, despite the political disparities that remain between members.
It seems to be clear that, for several months, close synergies have built mutual respect, which extends beyond the ideological abyss, which still divides members.
And it's quite difficult to say that a new era of cooperation has begun.
Both sides are now in the trial period - probing each other signs of collegiality and compromise, while trying to assess the boundaries of public opinion and voter patience towards a renewed party war.
Therefore, Tuesday's meeting and its later evaluation by the participants were also carried out with a minimum of requirements or recriminations.
The philosophical differences between the Obamas and the Republicans are too extensive and too deep to propose a rapid change in the circumstances that have been going on here for the past two years.
Whether it's a matter of taxes, spending, health care or rights, both sides are miles away.
However, Washington prepared the November election at least quickly.
"It's not a question of whether regulatory issues have changed," Pete Wehner, Bush's administrator, said now at the Ethics and Public Policy Center.
“They are unavoidable after the election.
The election aroused the emotions of the parties, and then they fell silent.
It's good."
On several issues, especially those to be discussed at the session, Republicans and Democrats have little chance of finding a solution.
Bush's tax cut expired at the end of the year, and no one considers it favorable.
Both parties want to find a way to protect some or all, either permanently or temporarily.
Similar to the likely compromise on unemployment benefits only because Republicans can't look at how they support tax cuts for the wealthy, while unemployed people refuse unemployment benefits.
After Tuesday's meeting at the White House, there is already greater optimism about a possible agreement on these issues, along with ratifying a new opening treaty.
But William Galston of the Brookings Institution points out that successful cooperation on fundamental issues won't necessarily lead to the relationship of two political parties when the 112th Congress convenes for January.
Ratherwise, a probable period of disagreement, or even unavoidable
"After doing things that have to be done, psychology, in a brief perspective, goes back to one of the confrontations," he said.
Wehner also expressed skepticism - which is largely widespread - that politeness will lead is real cooperation.
"I look at all the happenings between today and 2012 as a shadow box, when the main events occur in 2012," he said.
The reasons to a large extent are related to compiling a new Republican majority in the White House and the tea party voters who helped create it.
Republican leaders now face the dangers of exaggeration, they know they have to behave according to their promises and have to reduce government spending and repeal a new health care law.
These adherent lines are clear in their initial responses to deficit recommendations, which include reforming the tax code and raising some taxes, increasing pension age and cutting spending.
Social leaders Alan Simpson and Erskine Bowles have taken one of the toughest tasks in Washington and, in their view, have put pressure on the issue of the deficit to a public program in ways that cannot be ignored.
However, they need 14 votes in an 18-member commission so that they can present their plan as an official recommendation and therefore fight to get as close as possible to this number.
The biggest resistance is among the elected officials in the jury, who suggest that in the case of financial issues we have, at least today, a small foundation or will for compromise.
Galston predicts two phases in battles between the White House and parliamentary Republicans.
The first will begin next year in the form of partisan conflicts, when Republicans will call on Obama to resolve the budget, taxes and health care, followed by a period of greater partisan collaboration.
What's going to cause a shift?
public opinion.
There is a lot of evidence that, despite partisanship in the last election, most Americans want to continue to see two parties working together.
If it were possible to move towards the stalemate, as it did in 1995, then both sides will closely monitor the public's reactions.
Whoever threatens to lower the vote is likely to change course and seeks a compromise.
This can be an optimistic point of view.
Partisanship and direction are now greater than they did in the mid-1990s, and it turns out that a larger ideological gap has been created between Obama and parliamentary Republicans than it was with Republicans and Bill Clinton.
At the same time, activists on both sides see 2010 as a prelude to more important elections in 2012.
Based on that reality, the courtesy that emerged this week would welcome many Americans.
But it will cost a lot of effort on both sides to find common foundations on issues that keep dividing them.
U.S. documentary portrays Moscow as a haven for corruption
The management of the city is in the hands of "kleptocracy", which continues with part of the bribes and bribes for protection that come on the way to the Kremlin, the U.S. Embassy in Russia said in the February records.
Both the police and the Federal Security Service largely hold protective wings that help explain the high cost of living in Moscow.
They collect money not only from legal trades, but also from organized criminal groups.
Each layer of bureaucracy - which Prime Minister Vladimir Putin has called a "verticale of office" - will take his own when the money begins to move up the ladder.
The entry, which was sent to Washington under the name of Ambassador John Beyrle, was posted via WikiLeaks on their website on Wednesday.
The record is based on sources whose names have been edited.
The record was written at a time when Yuri Luzhkov was still the mayor, accusing him of a large part in corruption.
He was dismissed in October by President Dmitry Medvedev, but since then we can only observe minimal changes in purification.
Luzhkov and his wife Yelena Baturina, who owns a prominent design firm, have for many years steadily denied allegations of corruption, and won several lawsuits for perjury for such allegations.
In September, he said on a Russian television station when he was asked about the proven testimony of corruption: "It's crazy, it's dirt, it's crap."
The Russians, with absolute conviction, believe that if their society is corrupt, it will turn out to vote.
However, it is unusual to encounter such details spoken in such a way, in an embassy report, and it is also unheard that diplomats from foreign countries would do so.
"The government of the city of Moscow and its connections with the criminal elements have led some to call it 'dysfunctional,' and confirms that the government operates more like kleptocracy than as a government," the report says.
“Criminal elements enjoy the so-called ‘krysha’ (a term from the world of crime and the mafia that literally means ‘roof’ or protection) provided to them by the police, the Federal Security Service, the Federal Security Service (FSB), the Ministry of the Interior, and the Public Prosecutor’s Office, as well as everywhere in the government bureaucracy of the city of Moscow.
"Analysts have identified and a three-stage structure in Moscow's criminal world."
"Luzhkov is at the top."
"Federal security service, the Home Office and militias are at second level."
"And, finally, at the lowest level are ordinary criminals and corrupt inspectors."
"It's an ineffective system in which criminal groups fill vacancies in some areas because the city doesn't provide some services."
The report says the FSB is taking money from the biggest firms, and police are targeting smaller businesses.
One source said, "Owners of shops in Moscow realize that it is better to get protection from the FSB or the Ministry of the Interior (rather than from organized criminal groups), because they have not only more weapons, resources and forces than criminal groups, but they are also protected by law."
"For this reason, protection from criminal gangs is no longer so on course."
The record says that while withdrawing money is comprehensive, protection itself may already be in trouble; even those who pay can sometimes be arrested.
To those who do not pay fast enough, their trades close under different pretexts.
Medvedev has spoken several times about fighting corruption, but admitted that he has made little progress.
An embassy report speaks of men taking suitcases, possibly stuffed with cash, to the Kremlin itself.
"In its fight against corruption, Medvedev has to rely on bureaucrats," Georgy Satarov, director of Moscow's research team Indem, said in a recent interview.
"It's part of this bureaucracy."
"It's not part of the political class, because there are no longer political classes in Russia
Without politicians, without opposition, without the separation of powers, corruption is inevitable, he said.
Indem estimates that nationwide, corruption costs Russia more than €300 billion a year.
The country ranks 154th in a recent survey on the global perception of corruption by Transparency International (countries are ranked from the least to the most corrupt).
Revealing the report of the U.S. embassy, which appeared in Moscow only in the late in the evening, certainly did not provoke enthusiasm with the Russian government, although only to the extent that Luzhkov could be blamed for the Moscow deficiencies, it may be an opportunity for Kremlin to argue about the solution of the problem.
Speaking to CNN with Larry King, Putin said the documents WikiLeaks released may have been forgery and reacted angrily to a statement that U.S. diplomats called him Batman and Medvedeva Robin.
"The truth about the whole matter is that it is about our mutual synergy, which is an important factor in domestic politics in this country," he said.
"But to be honest, we didn't think it was going to be done with such arrogance, with such coercion, you understand, so unethically."
Khmer Rouge veterans are forced to come to terms with the past
In a spore-lit concrete classroom with dirty and peeled walls, Anlong Veng High School's principal reminded the man who built it, the leader of the Khmer Rouge Ta Mok.
"Everyone here loves Ta Moka."
"He was a good leader and took care of his people," Sreng Kor Ma said, 42.
Known as "butcher" because of its brutality during the Khmer Rouge rule, the commander remains popular in this remote Khmer Rouge possession in north-western Cambodia, where he built hospitals, bridges and other infrastructure, and where thousands of former soldiers of this organization still live.
But this year, 12 years after the Khmer Rouges gave up the government, long-standing loyalty is also undermined in Anlong Veng.
In April, victims at a forum of truth and reconciliation were allowed to publicly confront people who were part of the regime.
In June, the government provided textbooks explaining for the first time children the history of the Khmer Roudes and their former soldiers.
And in July, the United Nations and the Cambodian Court brought together the first conviction of a former member of the Khmer Rouge, a sentence of a former leader, the notorious violent Tuol Sleng association, Kaing Khek Iev, better known as the Spirit, to 35 years in prison.
With all such progress, there has been growing unrest among Khmer Rouge veterans in Anlong Veng, complicating reconciliation efforts and their attempts to re-engage in Cambodian society.
"There's resentment and fear among the former Khmer Rouges, but they're helpless to do anything," said Chhang Youk, head of Cambodia's documentation centre, an independent organization that examines the regime.
"For them, life among the Khmer Rouge was wonderful, but the regime became a symbol of evil."
“It divides families.”
During Cambodia's Khmer Rouge government, from 1975 to 1979, an estimated 1.7 million people were executed or starved to death, from illness or overhaul.
When the Vietnamese invaded and overthrew the Pol Pot government in 1979, remnants of the regime and its troops fled to the Cambodian border with Thailand.
There they launched an uprising that lasted until the last movement in December 1998.
Pol Pot, Nuon Chea and Ieng Sary managed guerrilla wars from their bases in the mountains and jungles of western Cambodia, while Ta Mok cultivated followers in Anlong Veng.
But in the mid-1990s, after a peace deal sponsored by the U.N
Pol Pot died of natural causes in 1998, and Ta Mok, who persevered, was captured by government forces in the non-distant Dangrek mountains in 1999.
He died in Phnom Penh prison in 2006 while awaiting trial.
Their patrons no longer cared for them, most former Khmer Rouge fighters sank into ordinary Cambodian rural life, and many of them nostalgically recall the time of the movement.
Although several elite Khmer Rouges have retained their local government position in exchange for their weapons, their position remains weak, like unqualified farmers.
"These people had only a minimal benefit from the surrender," said Sok Leang of the Center for Justice and Reconciliation, conducted by public forums throughout Cambodia, including Anlong Vengu.
"They are surrounded by the regime's utopian agrarian ideology."
"They were educated without any idea of conducting trade."
Sor Lim, 55, who joined the Khmer Rouge as a teenager in 1974, fought in the jungle around Anlong Veng before settling down as a poor farmer in 1998.
"Life under the Khmera Rouge was good," he said.
'Mok has supported everyone.'
"But now life is hard, because we have to secure everything ourselves."
The ongoing Khmer Rouge court has also raised concerns.
Earlier next year, the court will begin investigating Nuon Chea, Khieu Samphan, Ieng Sara and his wife, Ieng Thirith, another former Khmer Rouge minister.
The court has a mandate to prosecute the leaders and people responsible for crimes, but it has not been said whether it will continue on the given four occasions.
It was little to calm the concerns in Anlong Veng.
Recent media speculations centered on Im Chaema, 64, who was the regional leader in Banteay Meanchey province during the Khmer Rouge government in the late 1970s.
In 2007 she told researchers from the Documentation Center in Cambodia that she oversaw the construction of the Trapeang Thma dam, a project on which thousands of forced workers were believed to have perished.
Recent hot evening, Im Chaem returned from work in the field to her wooden cottage on a dirt road outside Anlong Veng.
As the sun began to descend and shadows appeared across the arid weeds, Im Chaem refused to talk about his past among the Khmer Rouges.
If the court called her in, she would refuse to go.
"Peace reigns in Cambodia and is stable," she said.
"If there are further prosecutions, there will be war."
Prime Minister Hun Sen, who defected since the Khmer Rouges in 1978, has also repeatedly warned that there will be instability if the court seeks other suspects, although experts and historians disagree.
"Cambodia has to dig up a deep hole and bury the past in it," he said.
But confronting the past is just what Cambodia has to do to move forward, Chhang Youk of the documentation centre said.
"Reconciliation in Khmerian proportions means sticking to broken pieces," he said.
"It is our duty to put these broken pieces together so that we can understand each other."
The center has released the first textbook approved by the Khmer Rouge government, it has 75 pages and the title "History of Democratic Cambodia."
It was distributed in Anlong Veng in June as an addition to a textbook in the history of the Ministry of Education, which contains less than four parties about the Khmer Rouge.
Like many people in Cambodia, young people in Anlong Veng knew very few details about the Khmer Rouge, despite the city's connection to the regime.
Touch Valeak, 19, a high school student at Anlong Veng said the new textbook helps students understand an important part of their history.
But his parents reacted skeptically when he took home a book to study.
"My family rejects many parts of the textbook and the courts," he said.
"They're suspicious because they're not sure how many people will be prosecuted by the court."
Such resistance makes the process of reconciliation more complex and difficult, Sok Leang said.
But public forums, textbooks and the trial are beginning to have an impact, he said.
The Khmer Rouge still has a strong charm here.
At the top of the Dangrek Mountains is a weed-covered path and emptied with discarded plastic bags that leads to a rectangle made of black soot, covered with a rusty tin roof.
Pol Pota's body was burned on a pile of tires after his death in 1998.
Nuom Sothea, 31, a cellphone saleswoman on the side of the road said she didn't know much about the man who was cremated at that location.
"But he has a strong spirit, many locals walk around and pray to him," she said.
Nuom Sothea's birthday was over, and she planned to visit Pol Pot's resting place later, where he would bring a bundle of ripe bananas in the hope that it would bring her luck.
Belarus agrees that it appears its stockpiles of uranium-based weapons
In a surprise victory in President Obama's campaign to secure nuclear material around the world, the Belarusian government announced on Wednesday that it would renounce its stockpiles of highly enriched uranium, a critical component of nuclear weapons.
The deal was released in a joint statement, issued after a security summit by Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton and Belarus Secretary of State Sergei Martynov.
The U.S. government for years has been trying to persuade Belarus to give up highly enriched uranium and appeared to have encountered a stumbling block last spring.
But U.S. officials told The Washington Post that in the past two months, a couple of classified operations have been carried out with Belarus to remove 187 pound uranium-based weapons from a Belarusian research facility.
This has paved the way for Wednesday's agreement to remove the rest of the material - approximately 500 pounds, according to an official speaking on condition of anonymity because this is a sensitive matter.
The deal proved to be one of the most important results of Obama's 47th National Nuclear Security Summit in April at which Belarus was decommissioned.
And it was apparently a good move to secure an invitation of the former Soviet Republic for a subsequent summit in 2012.
"I was genuinely fascinated by the fact that they wanted a place for a nuclear security summit2012."
"This proves that the process is working," said Ken Luongo, president of the Partnership for Global Security, a group that supports nuclear safety.
Clinton called the treaty "a "very important, important step" from Belarus.
Obama vowed in Prague in April 2009 to secure all sensitive nuclear materials around the world within four years.
Since then, the U.S. government has helped six countries get rid of all their stockpiles of highly enriched uranium.
Belarus will be seventh.
A dozen countries did so under the administration of George W. Bush.
At the time of Obama's Nuclear Security Summit, Belarusian President Alexander Lukashenko announced that his nation would never give up its uranium.
"We have highly enriched uranium - hundreds of pounds of what is essentially the basis for a weapon and less enriched uranium," Lukashenko said afterwards.
"It's our commodity...We don't intend to make dirty bombs, and we're not going to sell it to anyone either."
"We're only using it for research purposes, that's all."
A few months ago, however, Belarus acceded to the United States and expressed an interest in reaching an agreement.
The first commitment to remove Belarusian uranium in October was particularly complicated because the material was slightly irradiated, officials announced.
It was loaded onto a special train that traveled more than 1,300-miles to Russian storage and disposal facilities, officials said.
The second operation was concluded on Monday and was carried out by air, officials said.
The U.S. government spent £13 million on the program, most of it to supply the Belarusian research institute with less enriched uranium, which is not suitable for the production of nuclear weapons.
"Everything has been coordinated very carefully and managed on the basis of confidence," which led to an agreement signed by Clinton, said Andrew Bieniawski, an Energy Department official working for Global Threat Reduction.
This year, the James Martin Center for the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons Studies estimated that Belarus has at least 88 pounds of uranium usable to manufacture weapons.
According to the joint statement, Belarus will seek to eliminate its stockpiles by 2012.
Relations between Belarus and the United States have been strained for years, mainly due to Lukashenko's cruel repression of human rights.
But Wednesday's statement suggests the possibility of gradually thawing.
"Welcoming progress on these global security issues, the United States and Belarus have acknowledged that more consistent adherence to democracy and human rights in Belarus remains the basis for improving mutual relations and is necessary for the progress of the country and its people," he said.
The Spanish government didn't make darlings with CIA flights
The Spanish government did not do expensive about CIA flights carrying jailed talibances and Al-Qaida members to Guantánamo via Spanish territory, as the United States envoy to Madrid expressed in his telegram.
This is according to documents provided by WikiLeaks daily El País, which also states that Prime Minister MarÍa’s first representative Teresa Fernández de la Veg demanded that the Spanish executive be informed and “could thus show that she has proper oversight of foreign planes flying Spanish airspace.”
In June 2006, following a Communication from the Council of Europe accusing a dozen states, including Spain, of complicity in human rights violations, a meeting took place between U.S. Ambassador Eduard Aguirre and Fernández de la Veg.
A representative of the prime minister said the Council of Europe's communication had "extricated Zapatera's government from the game" and insisted that Spain had nothing to hide in the matter.
De la Veg stressed that Spain has no problem with flights of secret services through its territory.
"Simply they just want to be informed" so that they are not "caught unprepared," Aguirre says in one of the documents.
Miguel Ángel Moratinos, the then foreign minister, also hinted to the U.S. ambassador that the Spanish government "wanted to give this matter the lowest possible outlines, but because this matter was in the hands of the judges, the cabinet had limited possibilities of influence."
In February 2007, prosecutor Vicente González informed the Embassy that, as far as he was concerned, he did not intend to block the judge's initiative to demand declassification of documents from the National Secret Service Center (CNI) and the Ministry of Defence relating to CIA flights.
"This information does not contain any incriminated file or any sensitive information," the embassy explained after talking to the prosecutor.
However, the U.S. expressed concerns more because of "apparent coordination between a Spanish judge and German prosecutors" than because of declassification of CNI information or the Defense Ministry.
"This coordination among independent experts will complicate our efforts to solve this case discreetly among governments," Aguirre regrets.
In March 2007, on the occasion of the lunch of U.S. Ambassador and Interior Minister Alfred Pérez Rubalcaba, he told the ambassador that "although Spanish judges are very jealous of their independence and will do what they think is appropriate," he has already instructed his co-workers "to avoid angry comments with the intention of reducing tensions."
The ambassador also expresses his concerns about the actions of the political party Partido Popular (PP) in this matter.
"Despite its general pro-American orientation, our experience tells us that Partido Popular won't hesitate to take advantage of any suggestions that Zapatero's government has tolerated or deliberately ignored U.S. actions that could be interpreted as possible violations of human rights, even if these revelations could have negative consequences for the U.S. government."
Confessed mother-murderer complained on net at maternity 'worry'
The young woman was talking about the baby when it was already dead, and she had her blog: "Terror after Galicia"
Mónica Juanatey Fernández calls herself "Muki" on the Internet.
with this nickname, which she has used since she was little, she communicated with her cousin and at least one friend; both are from the village of Noia in the area of La Coruña, from which the young woman comes.
The reports are from 2008.
In order to understand them, it is necessary to know the woman's emotional life, which the ABC newspaper reconstructs with the help of the testimonies of its family members.
A 30-year-old woman from Galicia entered into a relationship with industrialist Iván Túnez in 1998.
They've been dating for a year.
Shortly after they broke up, she found out she was pregnant.
The man wanted to know if the newborn baby was his, but his ex-girlfriend had never run DNA tests.
She soon found a new acquaintance.
Her chosen one was Alberto, a young man from the nearby town of Lousame.
Their cohabitation lasted for a long time.
Little Caesar lived with them.
When the girl went to the Balearic Islands looking for a job, the baby stayed with her partner, who even came to Palma to visit her for a week.
However, it is clear from the context of her communication that the relationship fell apart.
In March 2008, she's alone in Menorca and writes: "Alberto is some upset that I've left for work."
"When I told him, it seemed good to him, but now he realizes that he has to be with the baby all day and he can't do what he wants."
Then she talks about the text message she just received: "I want you to come back later this month or give me an address where to send your baby."
He complains: "If he doesn't like me to leave the baby with my mother, I'll take it when I get back," he argues.
In May he talks about his return to my residence: "I'll go there this week, but I'll only abstain until the 19th, because I have to go for a job so I don't lose the place".
He explains that if all goes well, he'll take his son together.
"He'll be with whoever he's supposed to be with, that is, with me, and it'll never happen again in my life that I'm going to leave him with someone but my mother," a dubious intention given her bad relationships.
There's another post in July.
When he taps this report, it is believed that the child who arrived on the island on the first day of this month, and was probably murdered 10, is already dead.
"César is satisfied on the one hand, but on the other hand he's not."
"On the one hand, he's with me, and he quite likes it here, but on the other hand, he goes to school almost every day."
They're talking about moving to Majorca.
"He'll be going there (son) to summer school, so I'm going to save a little bit of money for a nanny."
Seventeenth greetings and complaints.
"Great kiss from Caesar asking me when you're coming."
"Alberto says he sent you what videos I don't know what kind of e-mails he wants, he'll meet me, sex is needed."
Thirtieth another message: "The kid's fine, he's going to summer school to learn Catalan."
There's also one message about Víctor, the current partner: "He's giving me money."
There are Mónika's posts on blogs dedicated to anime and manga on the Internet.
She even had her own on MySpace, now revised, where she called herself "Muki ex-prisoner."
"Terror after Galician (motto) ".
The last man with whom she lived, the guardian, expresses his regret and feels guilty.
He doesn't want to believe that everything would happen because she didn't tell him she was a single mother.
Mónica yesterday entered the provincial prison in Palma, where she arrived from a detention center in Menorca.
The attorney, Carlos Maceda, said the defendant didn't remember how things happened and "that she didn't intend to hurt the baby in any way."
Previously, however, she had admitted that she had drowned César in a bathtub and stored his body in a suitcase.
Both the defense and the prosecutor are asking for a psychiatric examination.
The People's Party would win 4 seats in the Chamber of Deputies, and the PSC (Socialist Party of Catalonia) would lose 9 seats
with the results of November 28, the PP party could have the main say at town halls in Lérida, Tarragona and Barcelona.
After a significant increase in votes received and the number of experienced MPs during the recent autonomous elections, the Catalan PP already has a given idea of a general election, whether there is an increase or not, its prospects are more than positive, since the number of votes in national elections is usually one of those compared to the Catalan elections.
In 2008, the Catalan People's Parties won 610,473 votes in the general election, while in the 2006 autonomous elections, this figure was only 316,222.
Last Sunday, PPC (Catalonia People's Party) received 384.091 votes.
If this tendency is sustained, which was supported by the transfer of votes from the PSC and the loyalty of PP voters, analysts of this party calculated that if general elections are held, PPC could receive 12 deputies in the Chamber of Deputies compared to the existing 8 (Convergencia i Unió, Czech Shoda and Union) would also lose at least 4 seats
These calculations would confirm that Catalonia is crucial for the PP and PSOE (Spanish Socialist Workers' Party).
According to this analysis of the People's Party, the ERC (Left Republican Party of Catalonia) would lose one MP - it now has 3, while the ICV would still have one mandate, possibly winning one more.
However, according to the electoral calendar, the nearest rendezvouse will take place in May 2011 on the occasion of municipal elections, where the People's Party could also have a decisive influence on the end of socialist rule in four major provincial cities, as well as changes in the Barcelona Council (Diputación de Barcelona), one of the administrations with the largest projected budget - 661 million euros for 2011.
Both nationalists and the people's people agree with the possibility that the PSC could lose town halls in Barcelona, Tarragona, Lérida and Gerona, where the Socialists would not receive a majority and would not even be able to turn to their traditional allies, the ERC and ICV parties.
Given the Republican debacle, it wouldn't be impossible for this party to lose its representation in the Barcelonian council - as at least 5% of the vote needs to be won.
The PP has increased the number of votes received in four capitals and could have decisive influence in Barcelona, Lérida and Tarragona.
Nationalists are convinced that they could play a major role in the Turnover in Tarragona, where they ruled together with the PP party before the current mayor, Josep Félix Ballesteros.
In Lérida, the PP received only 500 more votes than PSC, whose mayor Angel Ros has very good relations with Mariano Rajoy - during the last campaign, they met on several occasions.
The People's Party has also been acquired at other important town halls such as Badalona, Castelldeels and Reus.
Currently, the PP is represented at 34 town halls in Catalonia and would like to create candidates in as many municipalities as possible.
Catalan cell provides Al-Qaida with thousands of passports
In addition to seven detainees in Barcelona, three others have been detained in Thailand
Seven Pakistanis and one of the Nigerians, who were detained by agents of the General Information Commission in Barcelona, formed a jihadist cell controlled from Thailand, which arranged passports for various Al-Qaida groups, among whom were also "Tigers of the Liberation of Tamil Ílam" or "Lashkar-e-Taiba", while the latter group was the author of the assassinations in Mumbai.
The Ministry of the Interior attaches great importance to the investigation of this matter, aiming to ensure, with the help of Operation Kampai, the weakening of the counterfeiting apparatus of Al Qaeda at the international level and "limiting its operational capabilities".
Investigators estimate that within the two years it was active in Catalonia, it could send thousands of passports to Thailand, where they were counterfeited and then delivered to groups that form Al Qaeda's criminal galaxy, so that their "fighters" could travel across borders in Europe and other Western countries with impunity.
This broken cell was not the only active jihad network in Europe, which is why, in addition to the Thai services, other European police units participated in these investigations, and it cannot be ruled out that there will be no similar crackdowns in other European countries in the coming days, such as the one in Barcelona last Tuesday night.
In connection with this operation (when the Pakistanis Junaid Humayun, Atiqur Rehman, Jabran Asghar, Malik Iftikhar Ahmad, Mohammad Saddique Khan Begum, Tanveer Arshad and Nigerian Babatunde Agunbiade were detained), another developed in Thailand, in which the Pakistanis Muhammad Athar Butt and Zeeshan E
It was these men who managed from Bangkok a cell based in Spain and others in other European countries.
The Catalan network, part of which was liquidated in 2009, mainly stole from tourists passports that met the demands of Islamists.
This means that the documents had to belong to men between the ages of 25 and 45, had to be valid for several years and were without marked visas.
The investigative documents emphasize that so far there are no known facts that would link detainees from Catalonia to the assassination in Mumbai, even though the author of these attacks, "Lashkar-e-Taiba", was supposed to be one of the groups that received passports stolen in Spain and then falsified in Thailand.
However, it is worth noting that a few days before this assassination, about a thousand dollars were sent from Catalonia by the transfer agency, which were "inserted" for the purchase of cards into mobile phones, which were later used by members of the "commando", which killed 106 people.
Police have already seized a lot of evidence.
The People's Party (PP) is demanding Masa to halt the fiscal deal
The leader of the PPC (Catalonia People's Party), Alicia Sánchez-Camachová, yesterday had the opportunity to personally present to Artura Mas his conditions, under which she is willing to support his introduction to the office of President Generalitat; these conditions are consistent with those proposed during the campaign, and their priority is the recovery of the economy and the renunciation of risky businesses, such as the economic agreement that the nationalist leaders want to propose during their mandate.
The president of the Catalan People's Party came to a meeting with Arturo Mas with the certainty of 18 seats received in last Sunday's elections, which is a number more than satisfactory for gaininging popularity, so the CiU candidate needs at least one of the formations forming the opposition to abstain from voting for his appointment in office.
The possible agreement with the PP depends on future alliances, which nationalists in the House would like to maintain when the general elections are held, but so far the talks conducted by Mas with the rest of the political leaders in these days are in the Catalan spirit.
When it comes to PP's president in order to get her support for taking office, she "will have to put an end to all sovereign and liberation speeches," as well as an economic agreement - similar to the one from the Basque Country - which the CiU leader even wanted to leave to vote on in a referendum.
Both leaders would like to slim down the bulky political apparatus of autonomous administration and reduce inheritance and donation taxes.
Details of the negotiations on Masa's putting into office will be dealt with within PP Jordi Cornet and Dolors Montserrat.
Mas has already met with Joaquin Nadal (PSC), who has vowed tough opposition, Joan Herrera (ICV) and will meet today with Joan Puigcercós (ERC), Alberto Rivera (Ciutadans) and Joan Laporta (Solidaritat Catalana).
Geriatric facility's director assures there have been no deaths as a result of "lack of measures"
Introductions to downtown Olot do not rule out their caregiver killed more than 11 clients
The leadership of La Caritat de Olot (Gerona) gave a press conference yesterday to recount what kind of damage was caused by the institution of criminal conduct of Joan Villa Dilmé, a caregiver who - while employed here - killed at least eleven seniors he had in his care.
The director of this retirement home, Joan Sala, was accompanied by the facility's lawyer, attorney Joan Canada, and announced that "by wishing La Caritat's home it would be able to bring charges against the culprit" responsible for manslaughter, but "legality and responsibility oblige us to leave this process to the competent authorities."
In addition to damaging the reputation that this institution may suffer due to the fact that it did not detect the caregiver's criminal activities in a timely manner, a major threat to La Caritat's home is now the requirements for substantive compensation, which are already being calculated by legal representatives of the families of the bereaved.
Joan Vila Dilmé admitted that his first crime took place in August 2009 and that he has not stopped killing ever since
The judge ordered the exhumation of the eight bodies of clients of the home for the elderly on suspicion that in many cases no doctor had examined the deceased personally, but that - due to the age and poor health of the patients - everything was handled over the phone.
La Caritat resolutely dismissed this extreme through its director yesterday, stressing that "the lack of action could in no way have been the cause of what happened."
La Caritat's attorney added that "there are no indications in the case that, apart from his own accused, any third person has committed misconduct."
A man nicknamed the now "Angel of Death" confessed 11 deaths yesterday, specifying that he committed his first crime on August 29, 2009 and killed a second old woman a month and a half later.
in this case, it caused death by administering too high an insulin dose, as opposed to the first case of using a mixture of soothing pills.
Director La Caritat said it was complicated to control staff's access to medication.
Insulin and cocktail sedatives were likely in six occasions out of nine means used by this caregiver to kill patients in their efforts to relieve their suffering.
However, only the analyses, which are taking place in the laboratory, should show whether Villa does not lie and actually look for a less cruel death for his first victims or, on the contrary, killed them by administering lye, as he has already acknowledged in the case of the three women killed between October 12 and 17.
in La Caritat they do not believe the medical claims and do not rule out that the number of those killed can be higher than what he confessed to, and justify their behavior in the various versions handed down by Villa to the court, knowing that he must change his actions when it became clear that he had killed eight people.
That's why they're saying that they want to be available to the judiciary in order to illuminate the full extent of the matter.
The European Central Bank is in danger
Investors reacted sharply to yesterday's threat from European Central Bank President Trichet, which warned that "markets should not underestimate our ability to respond."
Marginal differentials closed by force and shares to European banks fell by more than 5%.
However, we should also not underestimate the ability of the European Central Bank to be wrong - the euro has just risen above critical value.
What happened yesterday is typical of an illiquid market, which, although there is a decline, is recovering - but thanks to short and very speculative positions.
It will take some time for an investor to lose concerns.
A month ago, the European Central Bank cited us to take back the liquidity rate today, and tomorrow it will have to appeal for the fourth time in two years.
Bundesbank demanded the termination of a plan to build the national debt for inefficiency and the elimination of all extraordinary rates of liquidity within a period of time, leaving unrestricted access for only one week.
After the Irish crisis, we expect the level of liquidity of forecasts "at least by March" and adhere to a three-month-long licitation with unrestricted access until the end of this trimester to guarantee liquidity at least June.
We also hope that they will continue with their bond-buying programme and that they will even announce its intensification.
If they do not meet the expectations created, it will create a reaction of the markets from yesterday, and if they continue to withdraw the level of liquidity blindly, God with us!
It was the same at a convention in May, where they argued that they didn't even consider buying bonds, and there has been a collapse of markets - history could be repeated tomorrow.
The European Central Bank must be credible and persistent.
In May, an armoured fist announced on Monday, and on Wednesday it said it had already purchased many, prompting a further collapse of the market.
