However, next Tuesday the government will need to get the approval of the new budget by parliament and still does not have sufficient support, so there continues to be uncertainty in Ireland.
Angela Merkel has tempered the tone of her speech in order to unleash tensions in her face for the next meeting of EU leaders on 14 December, at which it is necessary to reach a political compromise that would show that we are a union and we are all in the same ship.
The problem is that Germany wants to play Wagner, while the others would prefer Beethoven's Ode for Joy.
In Spain yesterday, the prime minister had the opportunity to present a plan of action, which will be approved at the Council of Ministers on Friday.
Pension reform, transparency in autonomous regions and financial institutions, deficit reduction, liberalisation, privatisation, measures to revive employment - to the ears of investors sounds like the Four seasons from Vivaldi.
Tricheta and Zapatera's double effect explains why the Spanish stock exchange rose 4.5%, doubly than other European exchanges.
Unlike in February, the reaction of the government and the Spanish National Bank was swift and convincing - we are probably benefiting from the effect of surprise.
Today, we have a three-year-old bond, which will benefit the effect of Trichet, for which, we hope, there will be a great demand for it, which will help dispel doubts about our refinancing capability.
Asking for a rescue plan would be self-sacrifice for Spain.
They would give us cash we don't need - there's 40,000 million in cash available in El Tesoro, three times more than in March - further high rates and access to markets would be closed to us sine die.
However, it is necessary to continue to use Cesaro's strategy - "not only to be that, but also to seem it."
We'll prove it.
Eulen's group's president will challenge negotiations by Vega Sicilia requested by his children
Storm in open conflict within the Álvarez family, owners of Eulen and El Enebro, does not cease.
David Álvarez, the founder of the group, comes to mind after conceiving it "completely insignificant," El Enebro's legal conduct scheduled for this month 16 and required five of his seven descendants, on whom they should try to force their majority and seize, with the help of the prosecutor's authorities of Bodegas Vega Sicilia.
David Álvarez and his sons Jesús David and MarÍa José are fine-tuning a legal group for a new chapter of their dispute to control part of society.
The founder of the largest corporate service holding does not seem willing to relent in his dispute over membership in the ruling bodies of the El Enebro Group.
five of their seven offspring were deposed from its administrative committee, who were appointed leaders during a meeting of shareholders on 18 January of this year and whose objection is entered in the Commercial Register.
El Enebro's court hearing planned for the 16th of this month, convened by an official of the Commercial Court No. 2 in Madrid, resumed thunder again in the womb of the Álvarez family.
An appeal to a judge is the only legal remedy that guarantees the convocation of the commission until the dispute is resolved.
David Álvarez considers the latest move by Juan Carlos, Pablo, Emilia, Martha and Elvira Mezquiriz to be "a desperate means of his five children, used by judicial deceit and a clear legal scam to strengthen and improve the January 18 negotiations, which were completely void."
This argument will serve as the basis for justifying the legal assault of this conduct as "completely insignificant".
The protagonists themselves, who are discreetly silent, have already tried unsuccessfully for the court to convene a hearing in June.
in El Enebro, a group owning, in addition to other activities Bodegas Vega Sicilia, controls seven descendants of David Álvarez of 100% capital.
However, according to family protocols, the founder of the group requires the right of use for 51% of capital.
This matter is a key issue that will have to be decided by the judge when recounting the question of judicial assault. The date of this trial has not yet been determined.
Red Bull triumphs in Formula 1 with secret weapon from Siemens
The software proposes real-time improvements in vehicles.
Ever since Red Bull invaded the 2004 Formula One series, its history in this extraordinary world of speed is characterized by surprise and success.
Initially, no one took too seriously the possibilities of this newcomer team, and despite the uprising tendency from recent times, they were all betting on Fernando Alonso's Ferrari triumph.
Without wanting to reduce the driving skills of the champion, German Sebastian Vettel, or the possibilities of the Renault engine that drives his car, a legend was born in the world of Formula 1 - the "secret weapon" that the multi-ethnic Siemens put in the hands of Red Bull to make it a winning team.
In fact, it is a powerful software simulating marches, which the German firm launched in 2007 for the purposes of very distant automotive sports.
The so-called PLM, this powerful software device, was used to design assembly lines at production plants around the world, in power plants, and for high-accuracity devices.
Its aim is to faithfully reproduce the product or simulate the process before it is actually created, thus avoiding unnecessary errors and expenses.
PLM has facilitated the development of Canon, Sukhoi aircraft and Nissan’s most famous commercial vehicles.
Siegfried Russwurm, head of Siemens's industrial division, explains that the use of this software device has enabled the Red Bull team to carry out "within a few hours, in near-real-time" modifications and refinements that are otherwise detected during plants and trials.
Russwurm says that in 1992, stables employed an average of 10 technicians who were able to perform a maximum of three or four improvements over one period.
The Red Bull team today works on 180 young highly skilled professionals equipped with powerful information technology who analyze and design every type of modification of racing vehicles.
The executive from Siemens explained that PLM software is able to analyze and improve all elements of Red Bull racing vehicles, with the exception of an engine, for which Renault is fully responsible.
Materials, fuel, aerodynamics and safety features are modified.
Changes can be converted from virtual reality into reality in less than 24 hours thanks to advanced production systems using a laser and highly technologically advanced autoclaves.
The only limit of this technical convenience is the amount of US$40 million representing the maximum that each Formula 1 team can issue for each period to improve its competitiveness.
Red Bull has set up about 50 km from London with a high-tech plant to design and manufacture its racing cars.
There are two buildings, which are restricted by visitors - especially for journalists.
Everything that happens here is considered a "great secret."
The list of participants in this project consists of 580 people, including two pilots - champion Vettel and Mark Webber.
Red Bull's hero in Formula 1 is, without a doubt, fresh champion Sebastian Vettel.
However, there are other protagonists whose help is invaluable, while no one knows, behind the immeasurable technological means that the brand of energy beverages has invested in their participation in racing circuits.
The head of manufacturing technology, Steve Nevey, said five years ago it was possible to say that advances in vehicle research and development contributed to a 50% victory, and the rest were thanks to riders.
Currently, this ratio has changed and the technology is responsible for 85% of its achievements, while pilots contribute 15%.
Nevey acknowledges that Vettel or Webber will never be replaced by computing because they are "unpredictable gladiators."
Within two years, HP will set up 20 of its own stores in cooperation with local distributors
Hewlett-Packard (HP), in partnership with its trading partner Pont Reyes, inaugurated its first exclusive store in Barcelona yesterday.
This company will be the first of a network of 20 stores with an area of 80 to 100 m2, which this multinational company plans to open in Spain in the next two years.
These stores will also be located in 16 existing department stores such as PC City, Carrefour, Saturn and El Corte Inglés.
Helena Herrer, vice-president of Spain’s HP, cited as an example the newly rebuilied store for Barcelona’s Gran Vía, owned by Pont Reyes, which HP has been working with for 30 years.
The North American company has prepared a plan to set up 200 sales outlets in Europe by 2013, which includes 20 Spanish stores.
The first stores were opened last week in Bucharest (Romania) and Belgrade (Serbia), so the store in Barcelona is the third.
Herrera did not want to divulge where other stores would be opened, however she admitted that Madrid could be one of the following venues.
Herrer, who also heads HP's personnel systems, has assured that the philosophy of opening stores will still be the same - using local distributors who have experience and knowledge of the sector.
The North American company advises here with an exclusive design of store equipment (with products that can be tried interactively), which will be identical in Europe.
In addition, HP is also engaged in the provision of personnel for shops.
Helena Herrerová denied that it was franchising, stressing that an exclusive network of stores aims to "improve customer service, services to small and medium-sized businesses when purchasing, servicing, after-sales service and even packaging."
The trade in Barcelona is part of a world-class pilot pioneering program that aims to solve any problems with computer devices under warranty.
The online service is connected with a technical center based in Maastricht (Netherlands).
"Then we'll be able to solve problems within 10 minutes, which may otherwise take up to two days."
"This system is designed specifically for small and medium-sized enterprises and for ordinary customers, given that large companies already have contractual technical assistance services," Helena Herrerová emphasized yesterday.
Sabadell is betting to "steal" a million clients to its competitors within three years
More optimistic than other bosses, delegated Banco Sabadell board member Jaime Guardiola believes 2011 will be challenging for the bank, rejecting a new liquidity crisis and predicting an end of a passive war.
Nevertheless, it expects to reduce interest rates, which they will try to compensate by discounting bank fees and loan prices.
Sabadell expects him to acquire a million private clients within three years.
Sabadell has found a way to take advantage of the next three years and power to survive in difficult economic conditions.
The bank will launch a new strategic plan in 2011, effective for three years.
The aim is, in addition to survival without the need to turn to public assistance in FROB, to gain a share of the market by capturing a million new private clients - 700,000, respectively, if deducted by those who possibly leave the bank - because until now until now even the institute pampered small and medium-sided entrepreneurs and large enterprises rather than small clients.
This year, the bank has already acquired 250,000 private clients and 50,000 businesses.
Its market share is 3.5% when it comes to private clients, compared to more than 10% for corporate customers.
Another of the basic ideas of the 2011-2013 plan is a commitment to productivity, or sobriety, in order to reduce the cost, the challenges of which will be demonstrated by the bank when it presents the results for the period.
At this time, few banking institutions dare to set goals and publish them, as the financial crisis has changed all the plans of world banking.
Sabadell will close its Optima program this year, set four years ago, although it was originally for three years, but the situation in the markets required an extension for the next period.
Clients who walk away from savers will be their main target group.
At an APIE meeting, Guardiola insisted that 2011 would be "heavy and very important."
In fact, he believes that the way out of the crisis in Spain "will be lengthy."
In addition, in two or three years it will be "very difficult to create employment," which is fundamental to the survival of the banking sector, he added.
And although he believes that a passive war - which cuts margins - is receding, the price of surcharges for the risk that banks and savings banks pay for their issuance to receive liquidity are already shiftinging to credit prices, thus offsetting margin reductions.
"The topic of liquidity and financing is structural and will represent a coercive factor on loans."
“We haven’t closed the tap yet, but the differentials are very high,” Guardiola said.
Banesto has already issued its network on Thursday orders to reduce long-term loans and increase differential prices - see CincoDías from November 22 and 27.
He also acknowledged that the tendency in banking "now is cutting commissions," but there's a "competitive framework."
Sabadell’s strategic plan is based on organic growth, but, as usual, the group does not rule out corporative operations.
Not in vain does he assure that in Spain "they will be demanded by larger sizes."
"Next year there won't be a stone on the stone," he assured.
It certifies that the needs of capital are "much diminished" and does not preclude that some Spanish institutions will enter among the red numbers.
And it points out that FROB can replace the collection of funds in the market ahead of the new capital measure.
After assurances that Sabadell may survive a temporary close-up of markets, it continues to pose a serious problem about what some bankers are predicting as a new liquidity crisis.
It notes that it cannot be issued, but unlike what was in June, short-term financing can be obtained using repo rates, which mitigates the crisis.
CECA eliminates the next wave by merging savings banks
CECA's chief executive denied yesterday that another wave of mergers of savings banks was to arise, and once again insisted on the speed at which the sector's reorganisation is being carried out.
When he kicked off the process of bringing together the Spanish savings banks, there were several institutions that assured that they would not participate in this first phase and wait for the second round.
However, José Antonio Olavarrieta, director-general of the Spanish Confederacy (CECA), ruled out yesterday that a new wave of such operations should be created.
"I believe that most mergers have already been carried out, incorporation of Cajasol into the Banca Cívica group cannot be regarded as a second round, what is happening is conventions."
"Most of them are completely implemented; it is possible that instead of 17 institutes we will go to 13 or 14, but all major processes are already done and under the protection of the Spanish National Bank, which cooperated very well in this matter," the director noted.
In his statements at the ADEA (Association of Aragon Leaders and Executive Officials), Olavarrieta recounted that the process of concentration takes place "at the highest possible speed and we need to be very satisfied, as is shown in the installments, which, not now, appeared in business plans with the Spanish National Bank," he stressed.
"Going from 45 savings banks with an averange amount of assets of 28,000 million to £175,500 million is a process that does not have comparisons in history, reducing the number of institutions by 62% to make these larger and thus in accordance with the Basel Treaty," he underlined.
Olavarrieta acknowledged that "there are a number of unhappy views on the slowness of the process" that show "a lack of seriousness and understanding" because "what the Spanish National Bank is doing is a reminder that we have to be on deadline before the end of the year."
CECA Director-General Jose Antonio Olavarrieta yesterday assured that Spain has the strictest financial system across Europe and "probably" in the world, and has attributed "a lack of knowledge" to those who dispute it.
The director noted that in the current favorable economic situation "we can be calm" about financial institutions and savings banks as well as a system that has handled a "very important" process of concentration and which continues "at the highest possible speed."
He also recalled that Spanish banking came out very well from the stress tests carried out by the European Central Bank in July.
Zapatero and the red German line
Berlin decided to keep the euro, but set conditions for the whole Union.
Tensions in the markets provoked a dual government response.
On the one hand, José Luis Zapatero proposed new adaptation measures, from tax cuts for small and medium-sized enterprises, through the regulation of private intermediation agencies or the abolition of 426 euros for the unemployed without benefits, to the privatization of 49% of AENA (Spanish airports and air traffic control), the administrator of Barayas and El Prat airports and 30% of the state lottery.
Vice-President Elena Salgad, with the support of former Prime Minister Felipe González, on the other hand, has joined the criticism of Chancellor Angela Merkel because of her responsibility for unleashing the current crisis.
How to interpret these movements?
Let it be said in advance that Merkel's position is not a mistake in her communication policy.
On the contrary, it is another point in the complex process of redefining the euro.
And here Germany has a radically new vision of what its future is supposed to be, alluding to the idea of observancing the old traditions held by a large proportion of other members.
In the face of European resistance, there is growing speculation in Germany about three possible paths from the crisis.
The first represents a strong eurozone, of which Germany, but also Austria and the Netherlands, for example, would be a part, while southern countries would leave the currency zone.
The second is a return to the mark - although it may now appear like a sci-fi, this version has widespread public support, approaching 50% of the population.
According to Germany's proposals, the third option is to redesign the euro with greater macro-economic surveillance, budgetary rigour, fines for guilty states, possible postponement of payments for some of them, and with the extension of the possible costs of regulation to the private sector.
The German position is clear and has been defined for some time, so there is no room for curvators now.
At first, when both the International Monetary Fund and the European Commission incited deficits in 2009, the German government pulled a constitutional reform out of its sleeve to prevent them from being prevented by 2016.
This was the first sign of discrepancy between what Germany considered to be correct (fiscal rigor plans and the stability of public finances), and what its European partners, including France, practiced.
The second was shown by this Central European country, when Merkel was painfully stretching the decision to save Greece.
It was already subject to sharp criticism back then, but the truth is that the political consensus in Germany and public opinion, which has also been published, were strongly opposed to saving Greece.
Finally, in the last days of October, Merkel has reappeared with a clear opinion when she demanded to reform the conventions of the European Union in order to allow the rescue of a member state, and at the same time that private creditors are obliged to bear part of the costs.
This opinion was interpreted as anti-European because it accelerated the Irish crisis and everything else.
However, its proposal, although in somewhat mitigated form, has been approved, and over the next few months we should monitor the reform process of the Union, which should allow these measures from 2013.
Germany has established a red line, which it does not intend to cross.
And he tells us all that, although measures must now be taken, with which he seems to disagree too much, from this date the old functioning of the euro will be over.
2013 will represent the birth of a new currency zone, in which states infringing rules will be severely sanctioned and may be forced to report deferrals, and the private sector will also have to bear some of the costs.
Germany is suggesting that we have to take a strict path of growth with an increase in savings and investment, moderate consumption, improved productivity and competitiveness, and capital reserves.
They simply do not intend to tolerate another option based on receivables and building, with huge gaps in the balance of payments that have reigned in Spain’s growth boom years.
The offer is clear.
And we'd make a mistake if we didn't understand.
It is not just about restoring the public deficit to 3% in 2013.
What they're asking us to do is much tougher.
And the worst part is that under these conditions it could happen to us that we are wrong about ourselves.
I hope these current turbulences will be replaced by more calm.
And that we'll take advantage of the opportunity they're giving us by spring 2013.
It seems that Prime Minister Zapatero also understood this, and his double statements (the reforms promised after the meeting with the big enterprises of last Saturday and yesterday's announcement of the new measures) indicates that, through critics to Merkel's address, it has opened the way for the idea that things need to be put right.
But the red line that Germany has set is here, and it doesn’t seem to want to move it.
And from the possibilities that can be considered, leaving the eurozone full of not very serious countries cannot be excluded.
Well, Germany will always be able to tell us that it has warned us.
Sport and circumnavigating the rules
The tricks shown by Alonso and Ramos in the match with Ajax show wilt not the intent to escape punishment.
The alleged suspension of two Real Madrid players (Xabi Alonso and Sergio Ramos) in the Champions League match against Ajax on November 23 caused a sea of ink to be listed about whether or not this was unsportsmanlike behaviour and whether UEFA should not punish both footballers.
A few minutes before the end of the match (which their team had clearly won), one by one, they deliberately wasted time when a foul was supposed to be played to provoke a judge, and he showed them a second yellow card.
In this way, both players meet the prescribed penalty in the next match against Auxerre - with Real Madrid ending up in their group in the lead - and will be able, deprived of cards, to continue to the next stage.
In the end, UEFA's disciplinary committee gave coach Jose Mourinho a two-match sentence and the guilty players were given a monetary penalty.
Provoking a yellow card under similar conditions is not a new practice, but it is more or less common in football.
It is so widespread that it is part of the ethos of football, i.e. rules, which are unwritten, but are monitored.
Part of the discussion in this case was due to the fact that its participant was the Real Madrid team (which boasts nobleness and honor in its anthem, and which has already fined its players for similar actions), and it seems also thanks to the dramatization led by Mourinho, which thus adds another episode to its long history as a comedian and provocater.
In any case, the debate revolves around whether this kind of conduct, which is not explicitly prohibited by the rules, is contrary to the principles of sport, i.e. whether there is a circumnavigation of the law in these deeds; these are indeed actions that respect sports rules, the meaning of which, however, seems to try to circumvent.
It deals with what type of damage such behavior causes a sporty spirit.
One of the requirements of sports behavior is the physical inviolability of opponents, which has not been violated in these cases, so there has not been a violent game.
Another of these requirements, characterizing a clean game, is to avoid players from having an advantage over the opponent's team due to their offense - which has not happened here either.
Thus, paradoxical at the action of Real Madrid players is the fact that what we normally characterize as deceit or circumvention of the law, there has been no hidden violation of the rules in order to avoid sanctions, but this behavior should have been as evident as possible in order to receive the punishment.
They waited and were happy to receive the punishment.
Another question, however, is what advantage the culprits of their deliberate offense will have.
Here it is necessary to state that the opponent does not get into a disadvantaged position, and that there is no benefit in the current match, because the obvious consequence is that the team itself will have fewer players.
And even this is not a useful strategy for the next match, because the club will not be able to count on these players.
The advantage obviously lies in the fact that the players will start the next phase of the Champions League without cards that could jeopardize their participation in a future match.
However, it is an advantage from a long-term perspective and with a certain degree of uncertainty, i.e. at first glance the team can be eliminated, and in addition to this action, the players will be punished, and move to the second round of cards, in which one card less than in the first round is enough to be eliminated.
So how the rounds will continue, it will not be entirely clear whether this is an advantage.
In short, these negotiations do not go against the sporting spirit, which includes making strategic decisions that make it possible to get maximum benefit within the rules.
in the case we are dealing with here, players did not have the aim of avoiding punishment (which would be a clear case of circumventing the rules), but rather chose when to submit to it.
They made a strategic decision without avoiding punishment or running away from it.
Although the line between wit and circumvention of the rules is not entirely clear, I believe it was unequivocally a wit in this case.
It seems to me that controversy arises for two reasons: because it was two self-exclusions and because of the way in which the transmission of instructions was carried out to provoke them from management.
Damaging sporting principles with these events is not as ethical as aesthetic.
The fight of offers for Christmas parties in Barcelona
The calendar's dictation will concentrate an avalanche of bookings for December 16 and 17 this year.
There is an increasing number of celebrations paid for by employees themselves, who want to spend between 20 and 30 euros.
Like Turkish honey or lotteries, with the arrival of December again comes pre-Christmas parties, which still act as an oxygen bomb for restaurants and leisure facilities in Barcelona, but thanks to the crisis have resulted in a fight to get clients by lowering prices: almost everyone wants to spend 20 to 30 euros for food, agree the interviewed entrepreneurs and associations from this sector.
In order to complete the complication of the numbers, the calendar this year (when Christmas Day landed on Friday) of the application for a week from 13 December, creating an avalanche of bookings for days at peak times (16th and 17.), while in the previous week there was almost no traffic.
As has been the case since 2008, when the skinny years began, there have been many firms that have cancelled collective parties for budgetary reasons.
In order not to abandon traditions, many employees pay to spend at parties out of their own pocket.
Regardless of the situation, many are demanding prices at the limit of tolerability, according to the president of the restaurant association Gremi de Restauració de Barcelona, Gaieta Farras.
At this time, a representative of the patronage of the restaurant does not dare to predict what the celebrations will be like this month.
"People will change their minds before they make a reservation."
"They want to come out, but when it comes to price topics, they're cautious," he adds.
Stores will go well to those "who offer quality and good service at a favorable price," he says, recalling that thanks to the crisis, customers tend to go down and most customers now want to spend around 25 euros.
The survey at various facilities shows that the biggest onslaught of booking requests is for the evening of December 17.
Soteras Group is reporting a real boom in bookings for this Friday, with the most demands being for spending up to 30 euros, which has been adapted by the menus offered, the total prices of which are from 25 to 30 euros.
In another important company, Andilana, comprising 16 facilities in the city (Hostal de la Rita, Market, La Fonda and others), brought the peculiarity of this year's calendar to more early bookings since the beginning of November.
"We've done both the possible and the impossible to keep prices from last year, and our menus, which everyone should choose from, range from 19.95 to 30 euros," they say.
In the AN Group, which includes El Mussol, Attic, QuQu and a total of 23 businesses, they remain optimistic after seeing an increase in profits since the second half and have relatively solid prospects for Christmas sales.
The company's sources stress that December 18 will also be a busy day as pre-Christmas celebrations will be held among friends.
to their six group menus they added a Christmas menu, with a budget of 30 euros or more, because compared to last year, they experienced a tendency to spend guests 10% more in exchange for other items such as cava.
They have also noticed that there are already companies that are inviting their employees again, and it seems that the size of the groups is slightly larger.
Fecasarm's vice-president (which includes institutions such as the Association of Bars, Restaurants and Leisure Centres in Barcelona) Enric Goma believes he is beginning to see the end of the tunnel, claiming that the number of bookings has "revived," although clients "contract to the maximum."
This impression did not give way even during the Christmas celebrations, whoever pays, he points out.
Pakistan's nuclear program spawned great unrest in U.S
Islamabad produces atomic weapons at a "faster pace than any other country."
WikiLeaks documents confirm links between Taliban and Pakistan.
The contents of the correspondence between the U.S. Embassy in Pakistan and the U.S. Secretary of State, published by WikiLeaks, confirm the extraordinary unrest caused in Washington by the Pakistan nuclear program.
In addition to clarifying and significant details, which they offer regarding the content of the communication and ideas of representatives of North American diplomacy in Islamabad, these documents reveal that cooperation between the two states in terms of nuclear security, instead of increasing, "is in retreat" and that this unstable Asian country produces "more nuclear weapons than any other state."
The peculiarity of Pakistan's nuclear program - that its weapons are not assembled, but stored after works, in order to make their destruction difficult in the event of an attack - represents one of Washington's main reasons of vigilancy.
This is calculated by Anne W. Patterson's ambassador to Pakistan in a report before a visit by then-national security adviser James Jones: "The proximity of some nuclear facilities to territory under Taliban control, the alleged dispersal of nuclear materials and the vulnerability of the transportation of atomic materials."
An increased number of people associated with nuclear arsenals and guided missile program is no longer observed only by the U.S., but also by Russia, as spreading and presenting a reason to be alert.
“Pakistan has had to hire personnel with strict religious beliefs to protect nuclear facilities; due to the decline in the level of education and culture, extremist organizations have more opportunities to recruit people who will work in nuclear programs or guided missile programs,” said Yuri Korolev of the Russian Foreign Ministry at a meeting with his U.S. counterparts.
Julian Assange's biggest news of the documentation is the ever-increasing cooperation between the U.S. and Pakistan on nuclear proliferation issues and the increased pace of Pakistan's production of such weapons.
A dispatch issued by the embassy in May that qualifies communication with Pakistan as "frustrating" argues: "Transparency often does not exist; aid offers remain unresponsive or are rejected."
In another report, Peter Lavoy, National Intelligence Officer for South Asia, notes that "despite an economic disaster, Pakistan produces nuclear weapons as quickly as no other state in the world."
Ambassador Petterson also asks Washington to "very well-clarified" during a visit by the army's chief General Ashfaq Kayani that "the Pakistani military must put an end to tacit support for terrorist groups."
The Hunt for WikiLeaks Boss
Obama appoints a high-ranking worker responsible for combating terrorism in order to avoid new information leaks.
Interpol issues a "red challenge" in order to trace and determine where Assange might be.
Everything about WikiLeaks and its founder Julian Assange is a complicated labyrinth, where it's hard to be sure of facts.
Interpol yesterday announced an "extraordinary challenge," the so-called red notice, to "locking up and tracking Assange's movement."
However, this is not a classic detention order.
In addition, this challenge is not new, but dates back to November 20.
And it has nothing to do with the latest leak of more than a quarter of a million diplomatic documents, but with allegations of sexual abuse that the Australian is facing in Sweden.
In addition, WikiLeaks lawyer Mark Stephens assures that "the police know where and how to get in touch with Mr Assange."
It was November 20, when an international police corps based in Lyon, France, issued this order at the request of Sweden, but did not publish it until yesterday, when it received permission from Swedish authorities.
In this situation, 188 Interpol countries have instructions to identify or locate Assange "in order to impose a temporary link to him and extradite him."
Although Interpol cannot order the detention of a red-call, many Member States, as the organisation has explained, consider the so-called red notice to enable arrest to be carried out - this must be ensured by the national police component - especially when states have a two-sided detaination agreement, as is the case in Sweden and England, where Assange is likely to be located.
The move scandalized Assenge's attorneys, who yesterday published a lyring where they declare that WikiLeaks' thirty-nine-year-old founder has shown a willingness to answer questions from Swedish authorities using videoconferencing.
They also recall that Assenge requested and subsequently received permission to leave Sweden away from the prosecutor of his case, which they compared with the legendary Lavrenty Pavlovich Beriya, the head of Stalin's security.
"This is a hunt and not a legal persecution," the letter states.
While the whole world is looking for him - and from Australia his mother defends his son's innocence - Assange is still in an unfamiliar place, but he is not silent.
On Monday he gave an interrogation via Skype from an unfamiliar location to the editor of Time magazine - in that interview he demanded the resignation of U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton.
“If it turns out that she was responsible for ordering U.S. diplomats to carry out espionage at the U.N. to violate international conventions that the United States has signed, she should step down,” he said.
His view was dismissed yesterday by White House press secretary Robert Gibbs, who qualified the demise requirement as "ridiculous and absurd."
"I'm not quite sure why we should care about the opinion of the individ who runs the website," he told CNN.
Obama himself has commissioned a high-ranking worker responsible for counter-terrorism, Russell Traverse, to prevent further leaks of documents.
President Barack Obama's intention to stop addinginging to Assange's importance contrasts with the views proclaimed by Conservative politicians and commentators these days.
Peter King, a member of the Congress, who will chair the National Security Committee in the Lower Chamber from January, demanded the State Department to explore the possibility of including WikiLeaks among terrorist organizations "in order to intervene and prosecute anyone who would contribute to them financially or otherwise provide assistance."
Tom Flanagan, an adviser to Canadian Prime Minister Stephen Harper, said Assange should "be killed," which he subsequently had to recall.
"Obama should put out a reward or use an unmanned plane or something like that."
Whoever didn't apologise was Mike Huckabee, a Baptist priest, former Arkansas governor and a possible presidential candidate.
“Whoever is in the government, whoever is responsible for leaking information has committed treason, and any punishment other than execution would be too mild
Puigcercós seeks peace in ERC by "blinking" at Caroda supporters
The Republican leader will allow the party a secret vote to remain.
Huguet proposes a more plural executive and the end of the meeting system.
Joan Puigcercós has apparently found a recipe to fight the already multiple internal revolt in the ranks of the ERC (Left Republican Party of Catalonia), fruitless due to the loss of 11 MPs and halved votes.
The party's chairman is aware that his gesture regarding the resignation seems inadequate to the supporters of former leader Josep Lluís Caroda-Rovira, and he also knows that the influence of this sector is now as weakened as its leader himself, and that he absolutely can't cause him as much concern as he had with other extinct currents.
That's why the leader of the Left will try to winge new critics by blinking, which will help him defend himself into municipal elections.
Puigcercós first announced yesterday that more than 200 members of the national committee will be able to secretly speak out about his remain in office.
By means of ballots, not by raising your hand.
If Caroda's supporters want to deliver them to the president's penalty vote, they will be able to do so without fear of retaliation.
In fact, the boss wants to avoid the split and subjugation of his person.
in several interviews, he argued that he would not consider himself confirmed with half of the vote and one extra, but added, without specifying the percentages, that there would also be not a good outcome "after Bulgaria."
Supporters of Caroda are demanding more from the chairman than making his office available - which they see as a gesture that has no weight, as Puigcercós controls the largest body at the conventions.
But given that they don't have a sufficient majority to force demises, they will try to remind the executive of the plan from now until May.
Because of this, they expect to participate in the drafting of a document on strategic reorientation, on which the leadership will give a vote to the National Council on the 18th day of the month.
Their aim is to support Puigcercós in exchange for promising to move the congress as early as autumn 2011.
The ERC's leader declared that "multilance" damages stability, and that its goal is that once and for all, the organization is able to launder its dirty laundry behind closed doors.
In this context, it seems to be a symptomatic article that current adviser Josep Huguet signed on his blog.
After Puigcercós' ideologue took into account this innovation adviser as a done deal that the leader would be confirmed by "exchanging for a few goals."
And he appointed five of them, two of which stand out.
The first is "recomposing" the executive so that it represents at least 90% of the members, which also means to include new faces, who are from a sector inclined by Carod.
The second goal can be great news.
Huguet proposes that the ERC should completely put an end to the meeting as a mechanism of operation.
And so he fights to reform the statutes in order to hold congresses through delegates selected by voting.
Ending the model of the meeting system, an exception in Spain's system of political parties, is the intended goal of Puigcercós's team, which has fought in this direction already at the last two conventions.
However, a convulsive assembly of 2008 decided that meeting must be judged at a special congress on the reforms of statutes.
This matter is still pending, as the situation within the party has discouraged the resolution of the matter.
Now, without lurking critical sectors, it might be a good time to deal with this matter again and try to close it once and for all.
23 murdered for the month of November; 390 for 2010
According to MILENIO, Mexico recorded a decline of 4 cases compared to 2010.
During November, 23 people were killed in Mexico, four fewer than in the previous month, according to MILENIO.
Among the murders are the cases of three women and the discovery of a pair of separate bodies in the Valley of Mexico.
In the penultimate month of the year, there are a total of 390 killings in Mexico, probably linked to organized crime.
Among these cases is the discovery of a corpse in San Diego de los Padres Cuexcontitlán, in Toluca County.
The victim suffered at least three gunshot wounds, a wound from grace to the head, and had her hands and feet handcuffed with rope.
The next day, a body was found in a suitcase that had been stored in the vehicle's luggage compartment above Mixtecas Street, in the colony of Santa Cruz Acatlán, in Naucalpan County.
Early on Thursday, November 4, co-operatives discovered a body bearing signs of torture and at least three deadly shots into the chest near the Tenango-Ixtapan de la Sal highway in the section of Villa Guerrero county.
Next to the body was a message signed probably by the criminal organization La Familia Michoacana.
A day later, a fifty-year-old man was murdered in Nezahualcoyotl with eight shots, after coming out of his house and wanting to start collecting garbage.
On the 7th of November, two 30-year-old casualties were killed by a shot in the 30-year-old colony of San Pedro Xalaxtoc, in Ecatepec County.
In Chalco, on a secluded road in the colony of El Naranjo, the corpse of a man shooted into his forehead was found, and under the body was found a casing from a weapon of caliber 25.
in Ecatepec, parts of the human body were found in the sewage canal in the Playa Golondrina colony.
The emergency services carried out a thorough search for other parts of him, but did not find them.
Residents of Tepotzotlán reported finding the body with their hands tied to the back and wrapped their heads at Pena Colorado's site.
The man had a blow from a firearm on the neck.
in the middle of the month, the murder of two men who were wrapped in bedding was announced and left in the luggage compartment of the vehicle in Villas de la Hacienda in Atizapán de Zaragoza district, the victims were also found with death threats.
A day later she was found in the colony of Valle de los Reyes in Los Reyes La Paz district a cardboard box that contained a man's head.
in a nearby colony was found the rest of the body, wrapped in a black plastic bag, with a message from El Incorregible.
On the 17th, two dead women were found wrapped in bed linen in Zoquiapan in Ixtapaluca district, and the next morning in Huixquilucan, a slain man wrapped in a yellow-blue sheet that was preliminarily, according to a report the killers left near his body, was identified as Mario Ríos.
In Ixtapaluca, a woman with no signs of life with two wounds to the neckline was found in the settlement of San Francisco Acuautla.
On the skin of a woman was discovered a tattoo of the sacred figure of Santa Muerte (literally "Holy Death") and also necklaces with her likeness.
At the end of the month, in the village of Zinacantepec, the body of a man with at least three gunshot wounds and a message signed by a criminal group was found in the village of Zinacantepec.
Two days later, the bodies of two men who had their heads wrapped in tape were found, and a message was also found, in the village of Puente de Piedra, in Santiago Tepatlaxco.
For the victims, the report was probably written by one of the criminal organizations, which said: "This will happen to those who do not adapt."
in Ecatepec, two bodies were found, which bore signs of torture and had their limbers tied, inside an abandoned taxis behind the Center of Justice in the San Agustín colony.
On the last day of the month, the remains of two men were found in Los Reyes La Paz and Nezahualcóyotl counties.
The first of them was beheaded and found in Cuchilla Ancón colony and was wrapped in black plastic bags, tied with tape and kept a message; while the second was tortured and was found in a plastic container in the streets of Colonia Juárez.
Along with him, the agents also found a ticket with a message.
China adheres to its goal of reducing greenhouse gases
The country's representative at the U.N. climate change conference COP16 assured that its country had made extraordinary efforts to reduce its carbon footprint, specifying it insisted on its promise to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 40 to 45% by 2020.
China will keep its promise to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 40 to 45% in 2020, Su Wei, the country's representative at the U.N
In his first public appearance at the 16th Conference of the Parties to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change, Su Wei assured that his country had made immense efforts to reduce the carbon footprint and transform its industry.
He said his country has already invested $245 million over the past four years in an effort to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.
But he made it clear that China will not change its goals and will continue to work together with the international community.
He referred that China's strategy on climate change issues is divided into the following four items: dialogue, capacity-building, institution-building and support for pilot programmes.
At all of these points, they have already made progress, which includes last year's approval of a new energy law, which includes proposed targets to reduce greenhouse gas emissions, he explained.
He further emphasized that in China, federal authorities have cooperated coordinated with provincial and local authorities to achieve their energy-saving, energy efficiency and emission reduction targets.
Furthermore, as he said, various several-year-old plans have already been implemented or have already been completed, which are financed by both the Chinese government and the governments of other states.
For example, it is, according to him, the Efficience Energy Recovery Programme that has been introduced over the past five years and which has received investments from the United Nations, the government and private initiatives.
There is also a provincial project of climate change, as well as a project to exchange fluorescent lamps for energy-saving bulbs and a project of mitigation and adaptation, he specified.
At the same table, Martin Krol, the country's expert adviser on climate change, assured that China's efforts were greater than widely thought.
For example, he recounted, greenhouse gas emissions per capita represent 6 tons in China, while in the United States it is 100 tons; China is responsible for 9% of its historic share of emissions.
It has also made unprecedented efforts in terms of energy use - for example, increasing energy efficiency by 20% over the past five years and expects it to make further progress by 2020, he added.
Expulsion Espina: César Nava
The national chairman of the PAN (Partido Acción Nacional) said that the former chairman of this party is entitled to claim with the National Riot Commission of his law.
César Nava Vázquez, the national chairman of the PAN party, assured that the exclusion of Manuel Espina Barrientos had been enacted, and that the former chairman of that party is entitled to turn to the National Riot Commission and demand his right.
In an interview taken before an objection filed by Gabin governor Cué Monteagud, he said Espino's expulsion was, in any case, a decision taken at the local level.
"This is an autonomous decision of the Riot Commission in Sonora."
"Mr. Espino has the right, in his case, to appeal to the National Council's Ribal Commission in order to be a decision made by a competent body," he added.
He further explained that now is not the time to think about 2012, and that she has never been at the negotiating table about the alliance's presidential candidacy.
Nava Vázquez expressed his belief that PAN will come out of the internal elections held on December 4 and 5, in which his successor will be elected, strengthened.
“This (the alliance in 2012) is a theme that has never been at the negotiating table, at least not for now, it was an intention for a local election that was successful and fulfilled its purpose.”
The PAN chairman said that 71 years of dialogue stood behind his party, and therefore "we are not afraid of internal competition, on the contrary, we benefit from it and strengthen us," he added.
He said that the committee can, without question, elect the best chairman or chairwoman that PAN deserves for the next three years, so he will hand over the relay to "whom the committee will designate."
"So I am absolutely calm and relaxed, to any of the five I will proudly hand over the office of party chairman."
He predicted that the rise seen by the party in the last elections of July will be reflected in future competitions, although "there is still no time to think about 2012."
"It is now necessary to accompany Governor Gabin Cué in this task."
In this context, he stressed that these three governments, rooted in alliances in Oaxaca, Pueble and Sinaloe, will represent a flag and be the subject of observations throughout the country, as "many anticipations and desires are put into them, and I am sure Gabino will live up to these expectations".
When we asked him about the critics of President Felipe Calderóła (Partido Revolucionario Institucional), because of his four-year speeches, which he governs, César Nava urged PRI members to think about the six-year terms of former presidents Ernesto Zedilla and Carlos Salinas de Gortari.
“If the PRIs believe that Zedillo and Salinas governments were better than Fox and Claderón’s rule, let them start defending them, let them come out of their shell.”
He said that the head of the federal executive had the right to express himself as he had done during a speech at the National Auditorum, as there is no gag or censorship in this country.
Obama promotes law on Twitter to support undocumented students
“If you think the approval of the Dream Act is correct, let your senators know and urge them to pass this bill,” he tweeted.
President Barack Obama today, by posting on his personal Twitter social media account, joined a national mobilization in support of the Dream Act, which should legalize students without documents.
“If you think the approval of the Dream Act is correct, let your senators know, and urge them to pass this bill,” Obama tweeted.
His short report draws attention to an internet link to a more comprehensive document asking the public to immediately get in touch with senators, as the vote on this bill is approaching.
“Since the vote is expected to take place soon, now is the time to let your opinion be heard.”
The report was released after 42 Republican senators threatened to block approval for any law unless an extension of the Bush-era tax cut and government funding was passed.
Such blocking, which was announced by a letter signed by 42 Republican senators, could affect voting on the Dream Act and other projects, such as ratifying a new treaty with Russia against nuclear proliferation, called START.
When he was asked about the Republican blocking of the Dream Act and other proposals, the presidential spokesman commented that he believed that once the fiscal issue was resolved, it would be possible to draw up a calendar for the rest of the projects.
"I believe there's still plenty of time left..."
"We all agree that we must resolve the issue of taxes before the end of the year..."
I believe that we will be able to reach an agreement on this matter at some point, and then we will be able to plan the order of other matters.”
Senate Majority Leader Harry Reid announced on Tuesday that he would ask for a "vote to conclude a discussion" regarding the Dream Act, so there could be a motion to legalize undocumented students from next Thursday, although more likely next week.
Senatorial approval of the Dream Act, or "U.S. Dreal," would legalize roughly 800,000 young people who made their way to the United States before the age of 16, resided in the country over the past five years and were admitted to an institution of higher education or the military.
But supporters need 60 votes to approve the proposal, which is why they need the help of several Republicans, so the organizations of its supporters are carrying out intensive agitation to ensure the approval of this bill.
Democrats, whose majority the Dream Act supports, have 58 seats.
However, at least one, Senator Ben Nelson of Nebraska, said he would vote against it, and only one Republican, Richard Lugar of Indiana, expressed his support for the bill.
That's why supporters of the Dream Act project need to get a few Republicans in case other Democrats decide to reject the proposal.
Among the Republican senators with whom supporters negotiate the most, are senators from Maine Olympia Snowe and Susan Colins, as well as outgoing Florida Senator George Lemieux, who supplemented Senator Mela Martínez's mandate.
Other Republicans who supported the Dream Act in the past are Orrin Hatch from Utah and Kay Bailey Hutchison of Texas, but they said they wouldn't support it again.
Another Republican who supported the proposal, John Mccain, has yet to comment at the moment.
Although the House of Representatives is leaning towards a different version, according to most analysts, the Dream Act will face the biggest hurdles in the Senate.
with the literature put their finger into the wound ("poner el dedo en la llaga", translated "put your finger in the wound" is similar to the Czech "hitting the nail on the head)
The second round of the cycle Pleasure from Language was a promenade, where the participants performed a literary gala performance
"I understand this with Sex in language, but... Finger to Wound? What's expected of me?" asked the writer Luisa Valenzuela, who was invited to start the second round of The Pleasure of Language, a cycle that celebrates literature written in the Spanish language, and whose question was used on this occasion as a subtitle (at another trail they devoted themselves to the tome of Sex in the language at the round table).
The finger "brings us to the narrows," said an Argentine writer to an audience composed mostly of young people gathered at the International Book Fair in Guadalajara to listen to this round-table debate, moderated by Marisol Schultz and with the participation of Valenzuel, Sergio Ramírez, Mayra Monter and Luis Garcia.
Luisa Valenzuela read the lyricism she dedicated to Gonzal Celori, and which Schultz then described as a great short story, in which Luisa acted in the first person as the protagonist, who, in search of knowing what the "brow" is like, walks from the doctor to the doctor, from whom she does not get answers, although the trace of the same story yes.
She walks away from the infectologer convinced: "patience is not my strength, maybe it'll be my wound."
Finally, after frustrating with the traumatologist who took her's breathe in the middle of the road, but explained to her that the wound is not a scientific term, it's a psychoanalyst who reveals the mystery: "the wound represents it really open, and the symbolic one is the scar."
The protagonist sighed lightly and concluded that "she only managed to learn so much about the wound that it is truly open and that it does not exist", but she became convinced that pleasures and pitfalls in the language must go hand in hand together.
Writer Valenzuela played with both.
Nicarrave Sergio Ramírez also stepped forward with an adorable narrative, which he called Praise of Invention, in which a finger in the wound is not named, but at one moment it appears.
with elements of magical realism she tells of a famous witch who played clouds, brought back people who had gone far away, or correct the honesty that a virgin had lost, this narrative, which was then reversed to be spoken by America, a great novel in which from the beginning his own chronicalists, who told him, was quoted by Bernal Díaz del Castillo, who criticizes
"Cervantes, who had invented us all (the writers), mocked us with a false testimony, which was presented in the stories as real, but in any other way it has never managed to tell the story of America with such imagination, which lacked even shame."
However, he emphasized, "they are fools and liars who have a greater imagination" and precisely where the reality and the lie, where it is no longer known, what of the story belongs to one group and to another, fictional literature reaches its peak.
He really calls it a "Cervant tradition."
"Thanks to his legacy, we have what we have."
Subsequently Mayra Monter highlighted and praised the diversity of interpretations, which the invitation of the International Book Fair with her discussion Finger to Wound allowed.
The Cuban-Portorical writer pointed her finger at a major blow to the social violence that passes through her country - and is shared by Latin America - and is the result of trade disputes and the sale of drugs.
"I think good literature, good poetry and good songs make a quiet compromise, which, of course, isn't intended," she said after stating that the reality of literature is communication and the dimensions of a compromise created between the author and the reader - and in their "chemical reaction."
In the evening, the Spanish poet Luis García Montero concluded, whose performance also emphasized "on this finger in the wound of a writer committed by a writer."
He said that in a world that lives in a hurry, also in intellectual habits and control of consciousness that overflows with dogmatisms, it is forgotten "that behind every acquiescence there is a small no, and behind every denial there is also a small yes."
“In order to be the masters of our thoughts and realities, we have to colorate: and to color ourselves many times is to insert a finger into a wound and harasse those individuals who are much more comfortable, because they think in cartoons, black-and-white, in fixed ideas, and without color, and to bother also the authorities who prefer this black-and-white world,” he said.
Romano goes for his first title
Rubén Omar Romano has three defeats in the final, he has experienced both good and bad during his career, but he has already been able to reach the decisive series on three occasions.
In Santos Laguna and Monterrey in the final, there will be two different styles characterized by their techniques, teams that have a noticeable difference in the results.
Rubén Omar Romano consists of three defeats in the finals, during his career he has experienced both good and bad, but he has been able to achieve a decisive series on three occasions, but until now he has left with an untaken, which is why this chance is extraordinary for him.
The Argentine had to be satisfied with a silver medal, stayed behind the winner and quaintly played with the same team the following final.
As a coach, he reached his first final in Apertura 2002, when he was in the hands of the reins of the club Morelia, with whom he succumbed 4-2 against Toluca overall; then in the Clausura 2003 tournament he again progressed to the decisive duel and also lost it with an overall score of 3-1, which was against the Rayados team.
Already with the club Santos Laguna in the tournament Torneo Bicentenario 2010, again against the "Devils" from Toluka, Rubén again suffered a defeat, although his team played against the "Smugglers" (another nickname of the club Deportivo Toluca) after 210 minutes of the game indecisive 2-2, after a series of penalty shots he finally took advantage of three-on-one and had two
Rubén has already lost two finals against the "Red Devils" from Toluka and one against Monterrey, his future rival, so he would like to avoid the same fate and get rid of the curse, so that he could celebrate the title for the first time, although he says that he is the most important institution for him.
"I want to come out as a winner, I don't want to break statistics, this is for the club, for the passion that I'm committed to, and of course the title would be for me too, but that's the most important," the coach commented when questioned about the numbers that speak in the final against him.
The previous final, in which he confirms, has learned a lot, "we have learned the lesson that until the referee whistles the end, we can't celebrate, we have to be ready for action, as well as the players, because last time, with two penalties, we were already ready to celebrate, and I don't think we've learned in this until the match is over, we can't celebrate."
He does not worry that this series could decide in criminal shootings, he trusts his players: "You never try to come to a series of penalty kicks, you try to finish in normal time, but sometimes there is no other option and we need to be ready for it, in the previous tournament we were ready, but unfortunately fate prepared us a bad move, perhaps because of the nerves or anxiety that we felt when the title was in our hands
Romano has in his hands the chance to stop being the other one and taste the glory brought by the titles for the first time.
The United States is changing visa rules
People will have to turn to visa Visa Services centres before an interview at the Consular Department; the costs will be lower and the waiting time will be reduced.
From January 10, 2011, the process of discussing visas at the U.S. Embassy and consulates will change.
with the new procedure, most applicants will have to turn to the Visa Applicant Services Centres (abbreviated from ASC English) prior to an interview in the Consular Department.
Personnel from the ASC will withdraw biometric information of the applicant and these will be evaluated by the Consular Department prior to the interview.
The new procedure will have certain advantages, including the following:
The total cost of processing the visa will be lower, there will no longer be a need to pay a fee to arrange an appointment, the second for the processing of the request, and another for the delivery of the document by the transport service.
The applicant pays only a single fee, which will cover all three services.
The current tariff will remain unchanged: an amount equivalent to US$140 per tourist visa, US$150 in cases of visas with applications (including visas for temporary workers) and 390 dollars per visa for traders and investors.
People wishing to renew their visa will not have to interview a consular officer, but will only contact the nearest ASC, where they will submit their documents and their fingerprints will be taken away.
Due to the fact that biometric information will be collected in the ASC, applicants who will require a second meeting with an interview in the Consulary Department will spend less time here than in the past.
Likewise, those who will apply for a visa at consulates in Ciudad Juárez, Monterrey and Nuevo Laredo will no longer pay an extra service fee of 26 dollars.
For further information on the new visa application procedure, please visit the following website http://mexico.usembassy.gov/boletines/sp101201_Visas-FAQs.html
NASA announces discovery of extraterrestrial life
The U.S. space agency convened a press conference tomorrow to present a discovery that could cause a breakthrough in search of extraterrestrial life.
The National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) of the United States will announce tomorrow, Thursday, an astrobiological discovery that could confirm the theory of the existence of extraterrestrial life.
The discovery will be presented tomorrow at 13 o'clock by experts on astrobiology, a science that deals with the study of origin, evolution, propagation and the future of life in the universe, at a press conference at NASA headquarters in Washington.
When inviting journalists, NASA assured that it would present "an discovery that will cause a turning point in the search for extraterrestrial life," prompting speculation from experts from other countries.
The discovery could confirm the theory that there are other creatures besides us, living in inhospitable conditions that have been considered uninhabitable, and which could represent the possibility that they will one day become intelligent creatures, such as humans, if the conditions in which they live improve.
According to appearances, it is a microbe that was found at the bottom of Lake Mono in the Yosemist National Park of California, where it lives in an environment that was considered too poisonous for any form of life, as the lake is rich in arsenium.
In some way, arsenic is used for its life, and its ability increases the likelihood that a similar life could exist on other planets that have no atmosphere as it is on Earth.
According to astrobiologists, if these organisms use arsene in their metabolism, it will be proved that "there is a different, second form of life, different from the one we know."
The space agency will announce the true reach of discoveries at tomorrow's press conference.
Geobiologist Pamela Conrad, who studies the possibility of life on Mars; biologist Steven Benner, one of the scientists who examine Titan (Saturn's largest moon) with the prospect that its chemical composition is similar to that on Earth.
PRD, PT, Covergencia and Patrón beget the opposition "bloc"
The reforms of the Constitutional Act on Congress lead to the arrival of "bipartism," they declare.
It was not yet the beginning of the 60th parliamentary period when an opposition bloc was created in Congress, led by members of the PRD (Partido de la Revolución Democrática), PT (Partido del Trabajo, Czech "The Labous Party"), Convergencia (Czech "Shoda" of the "Conformity")
Before the start of the session at the start of the 60th parliamentary term Felipe Manzanarez Rodríguez by Convergencia, Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza of PT, Javier Corvera Quevedo of PRD and Patrón Montalvo "threatened" against the reform of the Constitutional Act, as it decommissions them.
This reform, they repeated, begins "bapartism," because now it will be two people from the same number of parties who will control Congress.
These four deputies, together with Gloria Margarita Santos Aquilara, coordinator of the PRD party, were the only ones who voted against the Council, led by Salvador López Briton by PAN (Partido Acción, Czech "National Action Party") and Luis Cárdenas Fonseca as vice president.
Corvera Quevedo stated that these reforms institutionally constitute political marginality at the initiative of the PRI (Partido Revolucionario Institucional) and PAN.
He specified that over the next few days they would talk to representatives of PAN and PRI parties in order to modify the scheme based on political will, and if they did not have it, they would again be subjected to these two parties, thus ending the opposition coalition.
“When [the opposition coalition's" came here, that's what I'm looking at because we're eliminated from any decision, they don't even have the willingness to consider the opinion of others."
Felipe Manzanarez Rodríguez from Convergencia said they coincided with PRD, PT and Patrón and will therefore form a pluralist group.
It is impossible to allow, he continued, for Congress, after being monopolistic, to now be "duopoly."
The aspiration of reforming the law is not that there is endless bipartism in Congress.
This group begins with five lawmakers, and its members are now talking to other colleagues to get more votes, according to Felipe Manzanarez Rodríguez.
Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza of the PT explained that at the moment Congress has become the "prey" of the two - in allusion to the PAN and PRI parties.
"Previously only one side dominated it, today it is divided by two."
Patrón Montalvo demanded to respect the MP's personality, to give him a seat at the negotiating table.
"In order for the five MPs, of whom there are likely to be nine, but who are now seeing us in five, have not been left out because of vaulted agreements, each in a different faction, we have united to find a place at the negotiating table, because the problem is that we stand outside in the reform they have made and agreed, and we do not agree with this reform."
An additional 200 thousand pesos for "suppliers" are issued
The town hall in Mazatlán will pay another cheque discussed to the owner of the company supplying electromaterial.
Another cheque for a little more than 200 thousand pesos was sent through the town hall to Lorena Guadalupe Camachová Palazuelos.
Thus, the sum paid in this second half-year to the owner of the company supplying the electromaterial will climb to 1.200,000 pesos.
According to City Hall's Transparency page, this last cheque number 9258447 was dispatched on November 16 of this year, it was issued for 200.144.56 pesos, and as its subject was listed "compensation of unpaid payments".
Treasurer Luis Miguel López Moreno noted that the municipality will pay for this supplier, which was monitored by the High State Revision Office due to sales at high prices, an additional amount.
What wasn't said is the total that the town hall still owes, and how many cheques will still be sented in favor of Camah Palazuelos.
“Nothing has been bought now, it’s just that what we owe it, as well as to other suppliers,” he said in an interview.
The last cheques "reappeared" in the last quarter of the year: September 24, two on October 15, October 19, October 29 and this last on November 16.
Although this businesswoman has not yet been published in the Government Letter, as stated by the law in the case of companies audited by the High State Revision Office in order to prevent their services from being used by other administrative authorities, the treasurer assured that he did not use the services of the town hall supplier again.
In the previous period, after July 25, the Noroeste newspaper published a review of cheques issued by City Hall to its contractors, revealing that between March 2008 and July 9, 2010, during the three-year period of Mayor Jorge Abel López Sánchez, Camach Palazuelos paid more than 10 million pesos through 42 cheques.
It also carried out transactions with Jumapam, where it collected cheques for more than 3 million pesos.
The tourist places need maintenance
Tourist executives are complaining in connection with an accident involving a foreign tourist due to the poor condition of Sánchez Taboada's meeting in Mazatlán.
After the fall of a foreign tourist into a depression at Sánchez Taboada, the tourist sector in Mazatlán asks the authorities to pay attention to areas and places that are in bad shape and thus do not show the port in a good light.
Salvador Barraza Rubio, president of the Zona Dorada Association of Traders, has repeatedly demanded the impression of a destination, and the bad things that tourists find here to be replaced by new activities or routes.
He stated that the poor condition of the streets and sidewalks affects tourists who are strolling around and seeing disorder, which adversely affects nearby businesses.
“Politicians don’t know what it’s like to close a business for one day, and they have no idea of sales leaking, otherwise they wouldn’t have dealt with these matters for so long and without proper planning.”
Hotelier José Ramón Manguart Sánchez said the fall of the tourist was caused by a lack of precautionary measures and maintenance by City Hall.
"The town hall will have to take responsibility and prevent something like this from happening again will have to deal with preventive maintenance, not just repair existing defects," he said.
We would like to enjoy a clean city, because not only because we are a tourist area, maintenance should be done, after all, even as residents we deserve a clean residence, the town hall should change its strategy of maintenance of the city."
There are also other places that look neglected and pathetic, noted, such as Emblem and El Venadito.
“Today, in order for tourists to arrive, it is not just about promotion, it is necessary to offer a good product, i.e. a city abounding with the purity of its beaches and streets, where constant maintenance of tourist attractions is carried out,” he said.
“There was no attention to it, and these facts emphasize something that is well known to everyone, maintenance is lacking.
He talked about a number of places that need to be put in order in cooperation with City Hall, he said the priority must be to keep the city clean and in order to be attractive because it has tourist potential.
Raúl Llera Martínez, the sub-secretary of the Tourist Graduation, said that the rescue of tourist sites in Mazatlán is urgent because they are not used correctly and do not make a good impression.
Luckily, Mazatlán offers many historical and cultural goals and spaces, but it will be necessary to make these places more attractive first, so that we can put them on the list we have to promote this destination."
The IP is requesting results from Congress
Eduardo Dávalos, the president of the CCE, would like MPs to take great responsibility by set an example in seeking a better Sinaloa, in the various challenges facing the state.
A parliamentary period full of dignified work and no agreements between the parties and the State Executive is calling for a business sector in Mazatlan as Sinaloa struggles and faces major problems.
As of yesterday, the 60th parliamentary term, consisting of seven political forces, 19 MPs PRI, 13 from PAN, three from Partido Nueva Alianza, two from the PRD, one from PT, one from Convergencia and the last from the PVEM party, which includes a total of 40 national representatives.
The list of expectations from the new members of Congress is to be able to implement decent leadership, with commitments to society and various areas that require responsible legislative work, and that the same partisan and paternalistic patrons will not be repeated.
Eduardo Dávalos Zamora, chairman of the Coordinating Business Council (CCE), suggested that the recently elected 40 members of Congress should take great responsibility and set an example on the way to a better Sinaloa in the various challenges they face.
"We expect MPs to actually exercise their right, without agreements between parties, because they usually take measures to advance their own interests and become carnival processions of rulers and leaders of parliamentary factions."
That's why he was talking about the need to confirm the obligation to establish the necessary bridging that would move the ruling forward in order.
“We want them to do a decent job and, above all, to be committed to society and sectors, because it was the people of Sinaloa who trusted them with their voice in past elections.”
José Ramón Manguart Sánchez, president of the Tres Islas hotel association, said one of the biggest challenges for 40 national representatives will be to gather all their strength in an effort to achieve the country's economic growth, because the current economic situation is very complex.
The most important thing is that they stimulate economic growth to create incentives for investment and infrastructure, which will promote the creation of jobs in some way."
When it comes to tourism, he demanded that new lawmakers give him more weight within the state budget and support the Ministry of Tourism.
“I have learned that it is a ministry with the second lowest budget, which is disproportionate, given that it is an important economic activity, yielding valuations and investments, which, moreover, compete directly with agriculture.”
In addition, in order to issue funds for start-up tourist projects, Congress should help supply the state with certainty and assistance with the Alcohol Act, which has failed to be resolved in previous parliamentary terms.
They're looking for Three Kings
Dolls, toy cars and bicycles are, along with other toys on the children's wish list in the Coronado Valenzuel family.
It will be difficult for their parents to fulfill their expectations during the coming feast of the Three Kings, as their household consists of exactly 12 people and one ram.
However, Adelaida, Isabel, Jesús, José Alfredo, María Guadalupe, Isaías, José Juan and Miguel do not lose hope that Kaspar, Melichar and Balthazar will give them the toys and clothes they desire so much.
The family lives in a modest dwelling in the village of Colonia Salvador Allende in poor circumstances because the father has no permanent work.
"Sometimes it's hard because my husband's not lucky, but we're selling mandarines and potatoes... In the afternoon I'm coming out with "plebeians" on the street to sell on the street," said Cayetana Valenzuela Andrade, the mother of children.
Four-year-old Adelaida wants a doll or a bike, while her three-year-old sister Isabel would like a Barbie doll.
As for the ten-year-old Jose Alfred, he'd have wanted a "monster" car for some time, and for his eight-month-old brother Jesús, Cayetana would like one-year-sized clothes.
"He told me (José Alfredo) 'I'd like this monster car,' but I told him 'we'll see his son' because it was very expensive," he recalls with the sadness of Cayetan.
For older children who have just been at school, clothes and shoes would be ideal.
María Guadalupe, who is 11 years old, has a size of 12, a ten-year-old Isaías size 10, while eight-year-old Miguel wears size 8.
Fourteen-year-old Jose Juan lacks sneakers, so he'll need a five-and-a-half or six pair.
If you want to help fulfill the dreams of children from the Coronado Valenzuel family, you can take part in the "Fought with Three Kings" campaign, and bring a gift or money in cash at the Noroeste diary reception.
14 alleged drug traffickers who were arrested
Dozens of inspections were carried out in Estria, on Wednesday, as part of the liquidation of a network for the production and sale of Indian hemp.
Fourteen people have been arrested and are facing various charges.
The tours took place in Sherbrook, Orford and Stoke, mostly in places used to produce drugs.
"Approximately 750 beds of Indian hemp, four kilograms of hashish, money and equipment that were used for production were seized," Louis-Philippe Ruel, a spokesman for police in Quebec, said in a statement.
In total, eleven men and three women were arrested, both of whom the alleged bosses of this entire network, two men from Sherbrook.
Those arrested had to arrive at the Sherroboke Palace after their arrest.
They could face charges of manufacturing Indian cannabis, its possession with the aim of trafficking it, conspiracy and theft, among others.
These searches were held subsequently after a long-term investigation that lasted almost a year.
"It was the information we received from the public and that allowed us to proceed with these arrests," M. Ruel said in a statement.
This police operation was attended by 70 police officers of the regional department investigating the city of Estrie State Police of Quebec, the State Police in Sherbrook and the Sherbrook Memphrémagog Police Administration.
Illegal migration will be able to continue without problems.
The Conservative bill, which the government says should regulate the problem of ships loaded with illegal immigrants, will clearly be overwhelmed, while all three opposition parties said on Wednesday that they can't support it.
"To a significant extent, I rely on the Charter."
“I’m on the side of the party that built granite under your feet,” Liberal leader Michael Ignatieff said.
Liberals insist that the bill, as proposed, law that would allow for up to one year to detain any person coming from a ship, identified as a ship carrying illegal immigrants, would violate Canada's charter of rights and freedoms.
The head of the Quebec bloc, Gilles Duceppe, also said he could not support the bill because it was heading towards the wrong goal.
“We are against it, and it seems that all opposition parties are opposed, so we will see what the government will do.”
Criticist NPD on the issue of immigration, Olivia Chow, has dismissed critics who want her party not to be strong enough when it comes to illegal immigrants.
"Neo-Democrats agree to take action against the smugglers of illegal immigrants and that's what we consider to be the supporting part of another bill, C-35," Ms Chow said.
“We don’t support the Conservative bill because it blames refugees and puts children into prison for at least one year,” Chow said.
Immigration Secretary Jason Kenney blamed opposition parties.
"Canadians collectively tell parliamentarians that they don't tolerate this kind of illegal immigration, these penalty operations that take aim at Canada and treat it as some sycophant."
They expect Canada to take consistent measures, Secretary Kenney has responded.
The government did not declare a vote of confidence in its bill, which could lead the country to elections leading to its rejection.
One conservative told the QMI news agency that the government would call for a vote so that every MP who opposes would be held accountable for his position in front of his constituents.
A new unity does not mean unanimity
Despite notifications, about the possible creation of a permanent unit for the fight against corruption and collusion, many observers continue to demand the creation of a public commission of inquiry for the issue.
The Minister for Public Security, Robert Dutil, is trying not to fall into oblivionism: Québec intends to consult with the Department of Investigation from New York as early as January next year.
The metropolis has had a permanent anti-corruption unit since 1870.
That's why Tuesday's announcement surprised many of us.
It should be noted that during the meeting of the Permanent Council for Police Services in Quebec on October 29 last year, this measure was not discussed, even though Robert Dutil himself was present.
There was no need for a public security critim, PQ Stéphane Bergeron, to rate the announcement as "a rabbit pulled from his hat" and an "amatheric solution muttered on his knee."
Minister Dutil himself responded to those who fear that political power has too much influence on the possible future of unity.
"He'll settle for confirmation: you'll see the result when we announce."
"We are aware of this problem."
For the president of CSQ, Réjeana Parenta, nothing can replace the establishment of witness interrogation during a public hearing.
"Moreover, Mr. Charest confirms that he doesn't want to create this commission of inquiry, and people think, like you and me, that he's hiding something," he explains.
"The only meaning to achieve the creation of a commission of inquiry is the demise of Jean Charest," explains Yves Boisvert, a professor of ethics and political science at the Institute for the Education of High Public Officials.
In order to come out of this impasse, he proposed to Quebec to grant special authorization to the inspector general in order to investigate collusion and corruption.
"We have to be able to extend the mandate of the inspector general," he decided.
He also believes that Quebec could give an inspector the same powers as commissioners, to whom they allow people to testify.
The creation of a permanent working group is not the only proposal in the writings of the Charest government.
After Ottawa, Quebec would also change the Criminal Code so that it is better adapted for this type of crime.
This requirement would be based on Operation Marteau (Hammer), who informed Justice Minister Jean-Marc Fournier that the case-law in this kind of investigation is not explicit.
The Federal Minister for Public Security, Vic Toews, affirmed that his colleague from the judiciary, Rob Nicholson, is currently looking into some of the amendments associated with this file.
131 people were arrested during Operation Spring 2001, which affected groups of bikers, even though the Criminal Code has not yet been modified.
Ottawa subsequently made changes to the Gangstering Act.
Rivers under close supervision.
Several watercourses have come under strict control due to heavy rainfall that have fallen since Tuesday in several regions of the province.
There's been a landslide in Shawinigan, Mauritius on Wednesday afternoon.
Two houses were evacuated as a precaution.
In the evening there was another landslide in Saint-Lin-Laurentides, Lanaudière.
The road is bent.
From Tuesday to Wednesday evening, several regions were hit by heavy rainfall, i.e. it rained from 40 to 70 millimeters.
From 20 to 40 additional millimeters was not expected from today to Thursday evening, by regions.
Sections in the north of the river and in the Outaouais valley have been hit hardest.
A total of up to 100 millimeters could fall there.
If we add up these rainfall with melting snow accumulated in some sectors, we may be afraid of floods for some watercourses.
On Wednesday evening, the Quebec Rescue Service declared a warning about the dangers of floods for the Ouareau and Achigan rivers, in Lanaudière.
Rivers in the north, in Saint-Jérôme, in les Laurentides, and in Chêne, in Lotbinière, have become the subject of flood alert and continuous control.
In the de Lanaudière area, heavy rainfall caused flooding of some lakes in Sainte-Julienne, in Sainte-Marcelline-de-Kildare, as well as in Rawdon.
In Saint-Calixte, as a result of heavy rains, one makeshift bridge was torn down.
Two residencies had to be evacuated as a precaution.
In Outaouais the outpouring of some lakes from the shores caused the closure of some roads, especially in Mansfield-et-Pontefract.
The ambulance service has an emergency and oversees the situation in the area, said Annik Bouchard, a security adviser.
The Quebec Center for Flood Prediction issued a warning Wednesday of severe floods for several rivers, notably Maskinongé, Assomption, Ouareau, Achigan, Batiscan and Loup.
Dupont partially blanked
Quebec radio host Stéphane Dupont has suffered a partial setback before Canada's Council on Radio and Broadcasting Standards (RRTV) because of controversial statements regarding social assistance and Haitians and, as well as because of rude expression during broadcasting.
The Radio and Television Broadcasting Council considers that Mr Dupont and his CHOI 98.1 FM group had the right to express their views, but some statements contained "inaccurate information about social assistance and an inappropriate negative portrayal of Haitians of gross expression that does not correspond to broadcasts broadcast during the day".
The Council also concluded that discussions about suicide, including broadcasting the names of both people who took their lives, did not violate the rules for radio broadcasting.
The Radio and Television Broadcasting Council has received several complaints relating to the comments made during broadcasting between 2008 and 2010.
The first decision was made after a complaint from the Joint Front of the recipients of social support to Quebec.
The group complained that the amounts shown in the broadcast were inaccurate.
Moderators have to submit reliable information, not deceptive.
In terms of suicide, moderator Stéphane Dupont thought suicide was a gesture of despair.
Most of the members of the Commission concluded that the transmitted report was such that suicide could not be understood as a solution to the problems of everyday life.
"Most of the Commission considers that the contentious episodes of broadcasting "Dupont noon reports" have encountered a delicate theme in an impartial and appropriate manner."
There has been no complaint against him.
The latest feud concerned the statements that followed Haiti's earthquake.
Stéphane Dupont and Jean-René Dufort thus engaged in a media firefight that provoked many reactions.
The CHOI moderator declared that he did not send any donation to help Haitians, because we cannot know with certainty that those in need will actually receive the money.
The Commission considers that these comments were not unjustified, but that their cumulative effect creates an inappropriately negative image of Haitians.
"I've already undone my three-day postponement."
"My file has been intact for one-and-a-half years," the moderator said.
In all decisions, the Commission refuses to use gross expression in broadcasting.
In any case, CHOI-FM's radio broadcasts cooperated "beyond their responsibilities."
For the station, which will have to notify, among other things, these decisions once in the course of the main transmission within a period of three days, the consequences shall be minimal.
King du kung-fuvà of Montreal.
The inhabitants of Montreal, originally from Algiers, Nacereddine Zemmal, have an extremely strange run.
The athlete, who excels in "skung-fu "full contact"), in kickboxing and Thai boxing, enters tatami and rings around the world looking for new challenges.
"I started with martial artics like kung-fu and kickboxing 12 years ago, in Algeria, explained Zemmal, who now has been living in the capital since 2008 to study with professionals."
"In my case, I'm looking for matches kind of everywhere, because for those who don't like the game on the ground that appears in combat sports, competitions in North America are rare."
More recently, this guy, who works as a security guard and as a helper for UPS, has just delivered bad news to his rivals when he won two gold medals at the World Karate and Kickboxing Championships (WKA), from October 23-30, in Edimburg, Scotland.
"I've won the categories of full-seat kickbox and Thai boxing," he stated.
"I'm currently preparing for China for the World Cup in san shou."
"They were invited by opponents, who finished the top four at the last world championships in each category."
"I won bronze in Toronto in 2009."
"I finished eighth in Vietnam in 2005."
"In 2007 in China, I finished fifth in the tournament, while in Toronto in 2009 I finished third, he added."
"What's interesting is that I've made some progress in every competition."
"At this rate, I'll be the first this year or next year!"
"san shou" or "sanda" is a discipline coming from China, where punches of fists and feet, as well as throwing to the ground, are allowed.
Matches are traditionally played on the platform and, more rarely, in the ring.
The best-known boxer, arising from this form of combat, is an American of Vietnamese origin, Cung Le, well known in the martial arts world.
Although Nacer Zemmal is supposed to maintain only one result in his career, he will boldly confirm his participation in the "King of Sanda" supertournament, the Super Bowl in kung fu.
"This competition is the tallest and most prestigious in China, explains a sympathetic guy."
"It's kind of the equivalent for Stade Lumpinee (the biggest stadium in Thailand)."
"It's been a great experience."
Zemmal won his first match, in the semi-finals of his category (80 kg), before suffering a loss in the final.
"The guy who beat me was a real pro, he analyzed."
"There are four opponents per category (80 kg, 85 kg, 90 kg et 90 kg)."
"The semi-finals will compete in the final of their category."
"Then the winners of each category will compete, so that a single champion, the king of kung-fu, can be crowned."
450 contracted millions without public tenders
Hydro-Québec agreed 450 million by mutual agreement in contracts with companies providing engineering for the Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert hydroelectric project.
At the heart of this avalanche of more than 100 contracts, it is a consortium created from BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor, which won the most important contract, a contract for the management of the entire project totaling US$295 million without a tender.
This consortium, called Énergie gérance, was empowered in 2004 in parallel with Hydro-Québec's selection to entrust to the private sector its responsibility for managing this significant $5 billion hydroelectric project.
At the beginning of 2005 the consortium "Énergie gérance" won a lucrative contract without any announcement of a public tender.
For the Quebec side, a complaint about a consortium of businesses presents us with a serious problem of an ethical nature, which risks eliminating competition and provokes an increase in prices.
"It's a new way to make a collusion and eliminate the competition."
“This means that people don’t make ulterior agreements in secret anymore, they’re doing them openly today,” PQ MP Sylvain Simard said at a press conference yesterday.
A little earlier, in the morning, M. Simard asked this question to the National Assembly because it says it bears heavily how the public interest can be sold in this way.
In the afternoon, Nathalie Normandeau, the Minister for Natural Resources, vigorously denied that it was a collusion contract.
"I've got my own way," she thundered, claiming that PQ was "dishonest" and "lacking accuracy."
She said "it would be the slightest" that PQ would apologise.
The recipe for creating a consortium is common practice around the world.
It is usually an alliance, limited in time, which will allow two or several businesses to group their mutual expertise so that they can respond to a call for tenders for a complex project.
The Eastmain project is very complex with regard to the complexity of the works.
Construction is already underway and must be planned by 2012.
However, the treaty was granted without competition being able to act; it was trade.
It must also be acknowledged that the formula of consortia is no longer an exception in Quebec, even for relatively simple projects.
As Le Devoir revealed last week, a formula of consorties in engineering consulting is under investigation in government.
If we quickly fly over the business register of Quebec companies, it will allow us to discover more than 200 consortiums, some of which seem to have been created permanently.
This is how Dessau works in partnership with most of its main competitors; namely CIMA+, Génivar, SNC-Lavalin and Roche and Tecsult.
Each combination, and there are dozens of them, leads to one and the same legal entity, and that is Dessau.
When it comes to the Énergie gérance consortium, Secretary Normandeau highlighted in particular the fact that "la Baie James is characterized by a special dynamic of the workforce and that Hydro-Québec has been respondinging to it since the 70's."
The main patron of the Eastmain project, Réal Laporte, from the Baie James Energy Company (SEBJ), gave an explanation which would force us to wrinkle our forehead over the fact that the project management contract was concluded by mutual agreement.
"If you go to a tender and if there are few participants in the tender, your offered prizes may be higher."
"You have two options to choose from: i.e., that you take away the people who figure in the contract as participants in the competition, and that you say, "Let's make an agreement satisfactory and with a good price."
"But the main idea is that what is important is not the price, but the competence of the people," Mr. Laporte confirmed.
This contract awarded to the Énergie gérance consortium is part of a long list of treaties for more than $50,000, received by the Quebeku Party.
The contracts were concluded with Hydro-Québec or SEBJ for the Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert project.
After Verifying Le Devoir, he counted 109 contracts for consulting in the field of engineering, concluded between 2002 and March 2010 with companies or their branches, especially with laboratories conducting analyses of soil samples.
SNC-Lavalin won 22 contracts, of which Génivar won 16, Tecsult 14 and Dessau 10, without having a stake in "Énergie gérance".
All contracts have been concluded.
Together, $449 783 344, including a contract with Énergie gérance at $295 131 564.
Sylvain Simard suggested that the four firms that are part of the consortium (BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor) are "very well-known Liberal Parties of Quebec."
Secretary Normandeau leaked a report that PQ is also associated with companies that "finance" it, rather than clarifying that it was its managers.
The barometer of responsible consumption - "green" etiquette does not inspire confidence
More green, but openly skeptical.
The Quebec consumer has very little confidence in the actors of sustainable development, but also the "impartial" brands and environmental certifications, to be guided accordingly when choosing products.
Worst of all, even when we bathe in a media universe that urges us and urges us to consume more socially responsibly, this media universe laments a lack of plausible information, non-fulfillment of obligations that even qualifies as a brake for its commitment, Baromètre states responsible consumption, the first article of which was published yesterday.
Even though the consumption of so-called socially responsible products increased in 2010, Quebec residents who try to sell them still don't trust them
In fact, two-thirds of us say they don't believe in the promises of companies related to the environment, simply because green advertising by companies is figuring out a measuring tool developed by the University of Sherbrooke Responsible Consumption Observatory.
Other finding: half of consumers doubt the reliability of official certificates, attached to so-called fair products, but also about the credibility of products and brands that are referred to as ecologically responsible, Barometre said.
In the same way, the debate of politicians on the subject is accepted as serious by only 14.6% of Quebek's population.
Responsible consumption, however, seems to be paramount for stimulating this side of consumption, which is currently revived by almost a third of Quebec consumers.
Truly, a vacuum and a dubious nature of information would temper the enthusiasm of 46% of consumers.
That's the second brake identified by Baromètre, after the higher price of these products.
After all, one-thirtwened of the people of Quebekus are incredulous in relation to the reported data on products or "green" services that we are looking for to let them pass through our hands.
On the other hand, in 2010, almost half of consumers were willing to change their habits and, in particular, change the brand of one or several products, thereby responding to their beliefs.
61% of them favored purchases from merchants from their neighborhood and preferred to buy food grown in their region, according to Baromètre.
More "green" and accountable - the Responsible Consumption Index has reached 64 out of a maximum of 100 this year - Homo consumus Québeku likes to present itself as the king of recycling, which strives for the least possible impact on the environment by taking care of the protection of animals.
But their societal and ecological conscience obviously remained somewhere outside their car, according to Baromètre: in 2010, barely 28% of Quebek's residents said they were taking advantage of public transport or recommending the use of a car to drive at least two people, for the sake of nature conservation.
Walking and cycling was also supported by 39.1% of participants in this large-scale behavior survey who used this cliché.
Loan of the state for the purchase of eXcentris cinema complex
The government of Quebec, which we have applied for financial participation in the purchase of the eXcentris cinema complex by Angus et le Parallèle, agrees to participate, but only up to the amount of the loan.
The Developper company "Angus et le Parallèle," which sought a seven-million-dollar donation from the Minister of Culture to purchase the cinema complex on Saint-Laurente Boulevard and which demonstrated its mission as a lover of film art, did not get what it demanded.
The government accepts the fact that it will be included in the documentation, but on its own terms.
Rather than the expected donation, the government of Quebec is proposing a loan, the amount of which, after all, would be the original expectation, which could build the basis for a viable solution on favorable terms and with resources coming from the state.
The first action plan and the 12.8-million demand had already been rejected by Quebec last spring, but applications have already been revised for reductions.
The proposal and plan of activities was sent to Secretary St-Pierre by the President of the Society for the Development of Business in Culture (SODEC), François Macerola, which manages the eXcentris cinema project.
The final message was sent ten days ago.
But the government's counter-proposal came to SODEC on Tuesday.
Le Parallèle et Angus have until December 31 to respond.
However, it seems that the donation hypothesis from the state must really be dismissed.
Recall that the Association of Cinema Owners and Cinematic Centres in Quebec opposed direct investment by the state in order to be able to encourage the purchase of eXcentris, suggesting that the Minister of Culture is not supposed to finance a real estate project when it has not done so for other cinema halls.
But, on the contrary, several participants in the discussion demanded substantial economic support.
Daniel Langlois, the owner, was put into the premises of his cinemas within ten years of film art after he himself financed the construction of the complex, which may have been the reason for the amendment by the state's financial council.
Lack of halls for independent films is garish in Montréal, and eXcentris was able to win a permanent clientele.
Angus et le Parallèle, with Christian Yaccarini at its head, manages a campaign to release funds into the private sector, in the belief that it will spend £700 000 to push through the acquisition.
Since the spring of 2009, the only one in three film companies, Le Parallèle, continues to distribute films for eXcentris.
The aim of future acquirers is to reopen all three halls on February 27 next year and screen Xaviera Beauvois' most beautiful film Men and Gods.
But Christian Yaccarini didn't recall Le Devoir and so he's ignoring, he and his group think of proposals to shrink that are done to him.
Raised curtain to Canada as a whole...
The new diplomatic U.S. sources known through WikiLeaks reveal what the United States sees as an inferiority complex for Canadians towards the Americans, but with the same blow they reveal their sense of superiority of the latter against Canada.
U.S. diplomats are surprised that every Canadian voter cares about Canadian-U.S. relations when he goes to vote, and believes that President Obama’s visit has helped the Conservative government to adopt its budget in 2009!
Four new reports between the U.S. Embassy in Ottawa and Washington, last night to the public, also provide sensitive details to get "under each other's skin."
The September 22, 2008 communication, i.e. in the middle of an election campaign, details the point at which the United States is not part of the bets discussed by political leaders.
To the great amazement of the U.S. ambassador to Ottawa, David Wilkins.
Mr Wilkins provides several explanations for the situation.
"Canada's inferiority complex, almost fixed, conditions possible political leaders to talk about internal bets, rather than realizing that this election also applies to the United States (as in the 1988 free-trade campaign)," he writes.
“The United States, therefore,” he concluded, “a proverbial elephant in this election: pervasive, but potentially too threatening to be talked about.”
The ambassador understands this "deafening silence" all the less because the two countries maintain intense political and economic relations.
"It is logical that the ability of a candidate, a partie or, even better, a party chief to manage these important relationships should be a key factor that voters have in mind when they go to the polls."
Another expression of this sense of importance can be read in a message sent on January 22, 2009 by Terry Breese, chargé d'affaires in Ottawa à Ottawa, preparing President Barack Obama for his first visit to Ottawa.
"Your path will help ensure the preservation of the government in a vote of confidence at the beginning of February, relating to the federal budget, by which Canada publicly admits its first deficit in more than a decade, because of a recovery plan of 30 to $440bn," writes M. Breese.
There's no mentionion that the government had to adjourn a pre-Christmas session of parliament so it couldn't be defeated by a coalition, and that opposition parties were conditioning their survival on the quality of the proposed recovery plan.
Another January 2008 report signed by Ambassador Wilkins highlights an increasingly negative picture cast on the United States by dramatic series broadcast on CBC.
The ambassador is concerned about the Broadcasting of Border, Little Mosque on the Prairie, Intelligence and H2O, in which not very savvy references to Maher Arar, "No-Fly-List" can be observed, as well as abductions for violent interrogations by the CIA.
Another report that at their first bilateral meeting, Hillary Clinton asked her French partner Bernard Kouchner whether Europe was ready to accept some Guantánamo detainees.
Mr Kouchner replied yes, but on the basis of the principle of "case by case."
At the end of the meeting, it says, "he handed over some paper concerning Omara Khadr, a 15-year-old Muslim of Canadian origin, to the Secretary of State."
"The secretary accepted that she would revise the file."
Let's finally note that those affected by WikiLeaks are sweating.
Tom Flanagan, a former adviser very close to Stephen Harper, declared on CBC waves that the person responsible for this massive escape should be "destroyed."
It spread out, and Mike Huckabee heard about it in the United States.
A World Plan to Restore Lighting
The U.N. proposed a plan in Cancún yesterday to restore domestic lighting, lighting shops and industrial spaces in 100 countries in order to achieve a greenhouse-effect restriction (GES), equivalent to nearly one-third of the additional restrictions that need to be achieved to stabilise the planet's climate from now until 2020.
This ambitious plan is at the stage of large concrete projects that the United Nations wants to pursue in Cancún in order to compensate for the absence of hard-to-reach commitments to reduce on the part of industrialized countries.
According to yesterday's plan, if hot bulbs, which still monopolize 50 to 70% of the market, are replaced by flucompact bulbs or, even better, bulbs with electroluminescent diodes (DEL or "LED" in English), a substantial amount of 1.8 billion tons (Gt) GES emissions on a planet created due to illumination would break down.
Lighting is responsible for 8% of the world's emissions.
It's the equivalent of 70% emissions from a car park across the planet.
In Copenhagen last year, large countries with a significant share of emissions such as the United States, China, Europe and others, among them Canada, were involved in voluntary reduction plans that are expected to allow global GES issuances to fall from 53 Gt to 49 Gt.
But it will be necessary to deduct 5 more Gt from this balance in order to achieve the stabilization of climate heating to 2 degrees Celsius by 2020 in order to exclude that it will not get out of control.
In hot bulbs 95% of the energy is dispersed in the form of heat, and the remainder, i.e. 5%, is a product of light.
With flucompact bulbs, the energy consumption is reduced by 75% and with the new bulbs with DEL, we reduce this consumption twice more.
Flucompact bulbs last on average from 6000 to 10 000 hours.
The new DEL light bulbs, which it put into production this week - this company reportedly participated in a U.N. study - have an anticipated lifespan of 25,000 hours and consume 80% less energy than a hot bulb, giving us an idea of a possible saving with a replacement plan that would be applied in 100 countries.
All of these new bulbs can also work with rheostats and emit less heat.
They contain no mercury.
Several experts have expressed reservations about flucompact bulbs because they contain mercury and need to be sorted as hazardous waste, which is still not possible in developing countries,unless manufacturers set up collections.
However, according to the report, the main source of mercury emissions on the planet is not a flucompact bulb, but rather a thermal coal-fired power plant, which emits the many mercury needed to generate electricity wasted in hot bulbs.
In the United States alone, these headquarters circulate 25,000 tons of mercury a year.
The report also details the profits that any country would make from this international programme.
Indonesia, for example, would save a billion dollars a year to buy electricity and eight million tons of CO2, the equivalent of 3.5 thermal power plants, the construction of which would cost 2.5 billion
Côte d'Ivoire is eagerly awaiting the results of the election results.
The U.N. Secretary-General, Ban Ki-moon, requested the immediate publication of the results of Côte d'Ivoire's presidential elections after the deadlines have expired.
"The secretary-general underlines the need for an independent electoral commission to complete its work and immediately announce preliminary results, a spokesperson for the president of the U.N
"It asks all parties in Côte d'Ivoire and institutions to prove their sense of accountability and to refrain from any initiatives until the announcement of makeshift results."
Despite the pressures exerted by some foreign governments, the electoral commission has left the farthestest date - Wednesday midnight - without publishing the results, three days after Sunday's scroulation.
About forty minutes before the expiration of the deadline, the CEI president confirmed on television that the commission was "continuing to work" so that it could announce the results.
On Wednesday, calls for CEI to finally announce the name of second-round winner, the outgoing Laurent Gbagbo and opposition member Alassan Ouattara, have multiplied.
But the Gbagbo camp relies on annulation by the Constitutional Council for "Fraudulent" voting in the north, which has been administered by former New Army (FN) rebels since a failed coup in September 2002.
Pascal Affi N'Guessan, director of the Ivory Coast presidential election campaign, confirmed that the voter intimidation maneuvers, practiced by the FN (The New Army) in the north of the country, have discouraged supporters of Côte d'Ivoire from going to vote.
The election could be annulled if the Constitutional Council took into account complaints filed by Gbagbo.
However, opponents of President Gbagbo believe that the country’s supreme court is not neutral because it is run by Paul Yao N’DrÉ, a very close political ally of President Gbagbo.
Alassane Ouattara, a man from the north who denounces any connection to the former insurgents, condemned the delay in announcing the results as "unacceptable" and asked Gbagbo to respect them, but didn't go so far as to demand victory.
His allies confirm that Gbagbo is blocking the publication of the results because he knows he has suffered a loss, and that filing a complaint with the Constitutional Council after he prevented the Electoral Commission from broadcasting the results would be illegal.
The second round of the presidential election, taking place on Sunday, was seemed the coronation of the country's reunification and reconciliation process, which has been divided into two-half for years.
On Wednesday, the United Nations warned the Ivory Coast political faction that it could be held accountable for possible violence.
Laurent Gbagbo finished in the first round, on October 31, as winner with 38% votes versus 32% for Alassano Ouattara, who won public support from former President Henri Konan Bédié (25% in the first round).
The unemployment ratie remains stable in France in the third quarter.
The unemployment rate in France, according to International Laboury (BIT), remained steadily at 9.3% in metropolitan France (9.7%, including overseas areas), as shown by the provisional data with seasonal deviation correction (CVS), published by INSEE on Thursday.
The number of unemployed has held steady at 2.631 million in the metropolis.
Data from the second quarter were confirmed at 9.3% of the active population in the metropolis and 9.7% including overseas areas.
In one year, the unemployment rate increases by 0.1 points.
In a broader scope, 3.4 million people in metropolitan France do not work in the third quarter, but would like to work, regardless of whether or not they are available in two weeks to be able to work, and regardless of whether or not they are looking for a job.
The situation of current young people in the labour market has escalated with a new increase in the unemployment rate of 24.2% in metropolitan France (+ 0.9 points in three months, +0.5 points in one year) and 25%, including overseas areas.
In the third quarter, according to the International Labor Office, 637 000 young people between the ages of 15 and 24 were out of work.
In terms of the elderly, they recorded a slight increase in unemployment to 6.4% in metropolitan France (+0.2 points in three months, as well as in a year), i.e. 482 000 people from 50 years of age and 6.6%, including overseas areas.
Only populations aged 25-49 saw benefits from unemployment stability to 8.3% in metropolitan France (-0.1 points in three months, +0.1 points in one year) and 8.8% across France.
Insee no longer publishes the monthly unemployment rate since 2006, after a controversy about its calculation method.
Eurostat, the statistical agency of the European Union, in contrast, continues to produce monthly statistics and on Tuesday announced a rate of 9.8% for France (including DOMs) in October after it stood at 9.9% in September.
Alexandre Guérini is charged and taken into custody
Alexandre Guérini, 53, one of the heads of waste-processing companies and the brother of the PS Board of Bouches-du-Rhône, has been charged and imprisoned at a prison in Luynes, near Aix-en-Provence, as part of an investigation into public procurement, allegedly subtrusteously obtained.
Alexandre Guérini has been accused of abusing the company's assets, misuse of public funds and public property, withholdings, providing bribes, accepting bribes in order to influence the office and possession of ammunition, according to his attorney.
"Alexandre Guérini is combative and insists that he and the judge explain all the questions he intends to ask him, and not some delusions."
“It’s easy to pursue him for many reprehensible reasons, but it’s another thing to prove them,” his lawyer, Florence Rault, told AFP.
Mr Guérini stated through his attorneys that he had sent a brief message to the judge prior to his interrogation in order to explain everything.
One of his relatives, Philippe Rapezzi, was also charged and imprisoned Wednesday night.
According to his attorney Lucien Simon, his client has never done anything other than to ensure the functioning of the landfiles he was supposed to manage.
When it comes to the allocation of contracts or destinations of funds associated with their functioning, he never knew of anything like that, Mr Simon said.
In this matter, Daniel Pinna, the former director-general of Aubagne's agglomeration services, was charged Wednesday with misuse of public funds and plots, and left at large, under judicial overhaul with a ban on seeing, the protagonists of the whole affair.
"My client was able to give an explanation for all the facts he's being told."
"The investigating judge will take this into account and leave him at large," his lawyer, Mr. Yann Arnoux, said in a statement.
The other four people were charged and released on the night of Tuesday to Wednesday, saying they were under judicial supervision because of two of them, Deputy Attorney General Michel Raffin said.
Michel Karabadjakian, Deputy Director-General of Marseille Provence Métropole (MPM), is prosecuted for accepting bribes in order to influence the office and for passive corruption."
Two officials from Aubagne, Michèle Duval and Christophe Bringuier are charged, one for "estrangement and misuse of the company's assets," the other for "complete in the misuse of public funds."
Finally, the business associate of Alexander Guérini, Jeannie Peretti, has been charged for "concealing the misuse of the company's property."
Among those confronted with Mr. Guérini is Stéphane Brunengo-Girard, who has recently been charged in a concurrent case involving fraudulent public contracts involving Corsica.
Investigators suspect his companies of aiding money from large-scale gangstering.
The investigation is being conducted as part of several open judicial investigations against X in April 2009 following the receipt of anonymous letters relating to alleged fraud in the conclusion of public contracts, in particular on waste.
From the source of a close investigation, the search focuses in particular on the conditions for the allocation and operation of the landfire in La Ciotat, which collects garbage from Aubagne, as well as from some cities from the Municipal Community of Marseille (MPM).
Clotilde Reiss case: Elysée palace exaggerates Syria's mediation
Within more than ten months, from July 2009 to May 2010, Klotilda Reiss, a young researcher arrested in Iran over "espionage," united views in France, putting diplomacy on a combat alert and highlighting relations between Paris and Tehran.
All of this was closely monitored by diplomats from the United States.
U.S. documents, received through WikiLeaks and consulted with Le Monde reveal some of the disadvantages of this crisis.
They describe how the Elysee Palace explained its strategy to the Americans, going so far as to give them advice on how to handle such hostages in Iran (a small group of cruisers).
They show how leading French officials tried to convince Washington that the release of the young Frenchwoman was no compensation against the release of Majid Kakavand, an Iranian imprisoned in France and wanted by the U.S
Finally, they are subjecting sharp light the way in which the French presidency tried to manipulate the media to accredit the idea that the Syrian president, Bachar Al-Assad, played a central role in Klotilda Reiss' liberation.
He didn't really do anything about it.
A diplomatic telegram written in Paris on 12 August 2009 is headlined: "Iran:French comments on how to deal in the event of hostages".
An adviser from the Elysee Palace for Strategic Affairs, François Richier, accompanied by Quai d'Orsay's director for North Africa's Middle East, Patrice Paoli, speaks with U.S. diplomacy official Allegrone.
They explain the French method to him.
They advise Americans to do just as much for being transported back to the homeland of their own nationals forcibly detained in Iran.
"Make noise," they say, "consistently vigorously pronounce "in the media the words "fundamental rights of man," "innocence," "immediate deliverance."
It is necessary to bring into trouble the Iranian regime, sensitive to its image.
“Do the more they ask you to stop.”
Because attempts have been made to negotiate through discreet channels, "silence won't solve anything," the Iranians are "masters on evasive manoeuvres."
Mr Richier recalls that the arrests of foreigners are "an Iranian family tactic: taking hostages for "political extortion."
It announces that Klotilda Reiss's liberation (which occurs on August 16, the day when the French embassy gives her refuge) "will demand the payment of a large amount of money to the Iranians" (a deposit of 230 000 euros).
And he announces that the Elysee Palace anticipates hostage cases, a phenomenon that will "escalate."
The Iranians "could arrest another 18,000 French people living in Iran in the future," according to Reuters.
28 August 2009.
Klotilda Reiss left prison 12 days ago, but she still remains blocked in Iran.
Iranian Majid Kakavand, whom the United States declares to have been deported for the illegal trade of Iran-sensitive electronic material, is released in Paris on the basis of a judge's decision.
The U.S. Embassy, like many observers, inquires about this disturbing agreement.
For Washington, which pursues Iran's illegal networks to acquire material destined for a nuclear program, Majid Khakavand's expansion is bad news.
France agreed to haggling?
Liberation of the Iranian for the liberation of a young Frenchwoman?
Speaking to Kathleen Allegrone on September 4, François Richier assures that this is not the case.
The Elysee Palace, as it states, was not only "taken aback by this judge's decision," but was looking for an opportunity to oppose it.
"There have been conversations between the presidency and the Minister of Justice" (at the time, Michèle Alliot-Marie), but "the French government cannot change the court's decision," he notes.
Mr Richier also wants to demonstrate that Majid Kakavand is under "limited" surveillance by French intelligence services.
An adviser at the Elysee Palace dials a phone number, hangs up a telephone receiver, and then informs an U.S. diplomat that an Iranian woman is currently in Paris buildings managed by the Iranian embassy."
At the time of Klotilda Reiss' imprisonment in Iran, the Elysee Palace is making efforts, negotiating through communiqué and organized "leaks" to the media to convince that Syrian President Bachar Al-Assad plays a "significant" and "influential" "mediatorist."
Americans observe this activity not without irony and state that manipulation bears fruit, because several articles in the press reflect the French official version.
Telegram of 25 August 2009 evokes the media "freshness" of French officials.
On what basis is such a degree of "gratitude", expressed against M. Assad, asks the embassy in Paris.
“Despite their statements, French officials privately acknowledge that they only have a vague idea of what the Syrians have actually done.”
The document then deals with the reasons that forced the Elysée Palace to apply in this fiction.
Intensive communication "aims at validating President Sarkozy's policy of opening up to Syria," he analyzes.
The policy exercised in 2008 "was observed by many critical voices in both France and abroad and identified as premature and undeserved."
“By “exaggerating a Syrian role,” Paris is trying to “demonstrate to Syria what kind of praise it can earn when it plays a constructive role in the region.”
The goal is also to "sow a grain of discord between Syria and Iran."
The U.S. Embassy knows this from a very good source.
On August 11, François Richier confided to him: "Of course we don't know if the Syrians have done anything, whatever it is"
"But we thanked them."
"This should at least bring confusion and ambiguity among the Iranians."
Klotilda Reiss returns to France on May 16, 2010.
Nicolas Sarkozy receives it at the Elysee Palace.
In a communiqué he thanks the presidents of Brazil, Senegal and Syria for their "active role in favor of liberating our compatriot."
End of on-line games after midnight for young Koreans
South Korea's government will adopt a law called "Pecival," which will forbid adolescents from playing online games after midnight, by blocking access to them, government officials said.
The bill will be submitted to Parliament in December so that it can assert companies operating online games to block access to these games after midnight to persons under the age of 16, officials from the Ministry of Culture and the Ministry of the Family said.
"Many kids play these games all night and then have trouble learning at school and are unable to lead a normal life during the day."
“We believe that this bill is necessary to ensure their health and their right to sleep can be ensured,” Jo Rin told AFP.
On-line games will be newly accessible from 6 a.m.
The companies will have one year to prepare for the application of the law, he clarified.
The government also expects to ask companies to limit their day-to-day access time for young people whose parents request to do so, the responsible worker added.
It will also make free programs available to people who request it, to limit time spent on the Internet.
South Korea is one of the most "affected" countries, and the daily press regularly brings various facts associated with intensive internet use.
At the same time, the country is an undisputed champion in world competitions in online games.
In November, South Korea won four gold medals at the World Cyber Games, in the face of 58 other nations.
2018-2022 World Cup: Fifa establishes host countries.
Full unrest due to suspicions of corruption is established by the Fifa Executive Committee on Thursday the World Cup's host countries in 2018 and 2022, selecting a sovereignly politically-motivated, where fights for influence and lobbying risk overtaking technical criteria.
Thing is certain, the Royal Fifa competition in 2018 will be held in Europe , the traditional bastion of this discipline (4 candidates: England, Russia, Spain-Portugal, Netherlands-Belgium).
The 2022 Championship will be much more exotic, with the International Federation hoping to conquer new territories (Qatar, Australia) after its premiere on the African continent in 2010, or to continue setting into the United States or the Far East (Japan, South Korea).
The list of personalities who will participate in delegations (David Cameron, Prince William, José Luis Rodriguez Zapatero, José Socrates, Bill Clinton, The Prince of Qatar, leading government officials of Belgium and the Netherlands, Zidane, Beckham, Cruyff) gives themselves the opportunity to create an idea of the scale of economic and political betting around the world.
Negotiations behind the scenes face the risk of lasting until the last minute.
It is difficult for the rest to establish a hierarchy according to candidates.
According to the Fifa documentation evaluation report, English, Spanish-Portuguese (2018) and American (2022) contain the best arguments, as well as the documentation of their competitors.
But those reasons won't carry too much weight for the 22 members of the executive committee at an hour intended to vote.
At the beginning of the unveiling, leading to the suspension of two members of the CE/European Commission for Corruption (Reynald Temarii et Amos Adamu) may have destroyed the chances of their country, the cradle of football, which so far hosted the world championship only once in a year.
On Monday, the BBC put on the crown when it aired a documentary that "bugged on a pin" by three influential CE (European Commission?), Ricardo Teixeira, Nicolas Leoz and Issa Hayatou.
How to make Fifa dissatisfied and bring English leaders into trouble with David Cameron in the first place.
The Prime Minister hopes that he will be able to repeat the move made by his predecessor, Tony Blair, who managed to win the 2012 Olympic Games for London in 2005.
Will this intense activity be enough for England to add a new brilliance to its name at the CE (European Commission?)?
Nothing is less certain, as Spain-Portugal's pair does not lack trumps of already prepared stadiums, its adventures (the 1982 World Cup in Spain, the 1992 Olympics in Barcelona, Euro-2004 in Portugal) and their football culture.
But the grim economic and financial perspectives of both countries could give these ambitions a cold shower.
Possible retaliatory measures against the English and doubts about the solvency of Spain and Portugal only increase Russia's position, which was an outsider despite gaps in its documentation (stadiums, hotels, transport).
But Prime Minister Vladimir Poutin has decided not to relocate the championship to Curich and publicly declare "unfair competition in the preparation of the vote," in verbal allusions barely twisted on the accusations of the English press against members of the CE (European Commission).
"We're going to throw mud at them, we're going to try to compromise them," Mr Poutin said.
For 2022, they seem to be a logical favorite of the United States, with its considerable experience in 1994.
The world's highest output of the economy, the headquarters of Fifa's main sponsors, appears to have a big lead over Japan and South Korea, which, separately, have completed a beautiful project.
But these candidacies seem premature for both countries, which together underwent a "rehearsal" in 2002.
Another threat: the awarding of the 2026 World Cup to China, which Fifa fiercely desires, has rendered the Asian odds invalid for 2022.
With Qatar pulling a large ace from his sleeve in the form of futuristic stadiums, and Australia, Fifa would continue its expansion into new landscapes, but these two countries pose more risks than certainties.
Australia, like Japan and Korea, could pay for the flimsy TV fees (due to the time shift) paid by European countries, forming the driving force for Fifa.
Second mandate: clarity (certainity)
De Gaulle was really hesitant.
Mitterrand aroused the appearance that he was hesitating.
Valéry Giscard d'Estaing et Jacques Chirac did not hesitate in the slightest.
And Nicolas Sarkozy even less than them.
The Elysee Palace's mandate calls someone else.
Therefore, the current head of state will be a candidate in 2012.
But clarity is not in good agreement with the share of the secret associated with the presidency.
Therefore, it is necessary to act as if doubts are constantly persisting.
In one and the other sense.
In 2006, without any desire to believe it, Jacques Chiraco's friends demanded that a third mandate not be ruled out.
On November 15, Nicolas Sarkozy assured that he would “decide sometime in the autumn of 2011.”
As he recalls in front of members of UMP's parliament on Wednesday night at the Elysee Palace, the perspective of "two mandates" sowed a low-fevering state.
Was it an official announcement of his candidacy?
He wasn't far away.
The president only recalled that adjusting the Constitution in 2008 limited the number of five-year-old terms to two.
It was just a statement on his part, not an announcement.
This is not the first time he is commemorating a humorous tone of the presidential election in such meetings.
“At worst, or at best, you still have seven and a half years ahead of you with me,” he said in July 2009.
Rebellion in May 2010: "Don't think I'm not considering the next stage."
His previous statements will not make him a candidate more than the previous day; he will not make him a candidate less.
If they make comments, it's because of two truths.
Accelerating the path to candidacy for the PS, on the one hand.
But that's not the entrance to Ségolène Royalo's huddred, pushing Nicolas Sarkozy to convict himself.
On the other hand, this is because his candidacy in 2012 is evident that many in the PS want an arrangement into combat line-ups rather premature rather than intended.
Poor reconnaissance on the other side.
First of all, right-wing voters would prefer François Fillon's candidacy over Nicolas Sarkozy's candidacy (46% against 44%, according to BVA-L'Express).
"We have a candidate and we're not discussing the UMP issue," people from the prime minister's vicinity reacted immediately and cautiously.
“If he believes a possible defeat, he won’t run,” says Jean-Pierre Raffarin.
“He’s still going to believe he can win,” corrects Alain Juppé, balanced with clarity.
Euro: The Central European Bank is forced to continue its purchases of state titles.
The last meeting of the Central Governing Council in the year should have been an opportunity to clarify the conditions for taking you out of the crisis or, at least, an announcement of a halt to a large number of unusual monetary policy operations, which must end by the end of this year.
The crisis of the sovereign European receivables obliges, the institute for the issuance of securities will no doubt not be able to affort to be so categorical under the threat of the deterioration of the situation today already tense in the European securities markets.
In such a case, the political price would be even higher.
Yesterday, on the occasion of a press conference, French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde paid tribute to the active role played by the Central European Bank in favour of resolving the recessions of European states.
She didn't want to say whether she thought the bank could have done more, for example by increasing their sovereign bond buying program.
"I reserve my consideration for my European partners (...) ."
The evening before, in Brussels, during his interrogation of the European Parliament's economic and monetary affairs commissions, the President of the Central European Bank, Jean-Claude Trichet, did not prove more eloquent in terms of his intentions, and was satisfied that he had stated that the agenda for the purchase of state titles in the secondary market was "continuing" and that we would "see how we would decide" during a meeting held this Thursday.
The only certainty: The central European bank will monitor its basic immutable interest rate at 1%, its historically lowest level since May 2009.
In fact, the purchases of bonds from the treasury made by the Central European Bank have doubled in the last week to nearly 1.35 billion euros.
And thanks to the unreasonabless of contractors on the market, it made new purchases of Irish and Portuguese bonds yesterday.
In totall, central banks bought the Eurosystem for 67 billion euros since May 2010.
There are no doubts among anticipation contractors on the anticipatory market for the continuation of purchases (see page 29).
Economists do not expect any significant change of course.
If loan operations for more than three months have already disappeared from the arsenal of the Central European Bank, the latter bank proceeds to provide unrestricted liquid funds according to generous conditions and for a duration of one week to three months.
Economists today expect this mechanism to be extended by several months.
"I can't imagine her changing her agenda in a significant way."
"If it didn't, it would meat accepting the risk of inducing short-term new tensions," states Michael Schubert, an economist at Commerzbank.
"Progressive starting point announced during the first quarter of 2011 seems more likely," anticipates Norbert Bräms, an economist at Sal.
Oppenheim.
Norbert Bräms adds to the plan of its interventions in the public debt market, “the European Central Bank would have to maintain the purchase volume of its bonds in order to revive the dull market.”
In view of the exposures of European banks in the sovereign debt markets in the euro area, there is no choice but to maintain its presence on the market.
"It needs at all costs to replace the lack of investors in the market and act in such a way as to prevent the flare-up of paid performance by states belonging among the most fragile," a prominent bond manager testified yesterday.
Because if interest rates take off, states will worry all the more to reduce their debt, rather than stabilizing it.
It's saving the euro at that price.
Berlusconi's government hunches its back in Italy
After the beginning of the week for Italy, the worrying situation eased a bit, yesterday with the reverse flow of bond interest for 10 years.
A return to temporary calm.
At the end of a meeting with the economy minister, the region's presidents said they had heard Giulio Tremonti qualifying the situation as "unresolvable" and expecting an "unpredictable" weekend, which he then demented involved.
In any case, on Tuesday the deviation from the long German interest reached a record of 210 basis points, and the price of anti-bankruptcy (CDS) treaties flew up to 263 points, causing some upset in Rome.
In the Council of Ministers, Silvio Berlusconi noted that in Spain the deviation with German interest rates was higher than 400 basis points.
But his right-hand man, Gianni Letta, said he was "too busy" with "tumultuous market developments" and a possible contagent from Ireland.
"The good news is that Italy, the eurozone's third economy, isn't like Greece or Ireland."
"The bad news is that it's like Portugal," explains Irwin Stelzer, an economist at the Hudson Institute.
Clearly and unencrypted, its problem is the absence of growth and bland fiscal inputs, which goes hand in hand and exposes the state to the risk that it will not be able to cover its funding needs.
Brussels' latest forecasts were a cold shower for the government.
Italian growth is currently expected at 1.1% in 2011 and 1.4% in 2012... while the budget available to the Senate for its analysis is 1.3% and 2% respectively.
Under these conditions, the European Commission calculates, the general government deficit will still account for 4.3% of gross domestic product next year (against 3.9% projected by the government) and 3.5% in 2012 (instead of 2.7%).
From here, a rumour of a new summary of additions to the draft state budget, which could be introduced after the holidays, of 7 billion euros.
"If necessary, new strict measures will have to be taken," said Olli Rehn, Commissioner for Economic Affairs.
At the moment, Italy accepts the support of international institutions.
“The cornerstones of its economy are fine, and its calculations are correct, we’ve been repeating it for a long time,” said Arrigo Sadun, an Italian member of the International Monetary Fund’s board of directors under pressure from Italy on the International Monetary Fund.
"There is no immediate problem, not even short-term or long-term, because despite its significant debt (119.2% of the gross domestic product of 2011, NDLR), the stabilization effort required in Italy is smaller than the efforts required from other countries," notes Pier Carlo Padoan, chief economist at OCDE.
The question of political instability remains.
On December 14, Silvio Berlusconi will face a motion of no confidence in the Chamber of Deputies, after which elections could be organised at the end of March.
It would start a period, not suitable for taking a decision in the case of a serious crisis.
Europeans are thinking about strengthening anti-crisis mechanisms.
Christine Lagarde reiterated this yesterday as part of a press conference in Paris: "I think the Europeans demonstrated this on Sundays throughout the day and night, they are evidently very solidarity determined, committed to defending their currency and their currency zone."
Although everything is relative, yesterday's recorded expansion, concerning interest on European government bonds, has brought water to the mill to governments and the Central European Bank.
But we can't yet talk about the beginning of normalization.
The Office of Notation Standard & Poor's has announced that it could degrade Portugal's debt record, the target to which various speculations focus the most.
“Since there is an under-transparent and obvious policy in May, based mainly on banking systems,” says Exane BNP Paribas.
All the more because "political time is running slower than market time."
Aware of this interrupted context and the dangers, European countries are intensifying discussions to strengthen existing crisis mechanisms.
Informal consultations among the finance ministers, the most affected issue, and, more broadly with their G20 counterparts, are developing very well.
This is in the light of the Eurogroupe meetings and the Ecofin group from the beginning of next week.
The Central European Bank meeting takes place today.
On the agenda is the fate of unconventional measures to support euro area countries focusing on markets (see above).
As far as the European Finance Fund (FESF), its chief executive, Klaus Regling, ordered a trip to Asia in Singapore on Tuesday, to inform investors from the Far East about the development of the crisis in Europe.
If it is not the aim of increasing funds in this way, it certainly paves the way to achieve this goal.
FESF issuance is expected for next month.
A U.S. official told Reuters yesterday that the U.S. will support a possible increase in mobilized funds through additional direct involvement in FMIs.
First and foremost, Portugal and Spain are preparing to adopt structural reforms, in particular when it comes to organising the labour market.
Reforms that attach to strict measures with rapid effect.
Lisbon and Madrid have taken a different route than Greece and Ireland.
At all costs, both countries want to avoid applying for international aid even before applying their own budgetary directives and deciding on structural reforms necessary to regain their continued confidence.
Capital participants in European funds are finally talking about options, disposable for speeding up the process of normalising markets with sovereign bonds.
On his trip to Paris on November 24, Axel Weber came up with the idea that 750 billion euros mobilized by Europe and the International Monetary Fund would not be enough, the Union would not hesitate to touch the stockpile again.
Economists from Barclays Capital, which is the actor of the very first plan of trade in European sovereign claims, believe that an addition of about 100 billion euros would be enough, "preferably" in the form of a direct provision of financial liquidity.
There is a means: it is the European Financial Stability Mechanism, subsidized by 60 billion euros on that date.
The Council, which has all the chances to be tested in European capitals.
Especially when market conditions have deteriorated.
Snow and ice: 24 counties have an orange emergency.
Snow rations are still causing difficulties in traffic, especially on second-class roads in Brittany, and there is a risk of disrupting some economic activities.
Twenty-four counties now have an orange emergency due to the risk of snow and icing.
L'Eure, l'Eure-et-Loir, les Deux-Sèvres a la Vienne, have joined 20 counties, which have already declared an orange emergency, 18 as early as Wednesday afternoon and two, la Dordogne a la Gironde, later at night.
According to Météo France, significant torrents of snow are causing problems in the norse la Gironde and the Charentes forest, and it is snowing weakly on Ile-de-France.
Snow cover from 2 to 5 cm, sometimes from 7 to 10 cm, is expected in the affected districts and cover from 1 to 3 cm, sometimes up to 5 in the Paris region.
Charente, Charente-Maritime, Côtes d'Armor, Finistère, Gironde, Dordogne, du Poitou and district districts of Basse-Normandie, Ile-de-France and Limousin areas are affected.
The torrentials of snow are big enough to cause difficulties in transport and disrupt some economic activities, Météo France predicts.
On the Côtes d'Armor coast, some counties have banned the passage of heavy-duty trucks - trucks over 7.5 tons with the Guingamp-Lannion and Saint-Brieuc-Loudéac routes.
According to police officers, road traffic on second-class roads in Brittany is very difficult, and the police only recommend going out with their vehicle when it is really necessary and the operation of some trucks has been cancelled for that day.
In Picardia, school connections were banned for Thursday.
No special measures are foreseen on Wednesday evening to restrict freight traffic.
The district authority "calls all car drivers to exercise as much caution as possible on the roads and to adapt their driving to the nature of the road."
The Directorate-General of Civil Aviation (DGAC) has asked airlines to cancel 25% of their flights at Paris Roissy Airport on Thursday and 10% for Orly because of the expected torrential snow.
DGAC advises passengers using air transport services to connect with their company.
Gatwick airports in southern London and Edinburg Airport in Scotia are closed in the UK until Thursday.
Metro Grand Paris: the first financial proposals
Billions have been raining since the debate on modernizing transport at Ile-de-Frace is raining.
At least during the discussion.
In particular, we commemorate 23 billion on the Grand Paris metro, to be built by Société du Grand Paris.
In order for this construction to start, the state announced the granting of a subsidy of 4 billion euros.
In theory, this is an amount that will then allow SGP to use part of the funding from its dual source for contracts.
Today we are finally coming to the First Amendments.
The Rectification Act of Finance of 2010, which examines the Finance Committee of the National Assembly this morning, contains the mechanisms established to secure these funds.
There are two: the creation of a special tax on equipment and an increase in the office tax on Ile-de-France.
The first, we read in the text of the bill, Article 15, reads "in favour of the public institution Société du Grand Paris".
"The yield on this interest is 117 million euros a year".
The second is the reconstruction of the rate for offices, which already exists "to be modernized, to increase the proceeds obtained from it and to affect Société du Grand Paris".
How much would it yield?
Mirage.
That's not quantified in the text.
We're up to €4bn away.
"It is clarified that these fiscal resources connect to 4 billion euros from the capital subsidy, which will be mobilized by the state and invested in Société du Grand Paris according to the project," we can read in the explanation of the reasons.
It doesn't seem shocking to us at SGP.
"Since the moment we've generated revenue (both taxes), we've been able to cover studies for three years and then rake up to €4bn, it's been explained here."
"It will be incorporated into the budget and used as necessary."
SGP considers that 4 billion will not be credited until 2013 for the 2014 budget.
This mechanism, in which 4 promised billions belong to still absent subscribers, risks provoking a somewhat busy parliamentary debate.
The first public meeting will be on December 7.
Vengeful hackers and hard-lived spies
WikiLeaks case has a double meaning.
On the one hand, an obvious scandal is revealed, a scandal that appears to be a scandal only in the face of hypocrisy governed by relations between states, citizens and the press.
On the other hand, it speaks volumes about profound changes at the international level, and anticipates a future dominated by regression.
But let's proceed in order.
The first aspect of WikiLeaks is a confirmation of the fact that any document compiled by the secret service (of any nation) consists exclusively of press cuts.
The "extraordinary revelations" by Americans about Berlusconi's way of sex life are announcing what everyone has been able to read for months in any newspaper (all but these two Berlusconi owns), and Gaddafi's infamous cartoon profile has long been a theme for cabaret artists for sketches.
The rule, according to which secret documents can consist only of messages already known, is essential in the dynamics of the secret services, and not only in this century.
When you go to a bookstore dedicated to esoteric literature, you will see that every work repeats (about Graal, the mystery of Rennes-le-Château, the Templars or Masons) exactly what has already been written in previous works.
And this is not only because the author of the occult texts does not like to search for new information (he does not even know where to look for reports about non-existent), but because those who devote themselves to occultism believe only what they already know and what confirms what they have already learned.
That's Dan Brown's success mechanism.
The same applies to classified documents.
The informant is a lazy and lazy (or limited spirit) head of the secret services (and if he is not, he could be, as I know, an editor in the Libération newspaper), who considers only what he himself admits to be true.
The high-secret information about Berlusconi, which the U.S. Embassy sent to the state resort, was the same that Newsweek had published the week before.
So why has there been so much fuss about these documents?
On the one hand, they say what every cultivated person already knows, namely that the embassies, at least since the end of the Second World War and from the time when heads of state can make phone calls or fly by plane to dinner together, have lost their diplomatic function and, with the exception of a few small exercises in representation, have been transformed into spy centers.
Any viewer watching films about the investigation knows very well that only out of architecture it is seemingly ignored.
However, the fact that this is repeated in public defames the duty of hypocrisy and sheds a bad light on U.S. diplomacy.
Secondly, the idea that any hacker can obtain the most severely classified information of the world's most powerful country represents a hard blow, not insignificant, for the prestige of this state resort.
The scandal affects both the victims and its "executors."
But let's move from here to the deep truth about what happened.
Once, in Orwell's time, it was possible to hold all power, like a Big Brother who controlled every movement of his people.
Orvell's prophecy has been proven ever since, thanks to the ability to control every movement thanks to the phone, every transaction made, the hotel visited, the highway used, and so on, the citizen became an absolute victim of a wreath of power.
But when we demonstrate how it goes now, that even "tombs" with the most secret information of the powerful cannot withstand the intervention of a hacker, the control message ceases to be one-way and becomes a circular.
Power is controlled by every citizen, but every citizen, or at the very least hacker - elected as the avenger of a citizen - can know all the secrets of power.
How can he retain a power that no longer has the ability to preserve his own secrets?
It is true that Georg Simmel has already said that a true secret is an empty secret (and an incongressible secret will never be revealed); it is also true that all information about Berlusconi’s or Merkel’s character is indeed a mysterious fearless secret because it is extracted from public life; but to reveal, as WikiLeaks has done, that Hillary Clinton’s secrets have become empty secrets
WikiLeaks made no mistake with Sarkozy or Merkel, but he did something big to Clinton and Obama.
What will be so significant the consequences of this wounded power?
Clearly, in the future, states won't be able to send online sensitive information - this would amount to its publication on a poster glued to the street corner.
But it's also perfectly obvious that, with current technology, it's pointless to hope that it can be kept secret from a message over the phone.
There is nothing easier than to find out if and when the head of state traveled by plane and connected with one of her colleagues.
How will private and sensitive messages be kept secret in the future ?
I know very well that for now my prognosis belongs to science-fiction and is therefore fantastic, but I have to imagine government officials who transport themselves discreetly filling formalities along uncontrollable routes and carry only those messages that they know by heart or rather hide rare information in the heel of the shoe.
The information will be preserved in unique copies in sockets enclosed by the key: in total, an attempted espionagement in the Watergate affair was less successful than WikiLeaks.
I've had the opportunity to write that technology is now moving side forward, that is, backwards.
A century after the wireless telegraph revolutionized communications, the Internet rehabilitated a wire-based telegraph (telephonic).
Videotaps (analogues) have allowed cinema lovers to shoot the film step by step, move it forward and backward, and reveal all the secrets of the assembly,while now CDs (digits) allow you to just bounce through chapters, that is, after macroportions.
Through high-speed trains, we will arrive from Rome to Milan in three hours, while to travel by plane and transport, which includes we need three and a half hours.
Therefore, it is not extraordinary that politics and communication techniques will return to horse-drawn wagons.
The last note.
At other times, the press tried to understand what plotted in the secrecy of the embassy.
It's now it's embassies that demand confidential information from the press.
Climate: how France hacked its promises of funding
A year after the Copenhagen agreement, it is time to test the observance of promises for quickly unblocked financing (to put, fast-started or early), implemented by developed countries.
And the great French hacks have been uncovered
In the Copenhagen deal, developed countries pledged to provide $30bn in 2010-2012.
The European Union has pledged 7.2 billion euros for three years and France 1.26 billion euros (i.e. 420 million a year).
At the launch of COP16, the EU pulled out a large ace to convince its partners of the reality of its commitment to fast start funding: a report clarifying the actions of its Member States, a press conference, a grand side event, the distribution of brochures...
Transparent operation invaluable and valued at its true value: developed countries and civil society would like all developed countries and civil societies to do them equally and with the same degree of accuracy.
The EU announces that it released 2.2 billion euros in 2010, putting it on the right track to keep its pledge of 7.2 billion in 2012.
But if we focus on the detail, we'll find that things are much more complicated.
According to the Copenhagen Agreement, these funds are "new and additional" to ensure that the increased burden is carried out without prejudice to public development assistance.
Unfortunately, there's no consensus on what "new and additional" means.
While the European Commission has proposed harmonisation, some member states, including France, have blocked the process.
Today, everyone chooses the reference that suits him best.
For example, the European Commission considered 2009 to be a reference: international funding has been preserved and it still adds 150 million to it for three years of a fast-start funding title.
Other countries have chosen similar references with annual payment deadlines or with deadlines at the level of public aid for development.
By contrast, France believes that the financing of "all new projects" in 2010 is "new and additional."
In other words, there is no reference to the expenditure of previous periods or to the global level of public assistance for the development of d'Aide publique au développement.
In particular, the total amount of France's aid to developing countries may remain unchanged (or even decreased), it is intended to comply with its commitment to the financing of "new and additional "...
Another crucial element is the division into donations and loans.
There is no rule there yet and everyone chooses their own method of calculation.
Some, such as France, are considering € € borrowed equal to € € donated.
Evidently, it's hard-to-prove: the effort isn't comparable.
If the use of loans can be documented for certain investments that are profitable (often those associated with the reduction of emissions, consisting in more efficient technologies), it is foolish to present one euro, which we get, as the equivalent of one euro that we give... , we should not understand it by virtue of our obligations, but as the price of the loan for the lender country (i
But France can only give such justifications: it "adheres" to its 420-million-euro commitment, of which 91.5% makes a loan (389.8 million euros) and 8.5% donations (36.1 million euros) - see the EU document so that you can verify it yourself.
It is the only EU country to benefit from a loan on such a scale.
Germany has claimed 46% from there, while Denmark, the Netherlands or Slovakia (among others) adhere to their commitments with 100% donations.
In other words, if the vast majority of the European Union gives € €1, in the context of satisfaction its vow, France will give only 10 centimes and consider this effort to be comparable.
Lovely juggling piece!
For a summary, in its "fast start" France operates with a double trick-or-treater: on the one hand, with the development of stocks over time and, on the other hand, with a real financial burden.
Although he spends only 36.1 million euros, he manages to convince that he is issuing 420 million euros "new and additional" in connection with his promises.
The manipulation of the finance minister (because it seems that although it is Bercy who made such decisions) could have a double negative impact, at the same time within the EU (why would Member States continue to donate if they only need to borrow?) and in negotiations, because developing countries are not completely naïve...
The early funding chapter is just a small question, relatively short-term, in relation to a series of climate discussions.
But it could be an extra bean of sand in a huge machine like the UNFCCC.
Let's hope it won't be our case.
Whatever it is, it is necessary to draw knowledge from this first year of early funding.
France should first announce a change in its way of keeping accounting records for 2011 and 2012, in a spirit of respect for its commitments and decency and justice towards other countries of the European Union.
But above all, France should avoid discussions on long-term deposits (another crucial chapter currently discussed): we will not miss the issue of "suppliement" or the method of accounting for loans.
France should change its stance in order to become as progressive about these deposits as that of others.
Therefore, everyone puts their hopes into the new Minister for the Environment, Ms Kosciusko-Morizet, who will arrive in Cancun next week.
The surprising announcement of France's fast-starting launch and its vision of "additionality" and the use of loans could provoke an increase in the level of international confidence necessary to move forward in discussions.
An alarm about the pesticides that children devour.
A ten-year-old will eat 81 different chemicals one day, according to one study published this Wednesday, from the Association of Future Generations (ex- Movement for Rights and Conservation of Future Generations).
The association, which points out that its results "do not have a significant statistical value with regard to the low number of samples analyzed, but are characteristic of the processed issue", bought foods commonly consumed in four meals between July and September and one light meal as a light snack for one child roughly ten-year-old in supermarkets in l'Oise and Paris.
These typical meals, including official recommendations - five pieces of fresh fruit and vegetables, three dairy products and 1.5 liters of water per day - as well as confectionery, have been analyzed by research labs on chemicals, pesticides, heavy metals and other polluting agents.
The balance of the plate is overwhelming: 81 chemicals, of which 42 are carcinogenic, possible or probable substances, and five substances are clear carcinogenic substances, as well as 37 substances that could act as disruptive to internal excretion glands (PE), according to studies by several institutions such as the International Cancer Research Centre.
"While in almost all cases the legal limits for each chemical received individually aren't being exceeded, in our research we can see well that the reality in consumer exposure to contaminants, perhaps carcinogenic, and/or PE is startling," the association said.
For breakfast, butter and tea with milk contain about ten résidiums with possible carcinogenic effects and three proven as certain carcinogens, as is the case with about twenty residues that could disrupt the hormonal system.
The apple, imported from Brazil, cited by the study as a small snack, contains traces of six chemicals, among other things, a prohibited fungicidal agent in France.
The same is true for green beans from Kenya, included in lunch, for which studies have found traces of strong insecticides banned in the EU.
In addition, the steak of minced meat, tuna in the can and even bun and gum were riddled with pesticides and other chemicals.
In tap water, analyses revealed the presence of nitrates and chloroform.
Salmon steak for dinner was "richest" with 34 chemical residues discovered, and even an analysis of the plastic plate used to heat food in the microwave did not avoid analysis.
The ultimate risk to consumers of this "cocktail of contaminants" is likely to be underestimated," according to the study.
The Ecological Europe-Greens (EE-LV) has proposed "in the face of an urgent situation" a moratorium on the use of pesticides in order to halt the "increase in civilisational diseases."
In July, a study by the European Food Safety Agency (EFSA) looked at the status of the presence of residues of 365 different pesticides identified in fruits and vegetables consumed in Europe, including 76 in cereals.
